\subsection{Overview}

The literature on agricultural subsurface (tile) drainage converges on a shared meta-conclusion: despite decades of field and modeling work, the interactions among crop yield, nitrogen and phosphorus losses, and the field water balance remain insufficiently characterized across the full range of soils, climates, cropping systems, and drainage management practices that occur in tile-drained agriculture. Authors repeatedly frame future needs around \emph{extending scope} --- to more sites, soils, climates, crops, and longer observation periods --- and around \emph{sharpening the tools} used to synthesize and extrapolate, namely process-based models, monitoring networks, remote sensing, and integrated economic frameworks. \textcite{Ma2025} distil this program broadly, arguing that more research is needed to comprehensively understand the impacts of conservation practices on agricultural productivity, nutrient-loss reduction, ecosystem services, and their tradeoffs in tile-drained agricultural ecosystems. The strongest recurring lines of evidence pointing forward are: (i) the geographic and pedoclimatic narrowness of existing field trials --- concentrated in the U.S. Midwest --- which limits transferability \parencite{Zhao2016,Birru2023}; (ii) persistent structural weaknesses in the crop, cover-crop, drainage, and nutrient modules of biophysical models \parencite{Dietzel2016,Chatterjee2025,Feyereisen2006}; (iii) the need for long-term, multi-season datasets to resolve slow soil-health and climate-interaction signals \parencite{Kaspar2012,Bawa2023,Saha2024}; and (iv) the still-immature integration of biophysical, economic, and policy dimensions needed to guide adoption \parencite{Ferin2023,Malone2023,Qin2025}. This section synthesizes these open questions and research priorities rather than settled results.

\subsection{Broadening the empirical base: sites, soils, climates, and long-term observation}

A dominant theme is that existing evidence is drawn from too few sites and too short a window. \textcite{Birru2023} call for research that extends both the scope and geographic area to test and quantify the impacts of multiple cover-crop species under diverse management and biophysical conditions, and for studies spread over more sites with varied environmental conditions and management practices so that cover-crop benefits can be optimized without compromising crop-production goals. \textcite{Zhao2016} note the unequal geographic distribution of historical drainage studies and call for additional research across other states of the U.S. Midwest. Similar calls to extend testing to additional soils, climates, and management arise from \textcite{Grewal2025} (across different soil types and rainfall levels), \textcite{Negm2014} (different climatic conditions, soil types, crop rotations, farming practices, and water-management scenarios), \textcite{Saha2024} (longer periods and additional soils in the Northern Great Plains to identify systems that benefit both productivity and environmental health under a more extreme climate), \textcite{Takeshima2023} (different rainfall environments to confirm the interaction between subsurface drainage and planting pattern), and \textcite{Liu2025} (extending the experimental period and validating pipe burial depth effects on irrigation water productivity across diverse soil properties and climates, plus exploring multiple rotation systems). \textcite{BENOIT1967} likewise stress that more complete quantification of soil-water/plant-survival relationships is needed for extrapolation to other soils.

The call for \emph{long-term} experimentation is equally strong. \textcite{Kaspar2012} identify a need for additional long-term studies of the effectiveness of winter cover crops over time, and \textcite{Bawa2023} argue that a longer-term study is needed to observe the positive influence of a rye cover crop on soil-health parameters. \textcite{MartinezFeria2016} seek understanding of the long-term consequences of including rye cover crops in Midwestern systems and of the biotic and abiotic mechanisms by which rye affects yield and environmental performance. \textcite{Kumar2014b} note that long-term monitoring of fluxes under different crop rotations is needed because of seasonal climatic fluctuations. Several teams describe active or planned long-term programs: \textcite{Qi2025b} report ongoing rice--wheat and rice--oil rotation experiments to further assess subsurface-drainage performance over multiple seasons and climates; \textcite{Liu2025b} and \textcite{Liu2025c} call for long-term monitoring of subsurface pipe drainage and seepage-drainage effects across diverse environmental conditions and cropping systems; and \textcite{Thoma2005} caution that four years of data are insufficient to resolve whether higher residue cover minimizes pollutant losses and affects grain yield over time. \textcite{Acharya2019b} similarly call for a more detailed and longer-term experiment to understand climate--crop-management interactions.

\subsection{Cover crops: agronomic, environmental, and modeling frontiers}

Cover crops --- especially winter rye --- attract the largest concentration of open questions. On the agronomic side, \textcite{Waring2020} prioritize tools to promote rye growth in colder regions and study of long-term no-till in that climate, while \textcite{Bawa2023} highlight the need for management strategies to establish rye under unusually high or low temperature and precipitation and limited growing degree days. \textcite{Dougherty2020} flag that observed yield drag may reflect poor planter performance through cover-crop residue, warranting investigation. \textcite{Kaspar2007} suggest improving rye management by evaluating more cultivars, adjusting timing relative to planting, and improving establishment methods to reduce year-to-year variability and yield impacts, and note that establishment costs, logistical conflicts, and seed availability must be overcome before widespread adoption; permanent vegetation strips might likewise be improved through different species, wider strips, or altered management. \textcite{Dinnes2002} call for cultural and plant-breeding research to improve cover-crop compatibility, establishment, management, and reduced negative impacts on subsequent row crops, and \textcite{Liu2019} echo the need to improve establishment and cold hardiness. \textcite{Qi2011b} and \textcite{Qi2011} identify kura clover as requiring better agronomic management (living mulch with corn) to avoid corn yield loss under Iowa conditions.

On the environmental-mechanism side, \textcite{Lacey2015} call for research on the release of cover-crop-scavenged nitrogen in spring and on synchronizing N release from residue with the growth stages of corn and soybean. \textcite{Roth2018} identify determining the nitrogen content of grass cover crops through a producer-adoptable method as a research priority, and note that seeding rates could be adjusted while maintaining environmental benefits and that cover-crop and nutrient-management strategies could be developed to maintain cash-crop yields. \textcite{Askar2023} call for understanding the combined effects of cover crops and manure-rate application on subsurface dissolved reactive phosphorus loads and concentrations, evaluating climatic, soil, topographic, management, and crop-species factors. \textcite{Meyer2019} highlight the need to optimize trade-offs between the services and disservices of cover crops for water balance and to better understand the factors conditioning cover-crop effects on drainage. \textcite{Liu2019} more broadly call for coordinated common experiments across geophysical conditions to quantify winter crop-cover phosphorus contributions to runoff or leaching, to identify reliable plant and soil P analytes, to understand plant--root--soil--management--climate interactions on P losses, to evaluate water-quality tradeoffs under field settings, and to predict future-climate influences.

Cover-crop \emph{modeling} is a distinct frontier. \textcite{Feyereisen2006} enumerate needed advances: additional validation of the rye growth model, field verification of climate and soil-fertility effects on the ratio of rye N uptake to biomass, adjustment of radiation-use efficiency for atmospheric conditions, and better algorithms for plant N uptake. \textcite{Malone2007c} note that determining correct parameter values for winter cover crops requires thorough testing with several years of field-specific data, and that a more realistic N-uptake routine may improve corn-yield predictions. \textcite{Malone2014b} state that modeling germination and establishment of winter rye needs further research and that further analysis is the next step to assess regional cover-crop impacts. \textcite{Chatterjee2025} report that calibration of soil-organic-matter and drained-upper-limit parameters is critical and that a dedicated crop module for simulating winter rye could improve prediction of aboveground biomass and its consequences for cash-crop yield and water and N flux. \textcite{Dietzel2016} identify weaknesses in APSIM related to cover-crop effects on following-corn yields, winter soil temperatures under frozen conditions, and subsurface drainage in soybeans during flooding. \textcite{Gupta2022} propose developing a chemical-termination routine to enhance cover-crop impact studies, and \textcite{Gupta2023b} call for improving the drainage and soil-N mineralization/immobilization modules and studying extreme-event effects on cover-crop impacts to plan field-scale adaptive strategies. \textcite{Strock2004} report research in progress to predict cover-crop establishment and growth in Minnesota. \textcite{Singh2023} suggest investigating additional cover crops such as radish and legumes in hydrology and water-quality simulations.

\subsection{Drainage system design, water-table management, and materials}

Optimizing drainage design for the joint goals of crop yield and water quality is a persistent open problem. \textcite{Kladivko2021} argue that more field studies coupled with modeling are needed to develop site-specific recommendations and to optimize drainage design for both crop yield and water-quality purposes, calling continued efforts on systems approaches "urgently needed." \textcite{Zhao2016} suggest research into alternative drainage spacing aimed at balancing crop production against decreased drainage losses. \textcite{Singh2006b,Singh2007b} recommend field experiments in Iowa and the Upper Midwest to characterize how shallow and controlled drainage affect the complete water balance and crop production, including potential water-movement pathways, and note that a flexible scheme managing the control structure by soil-moisture status might reduce controlled-drainage impacts on relative yields and surface runoff. \textcite{Nash2015} state that more research is needed to determine whether managed drainage can consistently produce greater corn yield than free drainage, with benefits on claypan soils perhaps concentrated in higher-yield-potential years. \textcite{Nelson2011} call for further evaluation of drainage water management effects on high-yielding soybean cultivars, insect and disease management, cost-effectiveness in corn--soybean rotations, and grain-quality differences on claypan soils. \textcite{Dinnes2002} prioritize low-cost drainage-control and water-table-management designs that can be retrofitted to existing tile lines, and research on wetlands, buffers, and bioreactors to address residence time, carbon sources, land area, and cost limitations.

Cold-climate and peatland drainage raise specific needs: \textcite{Li2025} call for greater attention to optimizing winter subsurface-drainage and water-table management for croplands as temperatures warm, and \textcite{Läpikivi2025} identify needs to evaluate subsurface irrigation for maintaining shallow water-table depth in fields drained to current standards, to study water availability in catchments shared by multiple peatlands, and to map the distribution of cultivated peatlands and their water-table depths for catchment-wide application. \textcite{STEINHARDT1974} caution that under severe frost, subsurface drainage might irreversibly affect ploughed-soil structure and that other criteria (no-traffic systems, aeration-sensitive crops) may sometimes rule.

Drainage \emph{materials and installation} also feature. \textcite{Ghane2022} recommend long-term field investigations of knitted-sock envelope clogging across soil types to inform drainage design. \textcite{Qi2025b} report developing an integrated excavation-and-burial machine to reduce labor costs and call for investigating alternative rot-resistant straw-bundling techniques and burial methods to improve drainage longevity and waterlogging mitigation, while \textcite{Lu2018} plan to study the long-term impact of crop straw as subsurface-drainage material on drainage-water quality and soil properties under different irrigation schedules. \textcite{Ellafi2021} call for evaluating drainage designs' impacts on crop production, salinity control, and waterlogging using DRAINMOD, and for calculating predicted income across cropping systems. \textcite{Takeshima2023} recommend developing countermeasures against unpredictable rainfall, such as combining subsurface drainage with raised-bed planting.

\subsection{Nitrogen and nutrient management priorities}

Refining nitrogen management to reduce drainage losses without sacrificing yield is a core research need. \textcite{Jaynes2013} call for further field research or crop-modeling to pinpoint where between the V12 and V16 growth stages sidedress N ceases to be beneficial, and \textcite{Jaynes2015} note that sidedressing can be combined with soil tests or crop N-deficiency sensing for real-time N-rate adjustment. \textcite{Maas2022} recommend comprehensive tools integrating reliable weather forecasts, historical land management, local field and soil parameters, and crop-variety information to estimate optimum N rates, propose regional long-term agronomic-optimum-N-rate (AONR) means derived from fertilizer type, yield, and drainage status via surveys or census data, and call for study of the planting-date/agronomic-efficiency/AONR relationship and of the drivers of uncertainty and variability in agricultural systems. \textcite{Dinnes2002} prioritize better crediting of non-fertilizer N sources through soil tests, improved in-season soil-N monitoring for split applications (including earlier-season tests and models using soil respiration and meteorology), and reliable variable-rate N models incorporating remote sensing and hyperspectral approaches. \textcite{Cassman2025} propose low-cost proxy variables for monitoring soil N and water supply --- yield without applied N as a field-scale estimate of soil N-supply capacity, and partial factor productivity of applied N and rainfall as proxies for apparent N-use efficiency and economic return per unit rainfall.

At larger scales, \textcite{Zhao2016} call for constraining the magnitude and variability of crop-rotation impacts on yield-scaled N loss and for accounting for N losses on a rotation rather than annual basis. \textcite{Raymond2012} call for additional experimental and modeling work to resolve crop-management/N-export relationships, a more spatially intense monitoring system of riverine outflow and net anthropogenic nitrogen inputs coupled with detailed crop-management (particularly tile-drainage) information, and exploration of alternative drainage approaches, cover crops, and crops with more active rooting and transpiration. \textcite{Pasley2021} note very little research on more desirable rotation crops in simple Midwestern two-crop rotations from a leaching perspective. \textcite{Deng2011} plan to apply revised DNDC to more watersheds, improve parameterization for N-loss prediction, test the variance of surface-cover and management factors across crop-growth stages, and schedule aquatic biogeochemistry to address the fate of N in streams, rivers, lakes, reservoirs, and estuaries. \textcite{Kanwar1997} reinforce the need for studies on chemical placement, rate, and timing for various tillage practices to reduce tile-drainage chemical losses, incorporated into at least a two-year corn--soybean rotation to ensure economic and environmental viability. \textcite{Bakhsh2004} note that overprediction of soybean yields may require refining N-cycling algorithms for N\textsubscript{2} fixation and plant N-uptake. \textcite{Malone2018} call for field studies to evaluate fertilized/harvested rye cover crop, which if confirmed could offer a strategy to raise producer revenue and net energy production while reducing drainage N loss.

\subsection{Irrigation, salinity, and water reuse in arid and saline systems}

A parallel body of work addresses drainage in irrigated, arid, and saline settings. \textcite{Yao2017} call for long-term geohydrological and crop data --- including intensive electromagnetic-induction surveys, groundwater-dynamics time series, and crop biomass --- to validate simulations across irrigation/drainage systems and agronomic patterns. \textcite{Alzraiee2013} argue that future modeling must systematically evaluate alternative field-scale irrigation and drainage strategies to manage waterlogging and high soil salinity under ambiguity, that a statistical notion of the likelihood of achieving environmental and crop-production goals field-by-field would guide riskier watershed-scale plans, and that considering multiple irrigation seasons might reveal different spatial distributions of yield impact. \textcite{Bethune2008} recommend that, where tubewell drainage manages deep percolation, land-use planning steer irrigation development toward low-salinity, high-yielding aquifers, encourage sprinkler irrigation, and move new development to areas where salinity-management costs are low. \textcite{Qadir2001} suggest incorporating short-duration and salt-tolerant crops (e.g., Sesbania, Bermuda or Kallar grass) into reclamation rotations, and \textcite{Castanheira2010} call for quantifying rainfall amounts and irrigation-efficiency gains with respect to salt-removal capacity and reduced salt load.

Water-productivity and technology questions also recur. \textcite{Feng2019} propose using their outputs to design irrigation schedules under different hydrological years and soil water-salt environments and using the coupled HYDRUS--EPIC model to evaluate the future sustainability of saline-water irrigation. \textcite{Jia2017} recommend measuring water-table changes and their contribution to storage and evapotranspiration, recomputing water-application efficiency per day between soil samplings to account for water-table rise, reformulating the efficiency equation, and using longer irrigation durations or slower flow rates to improve uniformity. \textcite{Qin2025} call for scalable data-management infrastructures for long-term field deployment (energy-efficient edge computing, low-power IoT protocols), life-cycle cost and return-on-investment assessment under varying market and climate conditions, integration of agronomic models with environmental and economic frameworks in a unified precision-agriculture platform, and low-temperature crystallization to treat brine toward near-zero discharge. \textcite{Abdelkrim2021} note that specific irrigation dates and amounts should be scheduled using criteria beyond modeling, such as farmers' expert knowledge and sensors. \textcite{DINAR1989} call for a more complete economic evaluation of shifting irrigation technology including associated management variables, and \textcite{Qian2025} highlight the need to study solute transport during cultivation from a components perspective and to test applicability under drip irrigation. \textcite{ElHassanin2022} suggest that established thresholds could seed large-scale modeling or the development of water/drainage-management measures to raise field water-use fraction, crop-water productivity, and wheat production, and \textcite{RADSTAKE1988} recommend continued monitoring of crop yield and water-quality change to improve forecasts of groundwater suitability for irrigation in the Nile Valley. \textcite{Ayars2006} note that extending their method to other crops will require additional studies, and \textcite{Karegoudar2022} advise that policymakers may proceed under all three water-availability scenarios. Forage-water-use comparisons remain open: \textcite{Mundy2006} judge a comparison of the water-use efficiency of forages under flood versus spray irrigation worthwhile, and \textcite{Greenwood2006} defer forage yield and plant--soil--water responses to companion papers.

\subsection{Advancing process-based and biophysical models}

Beyond cover-crop and drainage modules, authors call broadly for stronger, better-validated, and more transferable models. \textcite{SKAGGS1994} state that much remains to be learned about the mechanisms governing pollutant losses from drained soils, that research must improve process understanding before quantification is possible, that methods are needed to quantify how alternative designs and operations affect crop yields and environmental impacts while considering interdependent cultural, fertility, and pest-management practices, and that better datasets are needed to test models across wide ranges of soil, crop, and climate. \textcite{Negm2014} call for more intense field measurements to validate their model and additional testing across conditions, noting its potential for simulating long-term climate-change impacts. \textcite{Grewal2025} prioritize improving prediction of high-load peak events, refining the concentration component of the model, further testing the fertilizer-effect hypothesis under ponding, and empirical data to validate predictions for cover-crop and controlled-drainage practices. \textcite{Gunn2018} report that DRAINMOD is being coupled with a crop model (DSSAT) to capture other factors influencing maize relative yield under subirrigation. \textcite{Tian2012} call for field verification of soil-physical-property changes, investigation of early-successional plant growth and its hydrological effects, verification of temporal stomatal-conductance changes against field measurements, and cautious evaluation of adding understory-growth and prescribed-burning modules. \textcite{Morrison2014} seek greater sensitivity to low flows, yearly crop-information inputs for calibration/validation consistency, and a macropore-flow algorithm to capture poorly draining soils. \textcite{Smith2008} argue that a validated process-based rather than empirical model should be applied to capture complex variable interactions. \textcite{Ndulue2024} recommend incorporating CO\textsubscript{2} effects, diseases, nutrient availability, cultivar type, and planting date for greater confidence in climate-change results. \textcite{Ma2025} argue that systematic data collection on water fluxes, solute-nutrient fluxes, greenhouse-gas emissions, root development, and aboveground biomass would facilitate model validation and refine mechanisms and parameterization. \textcite{Li2025} note that winter-crop-growth simulations need additional crop-genetic-parameter calibration and must account for elevated CO\textsubscript{2} and alternative management, and that simpler, faster biophysical models may be viable for large-scale winter-cropland simulation. \textcite{Dietzel2016} additionally call for improved subsurface-drainage simulation within soybean in APSIM. \textcite{Qi2010b} report a field-scale experiment being conducted in parallel to a non-weighing lysimeter study to validate rye cover-crop effects on soil-water dynamics and nitrate leaching, and \textcite{Malone2007c} note that per-plot soil-parameter calibration would improve drainage predictions.

\subsection{Climate change, extremes, and system resilience}

Climate adaptation frames many priorities. \textcite{Saha2024} stress continuing to identify practices and combinations that increase the resilience of agricultural systems to climate extremes. \textcite{Malone2020} lay out a modeling agenda addressing additional simulation periods, other representative concentration pathways, management options (reduced fertilizer rates; earlier cash- and cover-crop harvest, termination, and planting), cash-crop cultivars, cover-crop types, and additional soils and locations. \textcite{Ferin2023} recommend approaches allowing within-county variation in fertilizer application rate, crop-specific manure applications as data become available, multiple global-climate-model forcings from CMIP5 or CMIP6, and attention to co-benefits of perennial grasses such as carbon sequestration and lower N\textsubscript{2}O loss. \textcite{Dietzel2016} argue that efforts to raise water-use efficiency should design cropping systems that function under both drought and flood, diversify whole-farm designs to include drought- and flood-tolerant plants, and pursue engineering solutions such as capturing excess floodwater for later use in drought. \textcite{PeltonenSainio2021} call for more information on the costs and benefits of adaptation measures for changing precipitation, a thorough assessment of the payback period for irrigation investment in Finland, stronger knowledge-sharing on carbon farming and soil-carbon maintenance, and additional funding and decision tools for both irrigation and drainage investment. \textcite{Morton2015} identify a need for increased localized understanding of climate patterns and how farmers incorporate actual and perceived experiences of excess precipitation into decisions, more accurate downscaled climate information and local forecasts beyond 3--5 days, and better understanding of the relationships among practices and adoption timescales. \textcite{Gupta2023b} likewise call for studying extreme-event effects on cover-crop impacts to plan field-scale adaptation.

\subsection{Remote sensing, precision tools, and decision support}

Emerging sensing and data technologies are widely seen as enabling the next phase of research. \textcite{Khanal2020} outline a broad agenda: determining the optimal field conditions and image quality for detecting tile-drainage patterns and advanced processing for automatic extraction of tile-drain lines; downscaling techniques combining multiple data sources for finer-resolution soil moisture; integrating remote sensing with machine learning to assess soil properties and compaction; coupling crop growth stages with forecasted weather for supply forecasting; leveraging high-resolution remote sensing with crop modeling to understand within-field nitrogen-stress variability and variable-rate N benefits; mapping crop genotypes and their role in grain quality; developing databases for early crop-disease detection; applying semi-supervised learning and generative adversarial networks to weed identification; and site-specific residue management. \textcite{Cassman2025} propose a robust spatial framework combining climate and soil type with geo-referenced farmer-reported rotation, yield, and management data, tailoring a Climate Zone and Technology Extrapolation Domain framework to delineate conditions under which soil-health-improving-practice adoption yields similar impacts, and using remote-sensing, satellite-imagery, and on-farm databases to estimate field-specific benefits. \textcite{Reid2018} call for validating individual components and aggregate edge-of-field losses for Canadian conditions, a large effort to quantify edge-of-field-to-surface-water delivery ratios, improved input-data collection with possible remote sensing of topography, soil moisture, crop area, and soil cover, and intensity-based indicators of phosphorus loss per unit production developed with commodity organizations. \textcite{Julien2023} envision a soil-wetness-index approach forming part of a decision-support tool integrated into marketing-authorization recommendations, while noting that assumptions about drainage-season insensitivity to winter crop type and site-dependent thresholds need testing across soils.

\subsection{Cropping-system diversification: perennials, bioenergy, and rotations}

Diversified and perennial systems raise distinctive research needs. \textcite{Arrueta2022} call for establishing a clear causal link between perennial-crop implementation and nutrient loads across fields with varying physical and chemical soil characteristics, and note that government policies encouraging landscape diversification and rewarding environmental services may be needed to overcome adoption barriers. \textcite{Wilson2023} call for robust observed water-quality datasets for perennialized cropping systems to understand their regional water-quality impact, supporting watershed-scale learning and collective action around novel crops. \textcite{Guo2018} propose large-scale assessment of bioenergy-crop scenarios in the Mississippi River system and other regions (e.g., China's marginal lands), quantification of biofeedstock production and its water-quantity/quality impacts in mildly sloped watersheds, investigation of pasture loss during bioenergy planting, field experiments to quantify water-quality impacts, and monitoring of nutrient-reduction efficiencies with cost/benefit assessment to alleviate Gulf of Mexico hypoxia. \textcite{Gentry2025} report future work refining management within a diverse cropping system, documenting economic viability against the conventional two-year rotation, nested microplot studies of wheat varieties and seeding rates for double-crop soybean, and planned water-infiltration studies of earthworm influence on tile flow and nitrate loss. \textcite{Dinnes2002} call for perennial cash-cropping systems that maintain economic returns while reducing nitrate leaching. \textcite{Malone2018} highlight double-cropping fertilized rye as a promising revenue, net-energy, and drainage-N strategy pending field confirmation, and \textcite{HUNDAL1976} report ongoing studies of meadow cropping effects on soil conditions and grain yields.

\subsection{Economics, policy, adoption, and the human dimension}

Bridging biophysical findings to on-farm and policy decisions is a recurrent frontier. \textcite{Waring2020} prioritize the economics of cover crops. \textcite{Roth2018} note that quantifying soil-health impacts, surface and subsurface phosphorus losses, and denitrification changes could increase cost recovery, and that a long-term average of total cover-crop cost recovery could guide policymakers on subsidies. \textcite{Mourtzinis2021} observe that a long-term cumulative yield-and-profit assessment of drainage installation would require installation cost, yield responses in other rotation crops, land value, and other farm benefits, and could quantify drainage adoption's contribution to soybean yield gain. \textcite{Malone2023} call for information on producer participation, pilot programs in key locations, field trials and modeling clarifying how primary-crop yield, rye yield, and N losses vary with soil, climate, and management, and additional studies to advance policies optimizing environmental quality, bioenergy, carbon, yields, and profits. \textcite{Ferin2023} argue that future efforts should ultimately include the multitude of biophysical factors and socioeconomic nuances through integrated biophysical--economic modeling at the field scale. \textcite{Kröger2012} urge committing significant resources to replicable studies in the Lower Mississippi Alluvial Valley to estimate best-management-practice effectiveness under realistic conditions, plus additional field-based monitoring to generate efficiency estimates for conservation-program and policy analysis. \textcite{Bakhsh2007b} argue that landscape/hydrologic spatial zones should be managed with suitable tillage and cropping systems for watershed sustainability and that decision tools should account for soil and landscape effects on yields. \textcite{Nelson2011} include cost-effectiveness in corn--soybean rotations among evaluation needs, and \textcite{Jaynes2001} conclude that dramatic changes in management on artificially drained soils will be required before corn production can be considered sustainable.

\subsection{Soil health, biology, and physical processes}

Slower-acting soil and biological processes are flagged as understudied. \textcite{Han2017} note that the link between \emph{Fusarium} abundance and soybean root rot needs examination and that long-term drainage may affect field \emph{Fusarium} populations and, under certain conditions, soybean-root health and growth. \textcite{Kumar2014b} call for measuring biogeochemical processes involved in methane production and oxidation and modeling these fluxes from poorly drained soils. \textcite{Shipitalo2004} suggest that disrupting the linkage of cracks to earthworm burrows through the plow pan --- via conservation tillage as an alternative to moldboard plowing and lightweight tractors to reduce compaction and prevent plow-pan reformation --- might reduce sediment and contaminant movement to subdrains, noting no-till has reduced sediment losses in crack-prone clayey soils. \textcite{Daigh2014} call for precisely quantifying evapotranspiration to explain drain-flow differences among cropping systems, and \textcite{Tian2012} note early-successional growth and soil-physical-property changes need verification. \textcite{DarziNaftchali2017b} argue that the drought tolerance and phenological traits of rice cultivars should guide selection of drainage systems and drying periods, timed to cultivar sensitivity to water deficit. \textcite{Nash2017} advise farmers to test forage sorghum for toxic nitrate levels before grazing regardless of perceived drought. \textcite{Manninen2018} call for understanding the reactions and role of agriculturally derived dissolved organic matter in marine systems over time, and \textcite{GiménezValero2024} anticipate complementing metabolomic fruit analysis with studies of cultivation-technique effects on citrus fruit quality and size.

\subsection{Watershed-scale synthesis, monitoring networks, and data infrastructure}

Finally, several authors emphasize scaling from field to watershed and building the data infrastructure to support it. \textcite{Baffaut2020} propose modifying the Soil Vulnerability Index to differentiate surface from subsurface artificial drainage (adjusting the risk class of the runoff or leaching component), to consider the leaching component both with and without artificial drainage to distinguish permeable-soil from drainage causes, to account for irrigation impacts on surface-water quality and drainage, to incorporate rainfall intensity (possibly via the R-factor), and to determine exact soil components where complex soils or land leveling complicate classification. \textcite{Singh2023} suggest calibrating and validating models for multiple sub-watersheds and comparing modeled versus observed crop yield at the sub-watershed level for more reliable estimates. \textcite{Smith2008} recommend focusing future conservation-assessment efforts on managing potholes in cropped fields --- especially corn fields where potholes drain directly to ditches --- and their effects on tile-drainage water. \textcite{Guo2018} extend this to large-scale bioenergy assessment across river systems. \textcite{Ferin2023} and \textcite{Deng2011} both point toward multi-forcing, multi-watershed application. Underlying all of these is the data imperative articulated by \textcite{Ma2025} and \textcite{SKAGGS1994}: better, longer, and more spatially representative datasets --- spanning water and solute fluxes, greenhouse-gas emissions, root and biomass development, and management history --- are the foundational requirement for validating models, resolving tradeoffs, and delivering the site-specific, climate-resilient drainage-management guidance the field ultimately seeks.

6.body:
\subsection{Overview}
Reducing nitrate--nitrogen (NO$_3$-N) losses to subsurface (tile) drainage and the underlying groundwater remains one of the least settled objectives in drainage science: although a wide portfolio of management levers has been proposed, the field is still dominated by open questions about which practices work, under what conditions, at what cost, and with what unintended tradeoffs. Across sites from Iowa, Illinois, Indiana, Minnesota, and the Dakotas in the U.S.\ Midwest to Canada, and across Europe (Finland, Denmark, Sweden, Norway, France, Germany, Switzerland, Italy, Ireland, Latvia) and beyond, authors repeatedly frame their contributions less as definitive answers than as calls for further field experiments, better process understanding, and improved decision-support tools. \textcite{ROSSI1991} state the underlying urgency plainly: management directed at substantial nitrate reductions is needed, together with careful evaluation of the effects of fertilizer timing, rate, form, and placement on NO$_3^-$ loss via subsurface drainage.

Several broad lines of evidence and research need recur throughout this theme. First, \emph{fertilizer N management}---rate, timing, form, placement, and crediting of residual and mineralizable N---is widely seen as the primary but incompletely optimized lever. Second, \emph{cropping-system change}, especially cover crops, perennials, and alternative rotations, is promoted but still under-quantified across soils and climates. Third, \emph{drainage water management} (controlled drainage, shallow drainage, water-table control) and \emph{edge-of-field treatment} (bioreactors, saturated riparian buffers, wetlands) are promising but require optimization and long-term verification. Fourth, \emph{process-based modeling} is expected to carry much of the predictive burden as concerns about nitrate export grow, yet substantial algorithmic gaps remain---particularly for winter, soil C--N cycling, and groundwater interactions. Fifth, \emph{monitoring, tracers, and scaling} methods must improve to apportion sources and extend site findings to landscapes. Finally, cutting across all of these are unresolved \emph{tradeoffs}---between nitrate reduction and yield, greenhouse-gas emissions, phosphorus loss, and economic risk---that the field has not yet adequately characterized. \textcite{Du2017} anticipate that models will be relied upon extensively to estimate management effects as concern over nitrate export increases, and can enhance understanding of how N management affects water quality.

\subsection{Fertilizer nitrogen management: rate, timing, form, and crediting}
The most consistently identified priority is refining fertilizer N management. \textcite{Zhao2000} argue that efforts should concentrate on identifying N management strategies that increase N-use efficiency in corn production, pointing specifically to optimal rate, timing, and form as targets for reducing nitrate levels in the Minnesota and upper Mississippi rivers. \textcite{Randall2001} similarly call to fine-tune fertilizer N management by applying the correct rate at the optimum time and crediting previous legume crops and animal-manure applications, and to improve animal-manure management by knowing nutrient content and application rate, spreading uniformly, and incorporating in a timely manner. \textcite{Emmett2022} note that research determining optimal rates and timing of N fertilization can help reduce N$_2$O emissions and minimize tradeoffs between yield and environmental performance. At the watershed scale, \textcite{Dattamudi2020} conclude that N application schemes need reevaluation for better N management and environmental sustainability, suggesting lower fertilizer rates for corn, precision agriculture, use of soil N with high-yielding varieties, split fall/summer application, and proper timing. \textcite{Elmi2002} add that reductions may be possible by carefully managing fertilization rates and timing to match crop uptake.

\subsubsection{Crediting residual, mineralizable, and legacy nitrogen}
A recurring, more specific gap is the crediting of N supplied by soil organic matter, prior legumes, manure, and land-use legacies when setting application rates. \textcite{Bakhsh2007} state a need to assess proper N credit due to mineralization from the soil organic-matter pool and soybean residue when deciding N application rates from organic or inorganic sources, and \textcite{Bakhsh2005} likewise call for assessing proper N credit to soybean and the storage of mineralizable N in the root zone before applications, to reduce NO$_3$-N leaching potential with tile flows. \textcite{Randall2001} extend this to a methodological need: improved soil-N testing methods to estimate mineralizable N and carryover N, especially after dry years, legumes, or manure applications. In systems transitioning from perennial cover, \textcite{Huggins2001} propose adjusting N recommendations to include second-year credits for N mineralization following perennial-grass conversion to row crops, note that N strategies for corn must be developed to efficiently use the release of mineralized N from CRP land, and observe that conservation tillage during CRP/alfalfa conversion would likely slow mineralization and alter residual soil N buildup and NO$_3$-N losses. \textcite{Weed1996} recommend minimizing NH$_3$ fertilizer applied to continuous- and rotation-corn plots by using late-spring soil-nitrate tests. On land plowed out of alfalfa, \textcite{ROBBINS1980} counsel care in determining N rates and timing and note that improved timing and amount of applied water could decrease required N rates while making more N available to the crop.

For irrigated systems on vulnerable soils, \textcite{Derby2009} enumerate management options---reducing N application during the first years after irrigation initiation, setting reasonable yield goals, giving proper credit for fall residual soil NO$_3$-N, and adjusting applications for adverse weather or poor soil properties---and emphasize that field-scale studies spanning soil, vadose-zone, and groundwater levels across multiple management periods are necessary to understand water quality under irrigated agriculture. \textcite{Hurtado2011} report that lower N rates in cotton could reduce losses during the cotton season and the beginning of the following one. \textcite{Ale2012b} caution that model-predicted excess soil-N reserves at the studied N rates must be verified, while noting the potential to further reduce fertilizer from the lowest rates given soil-N budget surpluses, which may further cut NO$_3$-N losses toward discharge-reduction goals. The persistence and role of \emph{legacy} N is itself flagged as underexplored: \textcite{Williams2022} argue that a clearer definition of legacy nutrients is urgently needed to better apportion losses and develop mitigation strategies, and \textcite{Hu2024} call for research into the coupled biogeochemical and hydrological processes that regulate the formation of legacy N stores and their impact on NO$_3^-$ export in tile-drained agroecosystems.

\subsubsection{Enhanced-efficiency fertilizers, stabilizers, and application method}
Beyond rate and timing, several authors point to product- and method-level questions. \textcite{Kaur2024c} recommend that future research address enhanced-efficiency fertilizer technology---spring-applied anhydrous ammonia with a nitrification inhibitor---in conjunction with enhanced drainage water management in corn--soybean rotations. \textcite{Vincent2025} note that management of NH$_4^+$-N specifically can be achieved through N stabilizers applied alongside fertilizers, and that the effects of cover crops on field- and watershed-scale NH$_4^+$-N dynamics deserve further exploration. Regarding manure delivery, \textcite{Gamble2018} report that drainage NO$_3$-N concentrations from fertigated silage corn need further study to determine whether they can be reduced to acceptable levels without impacting silage yield, and stress that tradeoffs of fertigation---crop injury, disease, pathogen transport to waterways, and alternative N-loss pathways relative to slurry injection---require further exploration. \textcite{Bechmann2014} recommend developing a strategy to even out manure application within catchments of high livestock density, and that mitigation in cereal areas should focus on reducing soil mineral N in spring and autumn.

\subsection{Timing of loss: the early-spring and seasonal window}
A distinctive and repeatedly emphasized frontier is the temporal concentration of losses, particularly in early spring, and the need for management targeted to that window. \textcite{Bakhsh2007} state that strategies need to be developed to reduce early-spring NO$_3$-N leaching losses, and \textcite{Bakhsh2010} extend this to reducing off-site nitrate transport during the March-through-June period, adding that the temporal distribution of rainfall during the growing season needs further attention when evaluating and managing nitrate leaching to drainage water. \textcite{Nance2007} argue that assessment of soil NO$_3$-N status, tile flow, and NO$_3$-N loss during April, May, and June warrants further study. \textcite{Gentry2024} suggest that a reliable, winter-hardy cover crop ahead of corn may help reduce tile nitrate in corn--soybean rotations, and that moving fall N application to spring while using overwintering grass cover crops may be the best way to decrease N loss in intensively tile-drained watersheds. For drainage-outlet management, \textcite{Skaggs1995} recommend selectively managing drainage outlets to reduce peak NO$_3$-N losses, especially in years following drought-reduced yields, and both \textcite{Elmi2004} and \textcite{Elmi2002} note that further reductions could be achieved if controlled drainage is kept operational during early spring (and late fall), when NO$_3$-N losses are most severe and drainage is not needed for crop production.

\subsection{Cropping systems: cover crops, perennials, and rotations}
Cropping-system change is widely promoted but consistently flagged as needing more field evidence, especially across the diversity of soils and climates. \textcite{Kladivko2014} call directly for further research, development, and technical assistance to overcome practical obstacles to cover-crop adoption in the Midwest, for additional field experiments measuring cover-crop effects on NO$_3$ losses in drainage water on multiple soil types across the region, and for using the new data to calibrate and validate RZWQM for additional soils and improve regional estimates of cover-crop NO$_3$ reductions. \textcite{Oliveira2025} identify multiple open questions: how variations in cover-crop biomass, termination timing, and rooting depth interact with drainage dynamics to further affect NO$_3^-$ and dissolved N$_2$O losses, and how variable drainage intensity, fluctuating water tables, and differences in cover-crop biomass and species composition influence dissolved CH$_4$. \textcite{Lagzdins2015} list appropriate mitigation measures needing implementation---annual winter crops, winter cover crops where spring crops are the main crop, and no autumn tillage---to reduce nitrate-N losses to drainage water.

Novel and perennial-based options are also proposed for study. \textcite{Nance2007} highlight the potential of intercropping triticale with red clover for minimizing soil NO$_3$-N and urge that research and policy encourage development of winter triticale to reduce soil NO$_3$-N loss from U.S.\ cropping systems. \textcite{Emmett2022} note that improvements in productivity and land-use efficiency in camelina--soybean relay- and double-cropping systems will be essential to the viability of that sustainable-intensification strategy, and that efforts to improve fall establishment and reduce N surpluses may enable desired reductions in nitrate leaching. \textcite{Randall2001} advocate developing alternative cropping systems containing perennial crops to reduce nitrate losses, and both \textcite{Gowda2007} and \textcite{Nangia2008} suggest that attaining nitrate TMDLs or larger reductions may require alternatives beyond fertilizer management---such as partial conversion of cropland to pasture, changes in tile-drain spacing and/or depth, fall cover crops, or wetland restoration. \textcite{Kaur2024c} add that under variable climate, crop rotations may need modification, or cover crops planted in the winter fallow season to minimize N losses and capture unused soil-profile N. \textcite{Ma2007b} recommend explicitly evaluating controlled drainage, crop rotation, cover crops, and N management for reducing NO$_3$-N loss in drain flow, and \textcite{Malone2019} propose field studies (and paired model testing) of N loss to subsurface drainage with and without corn-stover harvest.

\subsection{Drainage water management and water-table control}
Controlled drainage, shallow drainage, and other water-table management approaches are seen as promising mitigation but require optimization, verification under varied climates, and clearer partitioning of their mechanisms. \textcite{Fang2012} stress that careful management of the timing and depth of gate operation is very important to optimize reduced N loss using controlled drainage. \textcite{Wesström2001} argue that further investigation of controlled drainage/water-table (CWT) system management is needed to optimize both environmental and agricultural benefits, and that water balances for conventional and controlled plots must be established before the reasons behind differing outflow patterns can be separated and quantified. \textcite{Smith2011b} note that continued investigation in years with normal precipitation may clarify whether controlled drainage offers advantages over conventional systems for water conservation, and that further work using shallow drainage under various climatic conditions may reveal its NO$_3$-N reduction potential. \textcite{Madramootoo2024} suggest that controlled-drainage adoption may become more relevant under wetter, warmer climates, and highlight the need for practical water-table control-structure management during wetter springs to mitigate NO$_3$-N concentrations while allowing timely field operations. \textcite{Bakhsh2007} additionally suggest variable-rate controlled drainage may be helpful. \textcite{Schott2017b} identify economic and agronomic questions: whether drainage treatment affects yields (via SEW$_{30}$) during springs with frequent, heavy precipitation, how such conditions affect planting dates, and how the economic risk of reducing NO$_3$ loss with drainage water management can be better evaluated. \textcite{Miller2022} argue that more work is needed to understand the collective impacts of automated drainage water management (ADWM) on the coupled timing of flows and composition of water leaving fields, and how slowing tile flows affects nutrient export, before its full efficacy as a BMP can be assessed. \textcite{Barkle2021} suggest water-table management and edge-of-field bioreactors as mitigation options depending on site conditions.

\subsection{Edge-of-field and in-field treatment technologies}
A distinct research frontier concerns engineered nitrate-removal structures---bioreactors, saturated riparian buffers, constructed wetlands, and filter-based drainage---where design optimization and long-term field performance remain open. \textcite{McEachran2020} state that a key remaining challenge is to develop better ways to predict nitrate removal so that more effective saturated riparian buffers (SRBs) can be designed and implemented; they outline methodological advances including deriving an unsteady form of the governing equation by solving a one-dimensional diffusion equation for the head, computing average linear velocity, and solving nitrate transport numerically, and note that using an optimal-width approximation would help determine effective designs. \textcite{Jaynes2014} propose that if their SRB results can be replicated across landscapes, the practice could prevent substantial NO$_3$ from entering midwestern streams, and that if sequestration is an important removal pathway, biomass harvesting (perhaps as bioenergy) could maintain long-term viability. \textcite{Abdalaal2023} recommend improving how nitrate removal is represented in buffer designs, incorporating more sophisticated removal and hydrology components, coupling design with economic analysis or user objective functions targeting specific annual load-reduction goals, and first determining maximum allowable buffer length before optimizing width to meet load-removal and financial targets.

For bioreactors and combined installations, \textcite{Husk2017} call for further study of controlled drainage with and without a denitrification bioreactor to quantify each component's contribution, note that treating greater water volumes at lower efficiency could improve overall N mass removal (to be tested in performance-optimization studies), stress verification with more on-farm installations, and warn that the suitability of slowing subsurface flow through the bioreactor to filter suspended solids and colloids must be evaluated given clogging potential. \textcite{Vallée2014} encourage planting vegetation and maintaining organic matrices (straw, dead leaves) inside reactive riparian zones (RRZs) to increase effectiveness, since plants could raise efficiency through absorption and contributions to degradation processes. \textcite{Randall2001} propose constructing wetland restoration areas or denitrifying ponds to treat drainage water before it enters ditches or rivers. \textcite{Mendes2021} outline a broad research agenda for constructed treatment systems: thoroughly investigating the driving factors behind performance to support local implementation and nitrogen-removal compliance, clarifying the relationship between water-flow dynamics and nitrogen treatment, elucidating design-parameter effects, conducting long-term field-scale tests of nitrogen biogeochemical processes, investigating volatilization in surface-flow constructed wetlands under anaerobic basic conditions, and developing operations or interventions to enhance winter efficiency and stabilize year-round performance. For filter-based drainage, \textcite{Tao2022} note that over long operation, nitrification and denitrification in the soil and filter cannot be neglected, that long-term field experiments with different filter materials are needed, and that explaining adsorption, desorption, nitrification, and denitrification for different filter materials may be a new area of future exploration.

\subsection{Process-based modeling: outstanding algorithmic and evaluation gaps}
Because field trials cannot cover all combinations of practice, soil, and climate, process-based models are expected to fill the gap---yet the literature documents extensive, still-unresolved modeling needs.

\subsubsection{Soil carbon--nitrogen cycling, plant uptake, and crop growth}
Many authors identify soil N-process representation as the weakest link. \textcite{Smith2020} recommend improving biogeochemical processes such as nitrification, denitrification, fermentation, and mineralization, and using an improved hydrology framework to target denitrification at more appropriate ranges of soil water and oxygen contents. \textcite{Fang2012} attribute high errors in monthly flow-weighted nitrate-concentration predictions to the complexity of crop N uptake, soil N cycling, and transformations among C and N pools, and call for improvement there. \textcite{Hu2007} argue that improving SWAT's simulation of soil N processes, soybean N fixation, and maize-yield simulation could yield more reliable predictions of nutrient-management outcomes in tile-drained watersheds. \textcite{Malone2019} recommend modifying SWAT's nutrient-cycling algorithms to improve immobilization and other processes relevant to corn-stover removal, comparing SWAT and RZWQM at the same site with stover-harvest data, modifying RZWQM's stover-harvest routine to remove greater portions of the upper stalk, and using the modified RZWQM of Fang et al.\ (2017) for more accurate grain and stover N. \textcite{Ma2007} list needed RZWQM improvements: accounting for subsurface lateral flow below tiles between adjacent plots, simulating N fixation rather than supplying all soybean N, improving plant N uptake, incorporating surface roughness/detention storage in runoff, reducing winter nitrification so more soil NH$_4$-N remains at planting, and applying system models to long-term field studies while evaluating all components rather than one. \textcite{Ma2007b} reiterate the need for better simulation of management effects on yield and N mineralization, improved soybean N fixation and plant N uptake, and enhanced post-tillage mineralization, and recommend extending RZWQM to other tile-drained Midwest sites to evaluate hypoxia-alleviating practices. \textcite{Ma2008} add that crop responses to N-application timing need improvement, that use of RZWQM without soil microbial growth needs evaluation for other soils and climates (especially where water is limited and soil organic C differs), and that rigorous evaluation of each soil C/N process and pool against data is required before microbial growth's role can be understood. \textcite{Ma2001} propose a wide-ranging RZWQM agenda: reexamining MSEA datasets with the newest version, introducing variable cracking or effective macroporosity, adding phosphorus soil dynamics for manure management, including mobile organic-matter movement for long-term simulation, studying plant responses to water and N stresses and parameterizing new crops such as wheat, standardizing calibration and database development, evaluating responses to irrigation/fertilization/manure/pesticide management at multiple locations, further evaluating new modules (temperature, freeze/snow heat transport, overland-flow and sediment routing, gas emissions), linking RZWQM to a GIS with economic decision support, and modularizing the model. \textcite{Marjerison2016} report ongoing revision of winter processes and of crop-available-N estimation as suitable datasets emerge, plus work to improve simulation of organic-C mineralization from SOM, residue, and manures and of gaseous N losses in the PNM model. \textcite{Bechtold2007} note that a more robust approach to quantifying grass biomass and N uptake may be needed to simulate C and N dynamics in drained grassland. At the watershed scale, \textcite{Qi2025} recommend developing a more physically based model to represent NO$_3^-$ leaching through tile drainage, pairing outlet NO$_3^-$ loads with soil-moisture and N$_2$O observations to reduce uncertainty in denitrification and N-gas estimates, using ``soft data'' to simulate within-watershed dynamics rather than only outlet loads, adding crop-yield/biomass observations to improve growth-induced N dynamics, and introducing a passive humus pool to address underestimation of organic-N loss via surface runoff.

\subsubsection{Winter, frozen-soil, and snow processes}
Cold-season representation is repeatedly singled out as inadequate. \textcite{Feyereisen2006b} state that better algorithms are needed for soil freezing/thawing and spring-thaw drainage events, that more detailed mechanistic plant-N-uptake representation is needed, and that more detailed snow--freeze--thaw algorithms are still being sought for agronomic systems models. \textcite{Yang2007} call for improving DRAINMOD-N to estimate NO$_3$-N loss more accurately in freezing and thawing seasons and to include inputs such as tillage and intercropping. \textcite{Saadat2020} propose investigating such processes in drained fields to improve DRAINMOD predictions in seasonally frozen regions. \textcite{Jiang2020} note that additional winter data---snow depth, melting, and frost-affected hydraulic conductivity---may help test and improve the model. \textcite{Li2024b} suggest adapting an impedance factor for the RZ-SHAW model's frozen-soil hydraulic conductivity, including soil ice-content measurements in field experiments, implementing a calibratable snow--rain air-temperature threshold, and devoting more attention to new winter management for planting windows and nutrient loss. \textcite{Ahmed2007b} state that continued development and testing of RZWQM for winter conditions is required, along with further testing of ET and plant-water-uptake components, simulation of a wider range of crops for BMP evaluation in sensitive groundwater regions, and more field work to identify coefficient values for the OMNI nutrient sub-model. \textcite{Ahmed2007} similarly recommend improving RZWQM's snow algorithm, incorporating the McBride (1983) water-retention model and Brooks--Corey parameter adjustments for Ontario silt loam, more field work for nutrient-component coefficients, and further testing of ET and plant-water-uptake components. \textcite{Yang2021} indicate it is probably necessary to improve RZWQM2's accuracy in simulating winter runoff-N in winter-wheat cover-crop systems. \textcite{Roy2020} call for research on swelling and shrinkage during infiltration of water into frozen soil. \textcite{Motarjemi2021} require improved winter-crop parameterization, further attention to soil-surface and macropore infiltration processes, evaluation of the Daisy macropore setup where drain-connected macropores exist (with extensive parameter calibration), studies revealing N-loss trends under current practices, and improved management to lower NO$_3$-N in drainage under conventional rotations.

\subsubsection{Macropores, preferential flow, and drain configuration}
\textcite{Smith2020} recommend improving runoff simulation under surface crusting, clay cracking, preferential flow, snow dynamics, and freeze--thaw. \textcite{Stamm2002} argue that more attention should be given to actual drain configuration to complete the picture of factors determining solute transport in drained soils. \textcite{Chelil2022b} propose upgrading SIDRA-RU with a new nitrate-transport model to assimilate nitrate observations, investigating the effect of sampling interval (weekly/bi-weekly versus daily discharge), examining second-order interactions when approximating total-effect Sobol indices, integrating a crop-rotation module if data allow, using water-level, concentration, and flux data to better constrain solutions, and considering time-variant parameter estimation with hourly discharge. \textcite{Jeantet2021} suggest adding reference sites to improve SIDRA-RU robustness, treating the S$_{inter}$ parameter as a variable adjusted to prior-year meteorology and soil parameters, coupling with the PESTDRAIN module for pesticide leaching, and combining SIDRA-RU with a nitrate-leaching module to assess water pollution on French drained plots.

\subsubsection{Drain-design decision support and validation}
\textcite{Moriasi2013} propose using the Revised SWAT model to determine optimum drain depth and spacing that reduce NO$_3$-N losses while optimizing yield, but stress that validation studies of the Hooghoudt and Kirkham tile-drain equations in Revised SWAT are needed before use as a decision-support tool. \textcite{Ale2012} recommend validating a nitrate-load methodology by comparison with N models such as DRAINMOD-NII or RZWQM and by evaluation in additional watersheds where loads from both tile drains and streamflow have been measured. \textcite{Ng2000} note that NO$_3$-N leaching modeling for at least one year is required and that further calibration with consecutive multi-year data may improve prediction for free-drainage treatments. \textcite{Skaggs1995} caution that additional testing for other soils, crops, and locations is needed before routine model application. \textcite{Malone2007} argue that overcoming regression-based limitations and comprehensively quantifying yield and nitrate loading requires process-based models, that simulation results under different conditions could feed a database for economic and risk analysis, and that developing regression equations is a step toward a simple, accurate, objective method to quantify management and climate effects on nitrate loading and yield for a region.

\subsubsection{Groundwater interactions and integrated transport}
Coupling drainage models to groundwater is a distinct need. \textcite{Addab2022} recommend using SWAT-MODFLOW-RT3D for physically based, spatially distributed flow and reactive-transport modeling once a salinity module is incorporated, simulating groundwater storage, flow, and groundwater--tile-drain interactions in a physically based manner, and exploring the balance between improved crop yield in tiled areas and decreased downstream water quality. \textcite{Hoang2017} state that improving SWAT's groundwater module to simulate water-table fluctuation, soil saturation, and groundwater--surface-water exchange is necessary to extend SWAT\_LS validity and realistically represent nitrate retention and removal in riparian zones. \textcite{Hojberg2017} argue that optimal regulation requires considering both spatial variation in N leaching and variation in N reduction across groundwater and surface-water systems. \textcite{Elsayed2023} propose developing integrated mathematical models for nutrient transport in surface-water--groundwater interactions, evaluating climate-change effects, assessing crop-management influences, applying machine-learning algorithms to nutrient-transport datasets, and better understanding governing factors of in-stream sediment and nutrient transport. \textcite{Barkle2021} note that site-specific investigations establishing defensible water balances, or detailed groundwater-flow modeling, are needed to realistically estimate the split between artificial drainage and groundwater flows. \textcite{Yang2008} call for further work to improve NH$_4$-N transport and transformation modeling.

\subsection{Greenhouse-gas tradeoffs and indirect N$_2$O emissions}
A prominent frontier is the coupling between nitrate losses and greenhouse-gas emissions, especially indirect N$_2$O. \textcite{OBrien2022} suggest that continued investigation of the linkages between nitrate leaching and N$_2$O emissions may reveal different dynamics when N application and precipitation are temporally disconnected, and note the need to identify strategies that mitigate losses and adapt to changing precipitation and temperature. \textcite{Sawamoto2003} call for more studies on the NO$_3$--N$_2$O relationship in groundwater, on both direct and indirect N$_2$O emissions under typical conditions, and specifically on indirect N$_2$O emission from subsurface drainage---particularly the NO$_3$-leaching--N$_2$O-leaching relationship important for predicting indirect emission. \textcite{Minamikawa2015} state that field identification is still required and that new or revised calculation methods for indirect N$_2$O associated with leaching and runoff are needed to reflect differences in dominant microbial processes, along with evaluation of the tradeoff between indirect N$_2$O emission and N removal by heterotrophic denitrification across groundwater, surface drainage, river, and estuary. \textcite{Minamikawa2013} note further investigation is required to evaluate groundwater-induced N$_2$O emissions through the soil surface, and \textcite{Minamikawa2010} call for clarifying relations between redox conditions and greenhouse-gas production in the subsoil, for $^{15}$N isotope analysis of nitrate and N$_2$O to locate N$_2$O production, and for understanding production, consumption, and transport of greenhouse gases in profiles with shallow groundwater. \textcite{Smith2019} suggest that future studies of dissolved N$_2$O losses from drainage water may reveal whether they exceed surface emissions, and that further research is needed on the benefits of controlled drainage as a climate-adaptation option in the Atlantic region. \textcite{Oliveira2025} urge exploration of long-term impacts of management on cumulative dissolved greenhouse-gas emissions to inform climate-smart strategies. \textcite{Webb2021} recommend, for artificial-water emissions accounting, examining seasonal impacts on emission factors, developing a process-based model as a likely improvement over revising default EFs (supported by more studies), developing gas-transfer-velocity models across specific artificial-water types, exploring N$_2$O-production thresholds in high-NO$_3$ environments, investigating the DOC/NO$_3$ ratio, building a global database on artificial-water size, density, and distribution (beyond the UK), and targeting field measurements across major agricultural industries. \textcite{Fontes2019} plan to monitor both N$_2$O and NO$_3$-N leaching year-round across all experimental units for better total-N-loss estimates, encourage testing remote-sensing technologies for environmental monitoring, and stress attention to temporal and spatial methodology when evaluating both loss pathways together.

\subsection{Monitoring, tracers, and source apportionment}
Improved measurement and tracing underpin many proposed advances. \textcite{Williams2022} call for higher-frequency measurements (sub-daily to daily), further work on the role of groundwater dynamics in DRP transport (including intra- and inter-event concentration patterns and field differences), more comprehensive methods for apportioning nutrient sources to improve estimates of soil and incidental-fertilizer loads, and further exploration of groundwater dynamics' effect on nutrient delivery to drains. \textcite{Hu2024} recommend quantifying $\delta^{18}$O$_{\mathrm{NO_3,nit}}$ in soils across a broad range of environments to understand its variability under varying thermodynamic and kinetic conditions, and coupling $\delta^{18}$O$_{\mathrm{H_2O}}$ and $\delta^{18}$O$_{\mathrm{NO_3,nit}}$ measurements in evaporating soils to quantify evaporative enrichment. \textcite{Briand2017} argue it is essential to monitor spatial heterogeneity and temporal variability of nitrate concentrations and isotopic compositions in topsoil, drains, and ditches to characterize soil's contribution to river nitrate, with temporal monitoring and multiple tracers needed to explain variations. \textcite{Lagzdins2015} highlight the need for additional research on interactions between drain flow and TN/NO$_3$-N concentration variation, and for more detailed analysis of the interaction between timing and rate of mineral fertilizer and outlet TN/TP concentrations. \textcite{Danalatos2022} ask whether near-term changes in flow-weighted nitrate concentration are predictive of long-term changes in nitrate load and yield. \textcite{Turunen2013} call for modeling and measurement studies to quantify the contribution of groundwater outflow to nutrient loading of surface waters and to study how hydrological changes are reflected in nutrient losses and applied in water-protection design. \textcite{Jokinen2024} recommend that groundwater outflow be considered in future N-loss studies and that fractions other than mineral N be considered when modeling nutrient leaching. \textcite{Barkle2021} add that accurate total-N-export estimates at sites with significant groundwater recharge require measuring all relevant N forms and accounting for the redox status of the underlying groundwater.

\subsection{Scaling, spatial targeting, and thresholds}
Translating site findings to landscapes and defining actionable thresholds constitute another set of open problems. \textcite{Bakhsh2005b} conclude that delineated zones transporting higher NO$_3$-N leaching losses require site-specific management to minimize off-site losses. \textcite{Smith2023} propose finding the NO$_3$-N threshold across a range of P saturation, waterlogging vulnerability, soil textures, organic-carbon availability, and temperature to improve the ability to restrict N and P loss, note that scaling up will require multiple techniques, and suggest combining GIS with hydrological modeling and saturation risk to assess DRP and NO$_3$-N loss from intensifying land. \textcite{Kennedy2012} argue that better understanding the coupled effect of controlling factors and drainage density in agricultural catchments is required for a complete mechanistic synthesis of stream NO$_3^-$ and Cl$^-$ dynamics, and that understanding watershed solute-export responses to increased drainage density requires more quantitative understanding of soil-water and groundwater transport via tile drains. \textcite{Salo2006} suggest identifying critical precipitation periods that create runoff, rather than using annual values, and using the resulting equations to estimate N-leaching risks in, for example, life-cycle analysis.

\subsection{Standards, policy, economics, and cross-cutting tradeoffs}
Finally, several authors point to the need for standards, economic analysis, and explicit tradeoff evaluation. \textcite{Elmi2004} note that developing drain-effluent quality guidelines for NO$_3$-N concentration offers a great challenge, and \textcite{Lagzdins2015} argue that scientifically based standards for water-quality classification in agricultural areas would help define nutrient criteria for agricultural runoff separately from rivers. \textcite{Nash2015d} recommend evaluating whether split N application with managed drainage can further reduce annual NO$_3^-$-N loss through tile drainage from claypan soils, noting it may be possible to further reduce losses from tile-drained claypan soils in the midwestern U.S. Overarching tradeoffs remain unresolved: \textcite{Derby2024} state that the tradeoff of increased NO$_3$-N losses remains unclear and requires further evaluation, and \textcite{Addab2022} likewise call for exploring the balance between crop-yield gains in tiled areas and downstream water-quality losses. \textcite{Gamble2018} emphasize unresolved tradeoffs among N-loss pathways when switching to fertigation of manure. Broader research-and-verification programs are also invoked: \textcite{BAKER1975} describe an early two-part project in which nitrogen was to be applied at 100--300 kg~ha$^{-1}$ to determine the effect of fertilization rate on nitrogen losses, illustrating the long-standing nature of these questions, while \textcite{Dattamudi2020} conclude that better N-management planning must be implemented to reduce nitrate-N contamination in tile drainage. \textcite{Fontes2019} frame the ultimate goal as providing timely, accurate information to enhance the efficiency and sustainability of food production---capturing the applied orientation that unifies this research frontier.

3.body:
\subsection{Overview}
The design of edge-of-field denitrifying bioreactors and their companion treatment structures has matured to the point where discrete performance demonstrations exist, yet the field is still defined more by open questions than by settled design rules. The literature synthesised here converges on several interlocking research frontiers: (i) how to size and configure bioreactors robustly, including for undersized systems and hydrologically flashy sites; (ii) how to select, amend, and manage reactive media---woodchips, corncobs, and specialised phosphorus-sorbing materials---to extend performance and lifespan; (iii) how to constrain the pollution-swapping and unintended by-products (greenhouse gases, dissolved organic carbon, phosphorus release, methylmercury) that accompany reductive treatment; (iv) how contaminants such as nitrate, phosphorus, pesticides, pathogens, and antibiotic resistance genes are transported through drainage inlets and macropore networks; and (v) how longer-term, multi-site monitoring and improved models can turn site-scale results into transferable design guidance. A recurring meta-message across studies is the call for standardised design procedures, broader performance-metric reporting, and long-term data collected across climates, landscapes, and maturation stages so that watershed-scale nutrient-reduction goals can be met \parencite{Christianson2021,Maxwell2022,Bell2015}. The subsections below organise these gaps by topic while retaining the specific hypotheses, methods, and conditions the primary studies identified as still needing resolution.

\subsection{Bioreactor sizing, scaling, and design procedures}
A central frontier is the reconciliation of site-level effectiveness with watershed-scale load removal. \textcite{Christianson2026} argue that effort should be directed toward better balancing bioreactors that are as effective as practical at the site level while still removing as much nitrogen load as possible, note that it would be valuable to assess the performance of undersized bioreactors in larger drainage areas, and suggest that greater flexibility in design processes may help scale the practice to those larger areas; they further caution that additional empirical data are needed to strengthen the loading-density relationship and that performance data from full-size bioreactors beyond Illinois are required to refine prediction models before they are used for design. In parallel, \textcite{Dougherty2020b} propose that particularly flashy drainage sites could consider sizing up bioreactor designs to meet performance goals, and repeatedly call for exploring simpler, standardised ``plug-and-play'' design approaches, positioning their flow analysis as a step toward a simplified design procedure. Standardisation is echoed at the practice-policy level by \textcite{Christianson2021}, who recommend refining the Conservation Practice Standard and streamlining the design process.

The adequacy of residence-time (T)-centric design is itself questioned. \textcite{deOliveira2023} advocate additional field-scale assessment of baffles in relatively wide bioreactors to increase hydraulic loading while maintaining hydraulic efficiency, call for more tracer testing at full-size bioreactors of varying designs coupled with nitrate monitoring and modelling, and specifically flag the weaknesses of current T-centric design approaches as a target for optimisation---including field-scale tracer testing of non-standard media. Foundational design-procedure validation remains incomplete: \textcite{Christianson2013} call for further field-scale validation of the drainage-bioreactor design procedure against performance data from bioreactors of different designs, and \textcite{Ghane2015} recommend validating hydraulic models at additional denitrification-bed sites and using them to develop optimal design guidelines, alongside using additional conservative tracers such as isotope tracers to better characterise bed hydraulics. Region-specific parameterisation is also outstanding; \textcite{Povilaitis2020} note that flow rate, seasonality, microbial community, and other limiting factors must be evaluated to obtain reliable bioreactor design parameters for Lithuania, and \textcite{Ghane2019} present bromide data intended to support the development of further sorption experiments underpinning tracer-based design.

\subsection{Reactive media, carbon sources, and substrate innovation}
Beyond conventional woodchips, several alternative fill media and carbon-management strategies remain incompletely characterised. \textcite{Law2023} encourage more field studies of corncobs as a bioreactor medium, including longer-term research capturing spatial and temporal variability for robust cost analysis, evaluation of the nitrogen removal rate range and whether a lower maximum hydraulic residence time (HRT) is needed, investigation of unintended pollution swapping such as nutrient and pesticide leaching, and assessment of greenhouse-gas production and biophysical properties under different environmental and operating conditions. \textcite{Porter2015} call for research into organic-carbon availability at different depths while accounting for moisture content, noting that if a link between moisture, carbon availability, and performance can be established, future designs could be altered---for example by using different substrates or by raising or lowering internal water levels---and suggest extending short-term microbial-diversity studies over longer periods to reveal recurring annual patterns in alpha diversity.

Carbon dosing and synthetic-carbon supplementation are active but unresolved. \textcite{Rivas2020} identify the specifics of adding readily available carbon---source, frequency, and amount---to optimise dissolved reactive phosphorus (DRP) and performance under highly dynamic inflow, while cautioning that more work is needed to minimise excess dissolved organic carbon (DOC) in outflow and potential biofilm clogging when carbon dosing is used. \textcite{Zhang2021b} identify continued study of operational parameters---especially HRT---the hybrid application of synthetic carbon sources, the design of low-temperature-tolerant reactors, and the mixing of synthetic carbon sources for complex water bodies as worthwhile directions. Microbial amendment strategies also require scale-up testing: \textcite{Jeglot2022} recommend that bioaugmentation, possibly combined with biostimulation, be tested under replicated pilot-scale conditions, with further testing to provide empirical and statistical evidence for upscaling to full-scale bioreactors treating agricultural drainage water. At the mechanistic end, \textcite{Ma2021} call for investigation of the inhibition and denitrifier-enhancement mechanisms caused by heated tourmaline and for field-scale tests of such bioremediation strategies for subsurface drainage water.

\subsection{Envelopes, filter aggregates, and structural materials}
A distinct materials frontier concerns drain envelopes and structural fill. \textcite{Kaboosi2012} recommend evaluating envelopes with different combination ratios of rice husk, sand, and gravel and conducting field experiments under real conditions before drawing conclusions on rice husk as a drain envelope, while \textcite{Navabian2017} stress long-term field experiments to determine the environmental and technical function of a combined rice-husk-and-sand envelope, given the degradation potential of rice husk and its tendency to create acidic conditions in the envelope medium. For aggregate sizing, \textcite{Byrne2023} recommend that aggregates ranging from 0.7 to 19~mm be tested in situ in clay-textured soils, with the identified optimal subset subsequently field-tested. Synthetic envelopes require material-by-material evaluation: \textcite{Byrne2023b} note that needle-punched non-woven geotextile envelopes need further testing, that thicker synthetic envelopes with performance comparable to aggregates may be required, that each synthetic envelope must be tested individually because of varying physical properties, and that filter-sock-plus-aggregate combinations should not be recommended until further research is carried out. Structural concrete alternatives also remain untested at scale; \textcite{Wang2016c} call for research into the practical application and durability of fly-ash- and coal-gangue-based concrete under field conditions with high groundwater levels.

\subsection{Phosphorus-removal media and filter design}
Phosphorus-sorption materials (PSMs) constitute one of the most heavily populated research-gap areas, spanning material selection, longevity, regeneration, and hydraulic integration. General selection criteria are laid out by \textcite{Li2020}, who argue that adsorbent choice must account for phosphorus concentration, interfering ions, temperature, flow rate, and sediment loads, prioritise selectivity for phosphorus at neutral pH, withstand seasonally fluctuating temperature, redox condition, flow rate, and sediment load without sacrificing removal efficiency, be testable for reusability and ease of recovery, and be economical for large field-scale application. \textcite{Mendes2022} add that the apparently synergistic retention mechanism and performance of filter media at high flow rates remain unknown, and that recycling and regeneration of spent filter media should be verified and pursued.

Iron- and steel-based media dominate the specific proposals. \textcite{Allred2017} call for optimising a modified iron-oxyhydroxide regeneration process using dilute hydrochloric acid followed by a NaOH flush, developing practices for collecting and treating the resulting acid/NaOH effluent, testing larger iron-oxyhydroxide systems capable of treating a greater percentage of total drainage flow, and evaluating low-cost goethite sources such as bog iron ore. \textcite{Allred2014} recommend more laboratory testing of zero-valent iron and related media to determine minimum contact time for phosphate removal, higher-flow-rate column tests, rigorous adsorption-isotherm tests to identify removal mechanisms, and field-scale filter treatment studies as a follow-on. \textcite{Hua2016} note that steel by-products may also remove methylmercury and may be regenerated by alkaline washing to recover adsorbed phosphate. \textcite{Sellner2019b} suggest connecting a steel by-product filter to a woodchip bioreactor only after the initial bioreactor flush (e.g. 1--2 weeks) to minimise DOC interference, and mixing fine steel particles with other media to increase hydraulic conductivity and reduce clogging. \textcite{Wang2020d} call for optimising the relationship among removal efficiency, retention time, steel-slag mass, and cost when designing a P-removal structure, while \textcite{Shedekar2020} plan to replace spent slag with a lower-cost gravel--metal-shavings mixture, note that future structures should use a thicker slag bed and reduced length to improve uniform flow distribution, and emphasise controlling sediment influx---especially from bank erosion---as a design consideration. \textcite{Penn2020} recommend PSMs that remove phosphorus via ligand exchange onto aluminium and iron oxide/hydroxide minerals (mine-drainage residuals, manufactured PSMs, aluminium-coated steel slag) and note that such materials suit tile-drainage treatment only where structures are designed for high-frequency media replacement---every 4--6 months in their study.

Fly-ash and pellet media raise saturation-timing questions. \textcite{Li2017} stress that saturation time must be quantified before field application so that fly-ash pellets can be replaced before they shift from phosphorus sink to source, and \textcite{Li2018b} direct follow-up research toward the hydraulic parameters of fly-ash pellets combined with recovery, and toward constraints such as poor saturation time, high outflow pH, and possible field desorption. Filter-media blends more broadly remain unoptimised: \textcite{Agrawal2011} call for determining the feasibility of filter installation and long-term field-scale performance, and \textcite{Agrawal2011b} report a need to determine the optimal media blend and configuration, to identify which constituent(s) offload phosphate and for how long before saturation, and to design larger or new filters capable of accommodating high drainage flows with sufficient contact time. Contact-time resolution itself is flagged by \textcite{Kumari2025}, who suggest exploring shorter contact-time intervals (e.g., 30~min) to capture the rapid initial adsorption phase. Anisotropic-medium behaviour warrants further study per \textcite{Kankarej2023}. Integrated woodchip-plus-PSM designs need multi-scale evaluation: \textcite{Husk2018} call for assessing mixed-media phosphorus-removal effectiveness under additional climate, soil, and cropping conditions over multiyear periods and at different scales to develop predictive design models, evaluating alternative or multiple media placements within bioreactors, examining effective P-removal lifespan and reuse/regeneration/recycling potential, and studying operational parameters such as pH, biochemical oxygen demand, and conductivity, including microbially mediated processes and removal of agrochemicals and microbial contaminants.

\subsection{Novel phosphorus dynamics and monitoring techniques}
Emerging mechanisms of phosphorus interaction with woodchips remain to be elucidated. \textcite{Zhang2024b} call for focused experiments on the processes leading to enhanced orthophosphate uptake by woodchips undergoing anoxic--(anaerobic--)oxic cycling, phenotypic identification of polyphosphate-accumulating organisms in woodchip biofilms to confirm enhanced-biological-phosphorus-removal-like mechanisms, and determination of whether anaerobic conditions are necessary to promote the observed uptake. Redox-driven phosphorus release is a recurring monitoring gap: \textcite{Bailon2025} call for evaluating bioreactor start-up flushes with immediate monitoring, aging bioreactors, and total phosphorus alongside higher-frequency DRP monitoring, and for further assessment of redox-driven P release and the use of drawdown plates. \textcite{Rivas2020} likewise call for continued multi-season monitoring to confirm the longer-term DRP removal potential of woodchip bioreactors and further study of the dynamics and factors determining DRP removal. Passive-sampling monitoring itself needs development: \textcite{Li2020b} call for a more reactive hybrid resin adaptable to a wide range of temperature and flow rate, investigation of particulate-phosphorus effects on passive-sampling estimates, mesh-bag materials with reduced biofouling for long-term deployment, and extension of the technique to large-scale watershed and river-basin monitoring. Fundamental characterisation of dissolved organic phosphorus is sought by \textcite{Liu2024b}, who call for investigating the roles of the origin and management of organic matter and organic P on P lability and DOP leaching, and the bioavailability of DOP in recipient waters.

\subsection{Pollution swapping and unintended by-products}
Reductive treatment introduces trade-offs that are only partly quantified. \textcite{Christianson2021} name reducing pollution swapping---especially at start-up---and improving understanding of the removal of additional pollutants, compounds, and biologicals as key research areas. Greenhouse-gas emissions are a prominent concern: \textcite{Brunton2024} recommend further greenhouse-gas research on bioreactors, fuller assessment of full-size bioreactor methane dynamics, examination of the impacts of bioreactor age and associated woodchip properties on GHG consumption and release, evaluation of ``hot moment'' GHG production, additional monitoring of methane production at new bioreactors, longer-term analyses paired with in situ woodchip physical and chemical property measurements, and further field-scale investigation of soil-cover benefits and trade-offs; they also suggest testing sophisticated automated control structures for real-time management based on outflow oxidation-reduction potential. \textcite{Strock2025} note that, because a 2-h HRT has been shown to increase greenhouse-gas emissions, additional research on this design consequence is needed, along with work on the longevity of P-removal materials, optimisation of performance under cold conditions, monitoring of DOC export, and strategies for managing P-treatment materials to minimise unintended phosphorus release. \textcite{Maxwell2022} suggest that minimising negative by-products such as mercury methylation could be based on an outflow nitrate-concentration design criterion rather than a mass metric. Nitrite and long-term P transport are flagged by \textcite{Bell2015}, who call for measuring nitrite concentrations in future bioreactor studies and for long-term evaluation of phosphorus transport from bioreactors. Carbon-export accounting more broadly is a monitoring gap: \textcite{Schilling2023} call for improved monitoring of aqueous total-carbon export for accurate carbon-sequestration accounting, multiple years of data to characterise carbon export under variable climate, and note that reducing water throughput via water-level control structures may minimise dissolved-inorganic-carbon loss.

\subsection{Longevity, clogging, subsidence, and construction}
The operational lifespan and physical stability of treatment structures are widely cited as under-studied. \textcite{Kohn2023} state that additional research is needed before widespread implementation, that bioreactor-function longevity is an important consideration, that estimating replacement interval and cost depends on key unknowns (performance, lifespan, and actual producer cost of agricultural residues), and that using a pre-measured feedstock volume per bioreactor with consistent placement could mitigate construction-method effects. Chemical clogging is mechanistically unresolved: \textcite{Guo2020} call for exploring the mechanism of chemical clogging and developing simulation models for technical prevention, a need echoed by \textcite{Guo2021}. Subsidence monitoring is a new methodological frontier; \textcite{Christianson2025b} suggest applying their LiDAR method at additional sites---especially following construction---to build subsidence time series, addressing whether subsidence is faster immediately after construction, collecting 3D surface data immediately post-construction, and assessing the practicality and benefit of targeted recharge near the inlet. Sediment management at construction is a design frontier for catchment-scale systems: \textcite{Feyereisen2023} plan to investigate the opposing effects of bypass flow (decreasing removal) and nitrate removal rate (increasing removal) in a sediment-exclusion system so managers can understand the net impact, recommend installing data-logging pressure transducers in each bed from project outset, and advise a pre-construction investigation of sedimentation sources with sediment-prevention measures included in design. Network-scale diagnostic methods also need extension; \textcite{Henine2021} recommend using an air compressor with higher injection pressure and airflow to extend a diagnostic method to much larger subsurface-drainage networks, monitoring electrical-resistivity variations with time-domain frequency electromagnetic methods and precise geolocation, and developing a safe air-injection system operable directly from the drain outlet.

\subsection{Pumped and energy-assisted bioreactor designs}
Pumping to increase hydraulic loading is an emerging but incompletely validated design avenue. \textcite{DíazGarcía2025} call for additional assessment of different pumping configurations and bioreactor designs to contextualise benefits and drawbacks, note that small but consistent post-batching temperature increases merit further study, observe that a power-storage unit could have increased flow treatment, identify operating pumps continuously for 24~h and doubling the pumping window to six months as hypothetical ways to increase hydraulic loading, and caution that pumping should perhaps be considered only in the most suitable applications. \textcite{Hartfiel2024} call for exploring solar-powered pumped bioreactors to reduce operating and unit costs, verifying pumped-bioreactor performance before policy implications can be realised, and optimising system performance while reducing unit cost. Pumped constructed-wetland-plus-woodchip-bioreactor systems similarly require further work: \textcite{Pugliese2020} call for testing that analyses the concentration and proportion of particulate and dissolved nutrient forms during highly variable, intense discharges, a cost-effectiveness analysis of the synergistic surface-flow-wetland-plus-woodchip-bioreactor combination versus standalone systems, and longer monitoring to define the pumping effect on total-phosphorus retention.

\subsection{Contaminant transport through drainage inlets and macropores}
Understanding how contaminants enter and move through drainage networks is explicitly under-studied. \textcite{Li2017b} recommend additional research on how nitrate, phosphorus, and pesticides are transported through drainage inlets, studies of inlet performance under different debris from different crop systems, and modification of the flow equations to represent actual inlet performance under debris conditions. The definition of a bioreactor's effective contributing area is itself uncertain; \textcite{Akay2008} question whether the contributing area should vary for water, tracers, and reactive contaminants. Sediment behaviour within tiles is likewise unresolved: \textcite{Stone1997} note that the relative importance of bacteria versus solution-chemistry changes on flocculation of tile sediment across a range of turbulent shear stresses remains to be determined.

Pesticide transport modelling forms a coherent cluster of gaps. \textcite{Wendell2024} propose implementing algorithms describing particle-mediated colloidal transport, delineating upland areas and floodplains together with pesticide accumulation when surface runoff infiltrates or evaporates, separately accounting for macropore flow when calculating pesticide leaching, and continuous improvements in modelling and observation to reduce uncertainty in pesticide degradation and transport predictions. \textcite{Zehe2001} argue that convection--dispersion or transfer-function process models must be improved to include pesticide transport by mobile particles and that application guidelines should include soil-specific interactions. \textcite{Fox2007b} identify the need for a quasi-two-dimensional procedure to distribute pesticide across the soil profile (rather than the current one-dimensional approach), site-specific calibration of the enrichment-factor parameter, more sophisticated accounting for macropore/subsurface-drain interconnectivity in chemical routing, and user awareness of model limitations. \textcite{Novak2003} call for better field-scale evaluation of metolachlor retention describing slow immobilisation, and for apparent-dissociation-kinetics processes to be incorporated in registration and management models, especially for persistent pesticides. \textcite{Dousset2004} argue that field-scale monitoring of herbicide losses in drains is necessary to compare molecule behaviour across soil types over at least two planting periods to reliably assess leaching risk, and \textcite{Leu2004} call for more quantitative functions relating available field characteristics to a field's vulnerability to lose herbicides. Analytical and marker methods also need development: \textcite{MUIR1978} note that persistence of triazine degradation products could be modelled under better-controlled conditions and that methods for determining hydroxy-triazines in water are needed for routine monitoring, while \textcite{Heaven2012} suggest 3,5-dichloro-2-methoxybenzoic acid could serve as a chemical marker of dicamba pollution and stress identifying contaminant sources. Continued monitoring is advised by \textcite{Gordon2005} given widespread linuron use in carrot and potato production. Over-winter conditions are singled out by \textcite{Siimes2006}, who call for detailed study of soil freezing/thawing and snowmelt effects on herbicide fate and note their dataset will test pesticide-fate models in over-winter conditions.

\subsection{Pathogens, microbial ecology, and antibiotic resistance}
Microbial contaminant transport and treatment span several open questions. \textcite{Soupir2018} call for investigation of pathogen removal under lower HRTs, examination of both \textit{Salmonella} and \textit{E.\ coli} removal at lower HRT values, exploration of the removal mechanism to inform longevity, and investigation of microbial removal at higher flows. The ecology of indicator bacteria in treatment wetlands is unresolved: \textcite{Stott2023} call for determining the relative survival and growth of faecal \textit{E.\ coli} strains versus non-faecal ``\textit{E.\ coli}-like'' \textit{Escherichia} (cryptic clades) to understand wetlands as sources of \textit{E.\ coli} and downstream health risks, and to confirm or reject whether wetland and aquatic habitats support extended persistence and growth. \textcite{Truhlar2015} ask whether curli expression responds to manure-application method and call for characterising downstream risks associated with curli-producing versus non-producing \textit{E.\ coli}, especially regarding foodborne illness. Long-term pathogen leaching following manure application needs study: \textcite{Malik2004} call for additional temporal and long-term studies on the survival and leaching of pathogens and indicators into subsurface water before firm recommendations can be made for swine-manure application. Fecal-source tracking methods require corroboration; \textcite{Gottschall2013} recommend using other markers---such as pharmaceuticals and personal-care products and fecal indicator bacteria---in tandem with sterols, and using multiple sterol ratios for cross-validation. Irrigation-related pathogen risks lead \textcite{Ding2021} to strongly recommend disinfection of wastewater effluents used for irrigation, lower winter irrigation rates to slow pathogen movement through wet, cold soil, and year-round supplemental disinfection. Broader catchment-scale microbial priorities are set out by \textcite{Collins2007}, including quantifying off-stream water, shade, and shelter sources for cattle; understanding cattle attraction to waterways to prioritise fencing; experimental studies across soils, slopes, and vegetation to improve understanding of microbial entrapment in riparian buffers; evaluating constructed wetlands, vegetated ditches, and farm ponds for treating microbes in stormwater and effluent; assessing the costs of advocated mitigation measures; better understanding water as a pathogen vector for human infection; tracing pathogen strains causing cattle re-infection; and examining links between cattle productivity and drinking-water microbial quality.

Antibiotic resistance genes (ARGs) are an explicitly emerging frontier. \textcite{Smith2019b} call for better understanding of the diversity of ARGs, their mechanisms of persistence and dissemination, and long-term environmental effects, and recommend targeted approaches such as qPCR to complement metagenomic characterisation. \textcite{Rieke2018} note that additional studies are needed to link the presence of resistance genes with host identification and transfer mechanisms.

\subsection{Companion practices: saturated buffers, wetlands, and controlled structures}
Frontier questions extend to practices deployed alongside or instead of bioreactors. For saturated buffers, \textcite{Ghane2025} recommend determining actual performance using a paired-field approach, further economic analysis of the buffer-flow component's cost-effectiveness, and determining average nitrate-load removal by the controlled-drainage and buffer-flow components under varying conditions; \textcite{Jaynes2019b} highlight the need to establish and monitor additional saturated buffers with multipipe configurations. Treatment-wetland performance limits are a persistent theme: \textcite{Tanner2005} call for longer-term studies of sustainable nutrient-removal rates across climatic conditions, process-based laboratory and mesocosm investigations to supplement field studies, determination of whether organic-carbon availability limits denitrification and whether organic-matter supplements or strategic biomass cutting increase removal, evaluation of different flow regimes using hydraulic residence-time distributions and dynamic water-mass-balance models, and further information on attenuation of other contaminants (including pathogens), net greenhouse-gas emissions, and biodiversity effects. \textcite{Tanner2003} recommend continued monitoring across seasons and years, intensive piezometer monitoring to quantify nutrient mass flows, studies of component processes to identify factors limiting nutrient removal, and supplementation of organic-carbon substrates with slow-release materials such as sawdust or straw. \textcite{Tanner2005b} note that further measurement and modelling are needed to estimate the frequency and composition of additional inflow sources, that performance might be optimised through different plant communities, soil types, organic-carbon amendments, or subsurface-flow zones, that longer-term studies are required to tease out event-to-event and year-to-year loading effects, and that larger wetland areas and/or enhanced areal denitrification rates would be needed to substantially reduce nitrogen export from pulsed drainage flows. Land-treatment wetland systems (FILTER) require subtropical adaptation per \textcite{Gardner2001}, who intend to increase applied-effluent nitrogen to representative values (about 25--30~mg~L\textsuperscript{-1} total nitrogen) and to clarify pathogen behaviour by tracking bacteriophage MS-2 after spiking. Controlled and flow-regulating structures also have open questions: \textcite{Popek2021} recommend further research on improving the connection of regulator elements to limit effective flow sizes, and \textcite{Schilling2023} note that water-level control structures may reduce dissolved-inorganic-carbon loss. Water and sediment control basins (WASCoBs) warrant study of pipe-riser type effects on nutrient and sediment transport, event-scale sediment and phosphorus trapping within ponded areas, berm-area-plus-pipe-riser interactions, and the impact of directly routing larger sub-basins to the watershed outlet \parencite{Gupta2019}. Nutrient-loss management and drainage-inlet flow control are further addressed by \textcite{Nédélec2010}, who note the need for additional work on soil-surface clogging, storage-area geometry, and internal-drain discharge capacity, experimental validation of flow, sediment, and pollution-mitigation efficiency, and study of detrimental effects such as bio- or soil accumulation of pollutants.

\subsection{Riparian, hyporheic, and effluent-management processes}
Subsurface attenuation processes adjacent to drainage require broader characterisation. \textcite{Puckett2005} call for more intensive study of hyporheic-zone processes at the study site, establishment of sufficiently wide buffer zones to guarantee adequate residence times, and further work on hydrogeologic controls on nitrate transport through riparian zones across a wider range of settings. Effluent- and manure-management gaps include \textcite{Monaghan2007}, whose ongoing work indicates low-rate effluent irrigation can reduce direct transfers of effluent phosphorus and bacteria through mole-pipe drainage by up to 95\%, and \textcite{Gordon2005}, who call for research on the microbiological safety of liquid-hog-manure practices and long-term (>10~yr) field trials with repeated manure applications to determine water-quality impacts relative to standard fertility practices. Nutrient-application optimisation is targeted by \textcite{Farahani2020}, who call for determining nitrogen and phosphorus application rates and timings to reduce concentrations, and by \textcite{Singh2002}, who recommend splitting a 60~kg~N~ha\textsuperscript{-1} basal dose into three 20~kg~N~ha\textsuperscript{-1} doses at 10-day intervals. Tillage and soil-biology effects on phosphorus leaching remain open: \textcite{Ulén2018} call for further monitoring to confirm any trend of reduced efficiency, long-term studies of untilled and shallow-tillage plots, investigation of biological factors such as earthworm populations, and long-term monitoring to quantify phosphorus-leaching effects, particularly for fields treated in several ways.

\subsection{Selenium and other trace-element legacies}
Trace-element contamination associated with agricultural drainage in the western United States remains a long-horizon research and monitoring commitment. \textcite{BENSON1993} outline continued biological and groundwater monitoring, continued research to identify long-term (10--20 year) strategies for selenium dissipation and removal from surface soils, annual site-management planning submitted to the regulatory board, a pilot-scale demonstration of microbial volatilisation in a former cattail area using a 0.8~ha test plot with irrigation and tillage, and evaluation of a no-action management alternative. \textcite{SAIKI1987} note that additional studies are planned or underway to determine whether fish and other aquatic animals elsewhere in the San Joaquin Valley are also contaminated or adversely affected by selenium. Ecotoxicological monitoring of other agricultural contaminants is likewise recommended: \textcite{Campbell2023} call for investigating the mechanisms behind decreased cerebellum size, a more comprehensive analysis of predation, competition, and other ecological factors at field-collection sites, further examination of the relationship between imidacloprid and imidacloprid-olefin brain levels under field conditions, and regular monitoring of neonicotinoid levels in waterways near agricultural activity with tighter environmental restrictions to protect vulnerable non-target organisms such as amphibians. \textcite{Dreyfuss2018} note that findings for \textit{Galba truncatula} require reviewing the biogeographical status of the species and considering conservation measures given its role as intermediate host in the \textit{Fasciola hepatica} cycle.

\subsection{Cross-cutting priorities: long-term monitoring, metrics, and models}
Synthesising across topics, several priorities recur regardless of practice. First, long-term and multi-site data: \textcite{Bell2015} call for more long-term studies across a variety of landscapes and more field-scale performance data from bioreactors of different designs and multiple Midwest locations; \textcite{Christianson2021} call for more long-term studies to advance bioreactor technologies, and \textcite{Bailon2025} recommend additional monitoring of a variety of bioreactor designs under different climates as treatment systems mature. Second, standardised and expanded performance-metric reporting: \textcite{Maxwell2022} argue that broadly reporting a variety of performance metrics will help scale both achievable treatment and implementation in non-point-source applications. Third, temperature-sensitive and mechanistic models: \textcite{Christianson2021} note that nitrate-removal rate analysis across regional or seasonal temperature gradients would provide added insight and that recent bioreactor models should incorporate temperature sensitivity. Together, these cross-cutting needs frame the overarching research agenda---moving from site-scale demonstration toward validated, transferable design guidance capable of meeting watershed-scale water-quality goals across the diverse climates, soils, and hydrologic regimes in which subsurface drainage operates.

2.body:
\subsection{Overview}

A recurrent theme across the tile-drainage modelling literature is that the credibility of both process-based and empirical models is limited less by their formulation than by the narrowness of the conditions under which they have been tested. Individual studies are typically calibrated at a single experimental site, over a few seasons, and for one soil--climate--management combination, and their authors are nearly unanimous in flagging broader empirical validation as the outstanding priority. The frontier defined by this body of work therefore centres on four interlocking questions: (i) whether calibrated models and their parameters transfer across sites, soil types, spatial scales, and longer time horizons; (ii) what field data and process measurements---particularly for nitrogen and phosphorus biogeochemistry---are still missing to constrain and refine those models; (iii) how calibration, sensitivity analysis, and uncertainty quantification should be conducted so that parameter reliability can actually be assessed; and (iv) how model structure, model coupling, machine-learning methods, and the measurement instruments that supply calibration data should evolve. Explicit calls to ``verify or modify coefficients when the model is applied to a new site'' \parencite{Li2006}, to test performance ``at other sites'' \parencite{Qi2015}, and to confirm simulations with dedicated field studies \parencite{Shipitalo2016,Yousfi2014} recur so consistently that they constitute a shared research agenda rather than isolated caveats. This section synthesises that agenda, moving from the empirical basis for validation, through the data and methods required, to the structural and instrumental advances that would make robust, transferable drainage models attainable.

\subsection{Broadening the Empirical Basis: Sites, Soils, Scales, and Time}

The most frequently articulated need is for validation datasets that span a wider range of physical conditions than any single study can supply. Models are repeatedly reported as requiring testing ``for longer periods of time and for different soil types'' \parencite{Salazar2009}, and across ``different regions and soil types, using extensive field data'' \parencite{Li2026}. \textcite{Li2006} propose the most explicit programme, committing to continued testing ``against more observations across climate zones, soil types, and management regimes'' and anticipating that empirical coefficient constants could eventually be replaced with functions of soil texture or other soil physical properties. \textcite{Chelil2022} frame the same concern in terms of spatial robustness, arguing that testing on additional fields with variable spatial scales and soil properties is needed to confirm that parameters such as nitrate transfer velocities are genuinely transferable. Similar generalisation requirements are stated for erosion and hydrological simulation, which call for data from several field sites and tillage conditions \parencite{Turunen2020}, and for controlled drainage algorithms, whose effectiveness ``under different conditions is critically needed'' before higher credibility can be claimed \parencite{Negm2017}.

Verification at independent sites is presented as an essential precondition for wider application. Authors variously insist that model performance be verified ``at different experimental sites where observed data are available'' \parencite{Azevedo1997}, that testing at other sites support single-site findings \parencite{Qi2015}, and that further validation with additional field-observed data precede broader use \parencite{Kalita1998,Sadhukhan2019b}. Site-specific algorithms developed for spectral or soil-moisture applications are likewise flagged for validation and adaptation before extension to other areas \parencite{Rijal2013}, and inconsistencies producing conflicting parameter values must be resolved before wider deployment \parencite{Dar2022}. Where models are inherently empirical, geographic transfer is envisioned through re-parameterisation with local data on soils, geology, crops, and climate \parencite{Azevedo1997}, and the deduction of region-specific coefficients where suitable reference values exist \parencite{FathAllah2025}.

Extending validation across the climatic and management envelope is a related priority. Models developed under humid conditions are singled out for testing under semi-arid or arid regimes, and for improved simulation of rainfall events following prolonged dry spells \parencite{Prasher1996}. Longer monitoring records are repeatedly invoked as the route to firmer conclusions: continued field measurement over ``a much longer period of time'' is needed to evaluate drainage envelope materials such as rice husk \parencite{Ebrahimian2011}, and more detailed data over longer periods might have altered both system response and apparent model performance \parencite{Negm2017}. Continued field monitoring and modelling are also cited as the means to validate reported results \parencite{Grazhdani1996}, while field verification is recommended before analytical tools are used for design or planning \parencite{Hornbuckle2012}. Design-geometry questions add a further dimension, with calls to explore drain spacings wider than 30~m through additional experiments \parencite{Mathew2001} and to answer regionally how much drainage efficiency loss to expect and which design rainstorm to adopt \parencite{Wiskow2003}. Finally, several authors stress that combinations of measures and models must be adapted and optimised to local field conditions rather than assumed portable \parencite{MULLER1988}, and that field studies remain necessary simply to confirm that simulations hold \parencite{Shipitalo2016,Yousfi2014}.

\subsection{Field Data and Process Measurements Still Needed}

Underlying the demand for broader validation is a scarcity of the process-level measurements needed to constrain the biogeochemical and hydrological routines that drainage models encode.

\subsubsection{Nitrogen biogeochemistry}

Nitrogen dynamics emerge as the least well-constrained component. \textcite{David2009} argue that systematic measurements of denitrification are required to test and refine existing models and to supply data for all major simulated nitrogen fluxes; their idealised design is a multi-year, tile-drained corn--soybean study measuring every major N flux alongside a full suite of physical measurements, with a simpler fallback of measuring a few key fluxes plus denitrification to provide a benchmark for modelled values. A model parameterisation based primarily on field and laboratory measurements, particularly of denitrification, is likewise deemed necessary to improve nitrogen simulation \parencite{Salazar2009}. Within SWAT specifically, improvements to soil-nitrogen algorithms are called for, together with better parameterisation and constraint through more detailed agronomic data, and the routine evaluation and reporting of soil-N fluxes such as mineralisation and denitrification as standard practice \parencite{Ikenberry2017}. Long-term plot-scale studies developing better carbon and nitrogen estimates are proposed to improve parameterisation and to help reconcile the discrepancy between plot- and subbasin-scale results, with cover-crop studies reporting long-term C and N dynamics singled out for validating long-term response simulations \parencite{Singer2011}. Better resolution of the interactions between soil-N processes and nitrogen export is needed to estimate the lag time between best-management-practice implementation and measurable reductions, and individual site assessment is recommended \parencite{Law2022}. Continued measurement and verification of parameters is also planned for improved subsurface drainage under heterogeneous soil nitrogen, where uncertainty prediction is flagged for future assessment \parencite{Tao2021}.

\subsubsection{Phosphorus, sediment, and preferential flow}

Phosphorus transfer is similarly data-limited. Many factors in phosphorus indices require further investigation for their effect on field-to-stream transfer, and a central challenge is to demonstrate that index-based field rankings actually reflect the risk of transfer to open streams \parencite{Bechmann2007}. A risk-class approach relying on a multiple-factor index, supported by a large dataset, is advanced as a more reliable predictor of phosphorus leaching \parencite{Beauchemin2003}. Confirming suggested trends in phosphorus movement is argued to require quantitative measurement of preferential flow using tracers \parencite{Salazar2011}, and higher temporal sampling resolution or tracer studies are more generally needed to prove preferential flow and to expose the processes behind observed concentration patterns \parencite{Tiemeyer2009}. Structural model deficiencies around surface runoff generation, surface phosphorus loading, and phosphorus transport in tiles are identified as priorities for more accurate field-scale representation \parencite{Apostel2021}. Where accurate phosphorus transport dynamics are required, automated samplers or real-time sensors are considered necessary \parencite{Dialameh2022}, and sediment behaviour along drainage ditches is proposed as a target for future measurement, aided by high-resolution topographic survey before and after major storms \parencite{Warsta2014}.

\subsubsection{Sampling design and analytical methods}

Sampling frequency and analytical methodology are recurring practical constraints on validation data quality. Weekly sampling may be required to capture complete nitrate spikes before they are displaced below suction-cup samplers \parencite{Zotarelli2007}, more frequent sampling at the start of the growing season is advised for model validation \parencite{Kalita1998}, and important summer discharge events should be sampled whenever they occur \parencite{Tiemeyer2009}. Further research is called for on the total-nitrogen analysis of aqueous samples by the Dumas method \parencite{Schindler1999}. More broadly, the difficulty and expense of collecting certain field data---such as water depth in potholes---is noted as a reason so few studies exist and a barrier to progress \parencite{Upadhyay2019}. Expanding monitoring networks is offered as part of the solution, with edge-of-field networks providing information to implement field-level management, and remote-sensing advances---LiDAR-refined digital elevation models, satellite-based leaf-area index, and tile-drainage detection---increasing the spatially explicit data available for model inputs and calibration \parencite{Apostel2021}. Improved sampling geometry is also recommended for spatial characterisation, where adding closer points would sharpen semi-variograms and hence the determination of optimal sampling density \parencite{GALLICHAND1992}.

\subsection{Calibration, Sensitivity Analysis, and Uncertainty}

How models are calibrated and how their parameters are interrogated is treated as inseparable from the question of validity. Multiobjective calibration frameworks that integrate statistical and graphical diagnostics, with calibration strategies tailored to the specific hydrological system and data and graphical tools used routinely alongside statistical metrics, are advocated for more defensible model evaluation \parencite{Bruhn2025}. Reliable soil hydraulic data---especially for the layer in which the drain is located or the deepest layers---is emphasised as a key calibration input, and calibration is advised to account for parameters that act mainly through interaction, with parameters of negligible total effect fixed at nominal values \parencite{Wang2006}. Independent calibration of particular parameters against observed drainage discharge is recommended so that interception and storage terms are not conflated with hydraulic conductivity and drainable porosity \parencite{Henine2022}. In empirical drainage-recession settings, careful calibration of the most sensitive parameters is urged to reduce prediction error, and drainage design variables such as depth and spacing are to be treated cautiously according to field setting \parencite{Timalsina2023}. Care is likewise urged where pedotransfer estimates substitute for measured properties, with Rosetta-estimated saturated hydraulic conductivity to be used cautiously when no alternative exists, and further calibration recommended so that both water-table depth and drainage discharge are accurately simulated \parencite{Malota2022}.

Automated and global approaches to calibration and sensitivity analysis are widely advocated. Automatic calibration methods are called for to support robust parameter sensitivity analysis, with ensemble Kalman filtering identified as a promising option for parameter identification \parencite{Warsta2014}, and autocalibration of parameters is recommended alongside algorithms to accelerate solution of the Richards equation \parencite{Sadhukhan2019b}. Automatic calibration is noted as an increasingly suggested practice generally \parencite{Singh2019b}. Global sensitivity analysis is proposed to capture parameter interactions not resolved by local one-at-a-time methods such as LH-OAT \parencite{Timalsina2023}, sensitivity analysis is recommended to understand parameter influence on model performance in depth \parencite{Chelil2022}, and model sensitivity to all parameters is urged to be analysed under several flow conditions \parencite{Kacimov2000}. For revised SWAT, further studies are needed to establish suitable lower and upper bounds of the sensitivity adjustment factor, with additional work underway on winter and growing-season water and nitrogen budgets \parencite{Moriasi2013b}. At catchment scale, spatially distributed streambed conductance is proposed for reach-level calibration, and groundwater--surface-water exchange fluxes are suggested as additional calibration targets to constrain models and as a possible proxy for the drainage contribution to streamflow \parencite{Karan2021}.

Explicit treatment of uncertainty is a distinct frontier. Consideration of uncertainty arising from parameter variability and measurement error is recommended \parencite{Alzraiee2013b}, and the role of input-data quality---including both parameterisation and driving weather data---on model performance and output uncertainty is highlighted as an area for further study \parencite{Upadhyay2019}, with uncertainty prediction flagged for future assessment in nitrogen-loss modelling \parencite{Tao2021}. For index-based predictions, a large dataset is considered necessary to improve reliability \parencite{Beauchemin2003}.

\subsection{Model Structure, Coupling, and Learning Approaches}

Beyond recalibration, authors identify structural refinements and integrative strategies as necessary for progress. Coupling complementary models is a common proposal: linking a biogeochemical model to spatial hydrological models such as SWAT or MIKE SHE is suggested to enhance regional-scale prediction \parencite{Li2006}, coupling nitrate-transport and hydrological drainage models is planned, together with assimilation of spring-fertilisation information via variational data assimilation \parencite{Chelil2022}, and the integrated use of more than one model or approach is judged more practical than single-model use \parencite{Singh2019b}. As spatial scales are refined, more fine-scale data are needed to calibrate and validate increasingly complex models, and advanced computer-learning and data-assimilation methods are advocated to exploit the growing volume of information, along with refinement of parameter estimates to smaller scales \parencite{Apostel2021}.

Machine-learning models raise their own validation needs: tuning hyperparameters and incorporating more diverse datasets---potentially via systematic grid or random search---is proposed to improve drain-pipe detection \parencite{Song2021}, and artificial neural networks are noted to require training data that include extreme situations, being readily retrained as such data accrue \parencite{Yang2000}. Several structural deficiencies are named directly. Site characterisation must be detailed enough to simulate physical processes and truly understand the system; regression limitations may be overcome by adding independent variables and non-linear dependences; and submodels for nitrogen mineralisation, crop nitrogen uptake, and plant growth under nitrogen limitation should be examined, improved, and freed of conceptual deficiencies \parencite{Ahuja2007}. More physically based improvements to soil-nitrogen algorithms are similarly warranted \parencite{Ikenberry2017}, and additional model complexity is identified as the price of improving predicted timing of drainage during snowmelt and soil thaw \parencite{Feyereisen2007}.

Refinements to the physical and analytical basis of drainage models are also proposed. Numerical models incorporating aquifer homogeneity, anisotropy, and varying bottom slopes are envisioned, with existing analytical solutions serving to verify them \parencite{Lin2025}; conversely, results from simple models are recommended for cross-checking against more general models and vice versa, collating several analytical approaches, running numerical codes such as MODFLOW, and verifying in the field \parencite{Kacimov2000}. Reanalysis using the Laplace rather than the Boussinesq equation, and extension of in-situ validation to slow-response systems, are proposed \parencite{Bouarfa2000}, while comparison of semi-analytical, fully numerical, and extended analytical solutions is reported as ongoing \parencite{Wiskow2003}. Recession-based equations are proposed for extension to spatially varying soil properties by replacing constant hydraulic conductivity and drainable porosity with functions of distance from the drain, with the effect of slope requiring further work \parencite{Cooke2001}. A three-dimensional flow model is suggested as potentially more suitable at a heterogeneous field site, with independent mathematical treatment and more controlled laboratory and field experiments warranted to make similar-media scaling approaches more robust \parencite{Mohanty1997}. Solute mixing is proposed to be represented with distinct mixing-layer depths and partition coefficients for mixing and outflow stages, the exact nature of the process remaining to be investigated \parencite{Shalit1996}. Additional targeted refinements include reconsidering the assumption of constant deep percolation and the treatment of shallow water tables persisting within 0.3~m of the surface \parencite{Kolars2019}, further study of spacing and depth through experiment and steady-state analytic--numerical simulation \parencite{Saptomo2021}, and concurrent comparison of outflow and water-table recessions observed at a single site \parencite{VANDELEUR1959}. Strengthening index-system construction and the evaluation of calculation reliability, supported by more samples and additional indicators such as soil thickness, grain size, composition, density, effective porosity, and permeability, is urged to enable deeper mechanistic development \parencite{Hou2018}, and building a dedicated simulation model to extend experimental findings is likewise planned \parencite{Tao2019b}.

\subsection{Instrumentation and Measurement-Method Validation}

Because model validation is only as sound as the data feeding it, a substantial share of the frontier concerns the instruments and equations used to measure drainage flows and concentrations. In-house calibration of flow-measurement structures is recommended repeatedly: weir calibration is advised for individual applications wherever possible \parencite{Christianson2019}, in-house calibrations representing field flow conditions are urged for accurate subsurface-discharge estimation---with an established equation recommended for one control-structure size and in-house calibration for others \parencite{Katuwal2024}---and individual calibration is likewise deemed necessary for weirs generally \parencite{Napierala2023}. Development of a stage--discharge equation before drainage monitoring is recommended for cylindrical control structures, with an ultrasonic sensor under investigation for compatibility with commercial structures \parencite{BinShokrana2021}. Post-hoc sensor calibration against collected samples, and grab-sample lab verification to provide test-specific calibration of bromide sensors during tracer tests, are recommended to improve logged-value accuracy \parencite{deOliveira2023b}.

Emerging measurement technologies carry explicit validation requirements before field deployment. A computer-vision flow-measurement approach requires more data to verify robustness in real-world environments and testing under more variable conditions, with model modifications---such as incorporating the Manning--Strickler equation to compensate for uneven horizontal initial velocities---and attention to data storage, transmission, and reduction for long-term deployment \parencite{Young2023}. For high-precision topographic survey under less-than-ideal real-time-kinematic conditions, hybrid RTK--rotating-laser systems or interpolated total-station surfaces are offered as alternatives \parencite{Freeland2014}. Sensor and device design is also flagged for refinement, including adaptation of a basic design for other target ranges and improvement of the coefficient of variation for mass-produced units \parencite{Enamorado2007}, and examination of the humidity-stability effect on sensible-heat-flux estimates from large-aperture scintillometry \parencite{Zhang2010}. Hydraulic-loss measurement remains incomplete: further work is needed on head loss in corrugated-pipe junctions and manholes under free-surface flow, with the possibility of discharge-specific head-loss coefficient relationships noted \parencite{Bayat2011}; on friction energy loss, flow velocities, detailed free-surface elevations, and transitional cavity-flow conditions \parencite{Nédélec2008}; on the influence of declining-bend slope on air discharge across several distortion heights \parencite{Gurklys2008}; and on modifying existing formulae to apply an appropriate Manning coefficient for design \parencite{Kouchakzadeh2011}. Strain-gauge experiments are identified as necessary to generate input data characterising the non-linear behaviour of single-wall corrugated HDPE pipe \parencite{Gaj2021}.

\subsection{Design Optimisation, Feasibility, and Implementation}

A final strand of the frontier extends validation from hydrological and biogeochemical performance to design optimisation, economic feasibility, and the institutional context of implementation. Optimal, least-cost design methodologies are sought that allow all alternative designs to be compared \parencite{Bakour2021}, and more optimal design approaches---such as two-column phosphorus-sorption-media configurations that reduce the required media, potentially supported by governmental conservation incentives---are proposed for edge-of-field treatment systems \parencite{Hauda2020}. Models are also envisioned as tools for exploring amended drain layouts and altered irrigation schedules \parencite{Alzraiee2013b}. Feasibility studies frame several technologies as unproven at field scale: numerous technical and economic problems---system hydraulics, operating procedures, regenerant-waste disposal, and the physical design and cost of a field-scale unit---must be solved before ion-exchange nitrate removal can be implemented \parencite{MAGETTE1990}, and socio-economic aspects and construction adequacy must be analysed alongside engineering considerations for drainage structures \parencite{Bozic2014}. At the institutional level, financiers and governments are urged to recognise capacity building as a complex, lengthy process warranting long-term investment, with a knowledge-creation framework offered to guide strategy \parencite{Ritzema2008}.

Several statistical and monitoring-design refinements round out the agenda for field validation. For regional soil-property prediction, weighting prediction times through exponential or logarithmic transformation, more objective assembly of model libraries, bagging of hillclimb datasets, and---at farm and field scale---proximal sensors such as ground-penetrating radar and electromagnetic induction are proposed for future study \parencite{Moller2018}. Analyses of within-field variation are advanced through additional lag parameters to capture response times exceeding one measurement interval, random monitoring-well effects where multiple wells lie at similar distances from drains, comparison of nearby fields under different treatments, and alternative physically based numerical models such as SWAP, HYDRUS, SUTRA, or MODFLOW \parencite{vandeCraats2025}. Finally, in-situ field validation planned or achieved but not yet fully reported \parencite{Hartani2001}, and follow-up work on solute transport during preferential flow and on spatial-variability treatment via similar-media scaling \parencite{Mohanty1997}, signal that much of the empirical testing the field regards as essential remains to be completed and published. Coupled with agreement that large-scale hydrologic applications and the simulation of additional, un-tested drain spacings are natural next steps \parencite{Talukolaee2022}, these open threads define a validation programme that is broad in scale, long in duration, and rich in the process measurements that current datasets lack.

9.body:
\subsection{Overview}
Integrating controlled drainage (CD) with irrigation water management is repeatedly framed less as a settled practice than as a research frontier: the recurring call is to move away from passive, one-way ``unilateral'' subsurface drainage toward systems that give farmers active, location-specific control over the water table and over the coupled irrigation--drainage water balance \parencite{Ayars2006b,Kesicka2023b,Ritzema2023}. Across arid, semiarid, humid, and irrigated command-area settings, authors converge on a small set of open problems: (i) developing design criteria, structures, and decision-support tools for \emph{active} rather than passive management \parencite{Ayars2006b,Youssef2023,Saadat2017}; (ii) automating and data-driving water-balance control through sensors and models \parencite{Youssef2023,Ikonen2023,Isomäki2024}; (iii) coordinating irrigation with drainage adjustments, including subirrigation embedded in regional water systems \parencite{Wahba2005,deWit2022,deWit2024}; (iv) sustaining drainage-water reuse and salinity control while protecting downstream water quality \parencite{WESTCOT1988,Sharma1998,Ayars2015}; (v) adapting design to changing crop water needs, climate extremes, and irrigation modernization \parencite{Rui2024,Ritzema2007,Ziaeifar2025}; and (vi) resolving the institutional, economic, and participatory barriers that condition adoption \parencite{Ritzema2011,Castellano2019,Qureshi2008}. A cross-cutting message is that many recommendations remain qualitative and site-specific, and that long-term, multi-scale evidence is still lacking. Kesicka \textcite{Kesicka2023b} frames replacing unilateral drainage with methods of controlling drainage outflows as a current global challenge, and continued research is broadly expected to keep refining drainage techniques to optimize agricultural water use.

\subsection{From passive to active, adaptive management}
A foundational research direction is the transition from ``no or passive management to active management with defined water management objectives'' \parencite{Ayars2006b}. \textcite{Ayars2006b} argue that structures for controlling water-table position should be \emph{required} in new drainage design and considered for retrofitting existing systems where practical, and that there is a pressing need to develop new design criteria and management methods for controlled drainage to support sustainable irrigated agriculture---a claim they note requires long-term field studies, possibly combined with modeling, for validation. Similar reasoning appears in system-design recommendations to include structures for controlling groundwater-table (GWT) position in new subsurface drainage designs \parencite{Dukhovny2018}, to incorporate ``more options for control'' so farmers can optimize integrated water management \parencite{Ritzema2023}, and to expand ``changeable controlled drainage networks'' across arable lands \parencite{Mahmoud2021}. \textcite{Strock2011} anticipate that producers may eventually view controlled and reduced drainage as soil- and water-management \emph{tools} rather than mitigation practices, and argue that fine-tuning drainage intensity should be a priority in designing all new systems.

The quantitative payoff of active management remains an open question. \textcite{Watkins2024} call for research on the crop-yield benefits achievable by actively managing the subsurface CD outlet during extremely wet periods---hypothesizing that active management could minimize wet-stress on roots and raise yields---and separately for study of how shallow drainage combined with CD affects drainage and nutrient loading and downstream water quality. \textcite{Saadat2017} highlight the need to examine the cost--benefit trade-off of active management, and \textcite{Helmers2012} suggest that yield reductions observed under CD could likely be mitigated with greater management of the system, while cautioning that installation cost, producer management burden, and the acreage of applicability must be weighed before broad implementation. Seasonally differentiated control is another explicit hypothesis: \textcite{Asgari2021} propose free drainage for quick waterlogging control in normal and wet seasons, CD in dry seasons to alleviate deficit stress, and possibly supplemental surface ditches and supplemental irrigation to manage the extremes.

\subsection{Sensors, automation, and decision-support systems}
Several groups identify automation and data-driven control as the next technological step. \textcite{Youssef2023} call for a new generation of drainage control structures and decision-support tools for active management of CD during the growing season, for sensor-based, data-driven water-management systems on poorly drained soils with frequently shallow water tables, and more broadly for technologically advanced systems that adapt to changing weather patterns. \textcite{Ayars2015} likewise identify decision-support systems for adaptive control of integrated irrigation and drainage systems as a need. In Finland, \textcite{Ikonen2023} lay out a concrete research agenda: improved dynamic modeling and data assimilation of the field water balance, a feasible water-balance control-problem formulation (cost function and constraints), fusing control automation with the physical drainage control-well infrastructure, and gaining practical experience from end-users and experimental peatland agriculture. \textcite{Isomäki2024} argue for data-driven water-management systems and appropriate control schedules for combined controlled subsurface drainage and open-ditch damming, noting that proactive management of control structures according to meteorological conditions can increase the probability of keeping water-table depth in the optimal range. Automatic water-depth control is also proposed for aerobic rice, where \textcite{Arif2021} introduce a conceptual sheet-pipe automatic water-depth control system that still needs field testing, alongside an opportunity to examine the ET$_c$--production relationship for aerobic rice. Earlier work anticipated these directions, describing farm decision aids and management modeling systems---slowly being developed---that would incorporate water quantity and quality, crop growth, management alternatives, and economic evaluation \parencite{THOMAS1995}.

\subsection{Integrating irrigation with drainage and subirrigation}
Coordinating irrigation adjustments with drainage control is a persistent open problem. \textcite{Wahba2005} stress that when CD is implemented, appropriate reductions in irrigation application must follow, requiring coordination and training among irrigation authorities, drainage authorities, and farmers. A recurring recommendation is to improve integration between irrigation and drainage networks and to use CD to help farmers optimize on-farm water management \parencite{Ritzema2011,Ritzema2016}. \textcite{Dukhovny2018} argue that controlled drainage (CTD) requires a genuinely integrated irrigation-and-drainage water-management approach, with careful site selection (favoring homogeneous soil texture with little sand or sand--gravel near the control structure) and careful monitoring of all water-balance components.

Subirrigation embedded in regional water systems is a distinct and largely unresolved frontier. \textcite{deWit2022} argue that regional-scale water needs for subirrigation should be known before responsible incorporation into regional water management, framing correct implementation of CD with subirrigation as an integral, catchment-wide question; they call for spatial and temporal insight into water availability at each location, optimized pumping strategies to minimize water use, and cautious use of alternative resources such as industrial or treated wastewater only where quality is sufficient and side effects are limited. The companion modeling study \parencite{deWit2024} shows how the SWAP model could identify suitable field sites and forecast the hydrological consequences of subirrigation before implementation, explore effects across crops and crop characteristics, and examine drainage design, water-supply design, soil type, resistance to downward seepage, and surface-water management under varying meteorology; it also suggests optimizing water supply by starting later, supplying water only when crop transpiration is potentially reduced, and coordinating surface-water levels to avoid drainage losses to ditches. Longer-term studies are needed to disentangle whether soil-property changes under drainage-subirrigation arise from the practice itself or from interactions with irrigation water quality, and whether drip or sprinkler methods interact with soil properties \parencite{Kaur2023}. Related integrative directions include continuing to combine water-table control (via CD and subirrigation) with agrochemical applications to minimize nutrient and pesticide losses \parencite{BENGTSON1995}, and pairing shallow drainage with wider perennial-crop strips along drainageways \parencite{Schilling2007}.

\subsection{Design criteria for changing crops, climate, and irrigation modernization}
A broad theme is that design criteria must be revisited for evolving conditions. \textcite{Rui2024} argue that future drainage design should account for changing crop water needs, evolving weather, and recent engineering advances, suggesting more efficient---ideally controllable---systems to improve long-term productivity of poorly drained soils. Design criteria should be revisited to meet environmental concerns \parencite{Akram2013}, and drainage design and water management should be optimized in the face of climate change through adaptive strategies such as adjusting planting schedules, improving water productivity, and enhancing drainage to mitigate waterlogging \parencite{Ziaeifar2025}. \textcite{Ritzema2023} recommend explicitly incorporating the impact of irrigation modernization into drainage design to account for changing soil-water and salinity regimes. Ageing systems, new crop varieties, crop diversification, water scarcity, deteriorating water quality, and wet/dry climate extremes are all cited as drivers of continued drainage research \parencite{Ritzema2007}, and changes in drainage-water quality are expected to require changes in drain-system design and installation \parencite{Ritzema2006}. Further research is needed to serve emerging and least-developed countries and to cope with climate, land-use change, and drainage-water quantity/quality requirements \parencite{Ritzema2011}.

Design intensity itself is an open research question. \textcite{Dialameh2025} note that where adjusting drain spacing is infeasible, other water-management practices may be needed to mitigate increased drought severity, and that future design decisions may need to account for management and economic factors beyond those modeled. \textcite{Singh2009} caution that reduced drain spacing would require increased capacity across the entire drainage network---a factor for future network design---and would heighten the emphasis on best management practices to reduce nutrient loss. \textcite{Castellano2019} advocate a systems approach integrating drainage, crop--soil processes, and nutrient-loss-reduction practices to design drained cropping systems that mitigate and adapt to climate change, and call for identifying long-term cost savings (e.g., greater fertilizer nitrogen-use efficiency) that could offset the higher installation costs of altered drain depth and spacing. \textcite{Strock2011} recommend detailed analysis with DRAINMOD or another drainage model to develop optimum drainage rates and drain spacing across U.S. locations and soils. Fundamental design questions persist about which soil parameters are most critical and what confidence intervals are required for each \parencite{Ayars2015}, and about proper design guidelines for specific geologic conditions such as the Mid-Atlantic states \parencite{SHIRMOHAMMADI1995}. The optimum design is repeatedly framed as a cost--benefit balance \parencite{HARDJOAMIDJOJO1982}, and irrigation is emphasized as necessary to reduce deficit soil-water conditions where drainage alone cannot raise yields \parencite{HARDJOAMIDJOJO1982}.

\subsection{Drainage-water reuse, salinity, and water-quality frontiers}
Managing salt and reusing drainage water remain heavily research-dependent. \textcite{Jia2007} frame understanding salt-and-water-balance dynamics as the first step toward better management of irrigated agriculture, providing evidence to target and fairly evaluate field water-management practices and improve regional water-use efficiency. \textcite{Ayars2015} enumerate needs to develop management approaches for subsurface drainage in arid areas, to reduce crop salt-tolerance and groundwater-management concepts to a manageable producer system, to quantify drainage-water-treatment efficiency (loading, flow rates, lifespan, maintenance, design), to refine salt-management strategies for integrated systems, and to develop and evaluate alternatives for managing, remediating, and removing nitrogen from drainage water.

Reuse-focused research spans agronomy, scheduling, and safety. \textcite{WESTCOT1988} list needs for site-specific load--flow relationships for trace elements, expanded knowledge of germination and early-seedling salinity tolerance, ratings of crops' use of saline groundwater, improved irrigation scheduling that lets farmers meet crop ET from a portion of the high water table while minimizing upward salt flow, wider commercial applicability of direct drainage-water reuse, better understanding of bioaccumulation and trace-element immobilization in evaporation basins, and caution in reusing salts until safety is better known. Field guidance likewise remains provisional: substituting drainage water up to 9~dS/m for wheat at post-germination stages where non-saline canal water is scarce, then reverting to non-saline canal water for one or two years after 6--7 years of drainage-water use, with careful case-by-case evaluation \parencite{Sharma1998}. Keeping drainage effluent as low as possible by improving irrigation efficiency and blending fresh and drainage water, plus disposal options such as farm ponds for storing and evaporating drainage water, are proposed \parencite{Datta2002}, as is minimizing effluent by improving irrigation efficiency and farm-level reuse while maintaining soil health \parencite{Qureshi2008}. Autumn or winter irrigation combined with subsurface pipe drainage is suggested for leaching excess soil salt \parencite{Dou2022}, seasonal groundwater undulation is deemed necessary to prevent salt accumulation \parencite{Mirlas2021}, and proper irrigation schemes---shifting from traditional to drip irrigation---are recommended to sustain growth while flushing salts \parencite{Mirlas2021}. Root-zone salinity and the water table should be kept low, at least in early growth stages \parencite{Singh2019}. Non-conventional treatment via engineered in-stream and off-stream wetlands is being explored at pilot scales \parencite{AbdelDayem2007}, and drainage water outflow is argued to require treatment before discharge to surface or groundwater \parencite{Filipovic2015}. Emerging soil-remediation directions include exploring acidic biochar in saline-alkaline soils and its synergy with subsurface drainage, plus plant-remediation desalination and other collaborative measures \parencite{Liu2025f}. Reclamation innovations include harvesting subsurface-drainage water in dryland/semiarid sites for livestock, windbreaks, gardens, or salt leaching, and establishing alfalfa in saline-seep areas fitted with engineered drainage wells \parencite{Steppuhn2017}. Progressive-flooding and progressive-leaching methodologies still require experimental validation and extension to finer-textured soils \parencite{Youngs2000}. Salinity control is argued to belong within a basin-wide salt-balance context \parencite{Qureshi2008}, and comprehensive assessment of waterlogging and salinity together (rather than separately) is recommended to yield more reliable findings \parencite{Valipour2014}.

\subsection{Nutrient, pesticide, and water-quality research needs}
Water-quality outcomes of controlled and water-table-management systems remain incompletely quantified. \textcite{FAUSEY1995} identify a need to evaluate the full potential of CD and water-table management for water quality, spanning short- and long-term effects on the fate and transport of agricultural chemicals, soil properties, productivity and trafficability, surface/subsurface water and sediment movement, root-zone biological dynamics, and plant development across crops; they also flag CD for animal-waste disposal on cropland, winter denitrification of residual soil nitrogen via CD, interfacing drainage with constructed or enhanced wetlands, and---as a high priority---a comprehensive water-table-management model incorporating solute transport, with results folded into practical management guides. \textcite{RITTER1995} add needs to quantify preferential flow in drainage-water pollution, develop field- and watershed-scale pollution-control modeling, determine year-round nitrate and pesticide leaching, gain more field experience with subirrigation and CD, document tillage effects on water quality, interface agrichemicals with livestock manures where tile drainage is used, and investigate cover crops for water-quality management. Continued pesticide degradation/movement studies and improved simulation of chemical losses under high water tables are being pursued \parencite{THOMAS1995}, and monitoring of drainage impacts on nutrient and pesticide transport---treating drainage as part of whole-farm or watershed management---is recommended \parencite{SHIRMOHAMMADI1995}. At watershed scale, controlling or slowing surface runoff and tile flow, with drainage-water-management devices and denitrification bioreactors at tile outlets, is proposed to cut nitrate loads \parencite{Li2015}, and strategies addressing both hydrology and nitrogen supply, focused at the drainage-district scale, are argued necessary for meaningful downstream benefits \parencite{Ikenberry2014}. Optimizing irrigation is crucial to minimize nitrogen leaching (e.g., from turfgrass) \parencite{Filipovic2015}, and coupling CD with conservation practices that reduce surface runoff is recommended \parencite{BinShokrana2023}. Reduced tillage should be combined with efficient subsurface drainage to minimize surface runoff \parencite{Koskiaho2002}, and CD is offered as one way to meet both crop-production and water-quality goals \parencite{Kladivko2005}. Nutrient replenishment under the drainage--irrigation--cropping system---including zinc fertilization---is needed for optimum yields \parencite{Gul2013}, and understanding nutrient-loss mechanisms during waterlogging and the benefits of nutrient application is an open question \parencite{Manik2019}.

\subsection{Modeling, monitoring, and long-term evaluation}
Authors repeatedly call for stronger modeling, longer records, and better monitoring. Long-term studies are broadly needed to validate active-management approaches \parencite{Ayars2006b}, and the long-term effects of CD---especially in arid and semiarid regions---remain understudied \parencite{Wang2020c}. \textcite{Wang2020c} further urge defining soils by internationally accepted standards (or fuller descriptions), investigating CD from a climate standpoint, combining future designs with proposed underlying mechanisms for more reliable evaluation, and classifying situations to understand CD's contribution to total nitrogen in drainage water. Watershed-scale water-table-management research is needed to develop best management practices and select the best water-management scenario, and more flexible CD schemes may alter impacts on tile drainage, surface runoff, and shallow-water-table days \parencite{Golmohammadi2016}. \textcite{Kim2023} present the SWAT-tile model as opening opportunities for water-quality research and tile-depth installation, and as a viable strategy for managing flow and nitrate transport at field and watershed scales. Steady/transient simulations of steady recharge are framed as an initial step toward broader analyses that could propose conceptual modifications to traditional design for water-quality control \parencite{GRISMER1993}. Regional modeling calls include extending simulation periods to capture dynamic equilibrium and long-term sustainability, completing calibration of groundwater flow-and-transport models, and refining field data on irrigation efficiencies, canal seepage, drainage, and return flows \parencite{Triana2010}. Environmental monitoring and decision-support systems should be designed at project onset to work hand-in-hand with drainage operations, built bottom-up and documented progressively \parencite{Quinn2009}. Additional model-supported directions include using a different field-scale hydrologic model to evaluate CD's yield effect \parencite{BinShokrana2023} and exploring interactive tools such as DRAINFRAME \parencite{Gopalakrishnan2007}. Remote sensing to detect drainage and salinity needs---via drones and satellite imagery \parencite{Yannopoulos2020}, and refined, upscaled remote-sensing assessment of waterlogged/saline areas \parencite{Gopalakrishnan2007}---is another data frontier. Reviewing prior research and projects is urged to avoid trial-and-error and reduce failure risk in future rehabilitation/construction \parencite{Valipour2014}.

\subsection{Crop, soil, and agronomic research directions}
Refining the biophysical targets of control is an active frontier. The required water-table depth must be refined by crop, soil type, and season, and it must be verified that shallow drains can control root-zone salinity over the long term before farmer implementation \parencite{Christen2001b}. Root-development responses to tile drainage---and consequent effects on plant water uptake during drought---need further study \parencite{Nash2015b}. Further work is needed on drainage and desalination in unsaturated soil and on the design/engineering of tile drainage under drip irrigation \parencite{Chen2021}. Winter crops that leave soils bare during hot summers warrant more investigation, and longer research is needed on CD in sugarcane \parencite{Akram2013}. On soils with high perched water tables, progress depends on understanding factors limiting crop growth and developing improved strategies, potentially including mole drainage or increasing drainable porosity by loosening soil above the drain \parencite{Muirhead1996}; strategies to reduce flow to shallow subsurface drains and more sustainable practices are also needed \parencite{Muirhead1996}. Soil-management options such as subsoiling, deep-rooted cropping, and drainage trenches are viable short-term solutions \parencite{Guedessou2021}. \textcite{Manik2019} outline a waterlogging-tolerance agenda: increasing soil-profile de-watering through soil-improvement and agronomic strategies, greater specificity of management--environment and management--management interactions, discovering new non-oxygen-associated QTLs, testing their effectiveness alone and combined under multiple constraints, evaluating agronomic/yield/quality effects, and developing management packages for varieties with diverse tolerance genes---plus cost/benefit comparison of different drainage strategies. Salt-tolerant germplasm, groundwater-abstraction regulation, and crop diversification (e.g., salt-tolerant medicinal and aromatic species) are recommended \parencite{Qureshi2008}, as are salt-tolerant crops such as eucalyptus or phreatophytes and drainage installation where leaching with poor-quality water is infeasible \parencite{Sarwar2001}. Supplementary measures---gypsum application, salt-resistant varieties, and irrigation-efficiency improvement---are needed to enhance subsurface-drainage benefits \parencite{Ritzema2008b}. Pressurized irrigation and reduced irrigation amounts are recommended to lessen waterlogging and drainage needs \parencite{Khorramian2022}, more research is needed on leaching requirements for modern irrigation in heavy clay soil \parencite{Mahmoud2021}, and alternatives such as surface irrigation and improved fertility management alongside proper drainage should be weighed for claypan soils \parencite{MOSTAGHIMI1989}.

\subsection{Economics, institutions, and adoption}
A large share of the frontier is socio-economic and institutional. Economic analysis is repeatedly flagged as future work \parencite{Kladivko2005,Saadat2017,Manik2019}, and installation cost, producer management burden, and applicable acreage must be assessed before broad implementation \parencite{Helmers2012}. A broader economic analysis is urged to reveal opportunities for improving water management, reducing drainage costs, and enhancing land/water quality, including the opportunity cost of drainage-investment funds, the scope to shrink drainage facilities through better water management, and pricing/allocation policies that motivate improvement \parencite{Wichelns2002}. The overall economic component of water-quality-management systems needs to be determined and incorporated into design \parencite{THOMAS1995}, and identifying long-term cost savings to offset higher installation costs, plus incentives and cost-share programs that couple drainage upgrades with edge-of-field nitrate-loss reduction, is recommended \parencite{Castellano2019}. Cost reduction is itself a research target: local manufacture of PVC pipes and synthetic envelopes and further labor-cost reductions \parencite{Datta2002}; and new low-cost drainage techniques, cheaper installation technologies, new drainage materials (pipes, envelopes), and national standards \parencite{Yannopoulos2020}.

Institutional and participatory needs are pervasive. Recommendations include shifting from standardized to flexible, location-specific solutions reflecting stakeholder preferences \parencite{Ritzema2011,Ritzema2016,Ritzema2023}; balancing top-down against bottom-up through participatory management across planning, design, installation, operation, and maintenance \parencite{Ritzema2016}; improving farmer participation throughout the drainage-infrastructure lifecycle \parencite{Ritzema2023}; and better identifying stakeholders' needs, preferences, and willingness to contribute while linking their tacit knowledge with researchers' explicit knowledge \parencite{Ritzema2011}. Capacity development---integrating research, education, and advisory services---is emphasized \parencite{Ritzema2016}, along with the substantial education and policy change needed to lower adoption barriers \parencite{Castellano2019}, public/institutional education programs on drainage and water-table management \parencite{SHIRMOHAMMADI1995}, and addressing negative perceptions that conflate agricultural drainage with wetland drainage before the application base can expand \parencite{THOMAS1995}. Scientists and policy institutions should help farmers adopt innovations through financial support and knowledge exchange \parencite{Kesicka2023b}. Governance recommendations include developing procedural/organizational frameworks and national or regional support groups, working groups for diverse agro-hydro-climatic regions, and multi-departmental coordination committees in command areas \parencite{Singh2019c}; enhanced inter-agency coordination for water allocation \parencite{Qureshi2008}; a dedicated state-level Drainage Division and national drainage institution, standardization of components, and promotion of private-sector reclamation and local SSD installation \parencite{Gopalakrishnan2007}; drainage policy papers with time-bound reclamation action plans \parencite{Ritzema2008b,Valipour2014}; and appropriate institutional arrangements for farmers' participation \parencite{Ritzema2008b}. Adaptive regulatory frameworks that promote local irrigation/drainage flexibility and grower innovation, communicate successes, recognize failures early, and disseminate information are recommended \parencite{Quinn2009}. Programs to create awareness and promote subsidies and micro-finance \parencite{Satyanarayana2007}, to convince farmers to clear drainage blockages and maintain systems \parencite{Manjunatha2004}, and to ensure participatory operation and maintenance for sustainability \parencite{Gopalakrishnan2007} round out the adoption agenda. Continued technical and financial support is deemed essential to sustain irrigated agriculture \parencite{Qureshi2008}, research in irrigated agriculture is ``not yet over'' \parencite{Ritzema2007}, and a participatory approach is demanded by changing socio-economic conditions \parencite{Ritzema2007}. Practical framing choices---outsourcing/privatizing drainage activities \parencite{AbdelDayem2007}, budgeting for certified local consultants to support decentralized design \parencite{Ritzema2023}, and switching from gravity to pumped drainage to enable reuse and shrink open-drain dimensions \parencite{Ritzema2023}---are also proposed. Large-scale drainage investment tied to adjusted irrigation planning is viewed as unavoidable in the long run \parencite{Sarwar2001}, and accumulated reclamation experience is expected to inform future developments worldwide \parencite{TeKrony2004}.

\subsection{Implementation, maintenance, and scaling}
Finally, several frontier items concern getting systems built, maintained, and scaled. CD could be propagated on a large scale in irrigation command areas \parencite{Karegoudar2019}, and adopting CD, drain-water reuse, and standardized components is called for \parencite{Gopalakrishnan2007}. For large-scale implementation, trencher and trenchless drainage machines may be considered \parencite{Ritzema2008b}. Later-stage management and maintenance are highlighted as important, with sludge removal from drainage pipes identified as the chief issue in later utilization \parencite{Yan2013b}; upgrading and carefully maintaining existing tile drainage---and exercising more care in construction and maintenance---is recommended for better efficiency \parencite{AbdElMageed2009}. Efficiency-oriented site actions include better canal maintenance, gate operations, and application techniques, converting gravity delivery to closed (piped) delivery, widely implementing drip and sprinkler networks, preventing erosion/sedimentation in storage structures, ensuring good subsurface-drainage management against salinity and waterlogging, and avoiding deep-groundwater use to prevent saltwater intrusion \parencite{Kanber2019}. Modeling procedures can pre-screen appropriate field sites and forecast hydrological consequences before implementing subirrigation \parencite{deWit2024}. Sustainable effluent disposal must ultimately be resolved \parencite{Datta2002}, low-cost and easily understood CD options should be pursued within an integrated water-saving approach \parencite{Wahba2005}, and profitable, feasible water-management alternatives for problem soils should be selected on a site-specific basis \parencite{MOSTAGHIMI1989,Sharma1998}.

11.body:
\subsection{Overview}

Watershed modeling occupies a pivotal but unfinished position in the science of drainage water management: it is the principal tool for translating field-scale process understanding into landscape-scale predictions of nutrient and sediment fate, yet the models themselves, the data that constrain them, and the scales at which they are applied remain the subject of active methodological debate. The recurring call across this literature is to \emph{refine, build, calibrate, and validate} watershed hydrological models used to assess how drainage water management affects the nitrogen and phosphorus cycles \parencite{Sunohara2015}, and to pair such modeling with large-scale efforts that prioritize where drainage water management will yield the greatest benefit \parencite{Ross2016}. Several intertwined research lines emerge from the findings. First, models require improved representation of processes that are currently poorly resolved---phosphorus sorption--desorption, preferential flow, in-stream processing, channel-network routing, and reactive transport---together with explicit treatment of uncertainty \parencite{Wang2020b,Kujawa2020}. Second, a persistent scale problem separates well-characterized fields from the watersheds where water-quality goals are set, motivating explicit field-to-watershed linkage and upscaling frameworks \parencite{Williams2015,Kyllmar2023}. Third, the empirical base for calibration and validation remains thin: long-term, year-round, coordinated multi-site monitoring is repeatedly identified as the binding constraint \parencite{Christianson2015,Bauwe2020}. Fourth, the field is called on to expand beyond its intensively tile-drained Midwestern core to less intensely drained, newly drained, and geographically underrepresented watersheds \parencite{Kujawa2020,Christianson2015}. Finally, modeling is increasingly asked to serve decision support---placement of stacked conservation practices, adaptive management, and prioritization of critical source areas---rather than description alone \parencite{Kröger2013,Tomer2015}. The subsections below organize these open questions by the modeling task they most directly challenge.

\subsection{Refining process representation in watershed models}

A large share of the frontier concerns processes that current watershed models represent crudely or omit. For phosphorus, \textcite{Wang2020b} set out an explicit development agenda: incorporate dynamic P sorption/desorption processes and new subroutines for direct P loss from manure, fertilizer, and dung; integrate pedotransfer functions for the P sorption coefficient and distinct P pools keyed to soil characteristics and weathering; include in-stream phosphorus processes and critical-source-area identification based on variable source areas; and, crucially, model preferential flow and connectivity between fields and adjacent water bodies. Preferential flow recurs as a first-order gap. \textcite{Djodjic2002} argue that preferential-flow losses of P in structured soils must be incorporated into phosphorus indices and decision-support systems, that identification of preferential pathways at the field scale would sharpen leaching-risk assessment, and that employing more than one hydrological model within a decision-support system may better describe water and pollutant transport. Consideration of preferential flow in the determination of critical source areas, especially at field or sub-field scale, is likewise flagged as necessary \parencite{Wang2020b}. The adequacy of our understanding of subsurface hydrologic pathways---and whether they can be accurately modeled at the watershed scale---remains an open question in its own right \parencite{Sims1998}.

Channel networks and in-stream processing are a second underdeveloped domain. \textcite{Giraud1997} emphasize that as watershed size increases, channel networks must be modeled as a whole set of interdependent reaches---whether looped or dendritic---and that modeling flow paths and travel time of water within the network is essential to predicting non-point-source pollution reaching coastal waters. At the watershed scale, more detailed methods of channel-network description and extrapolation may reduce uncertainty, and evaluation of historical land management and channel stability from aerial photography could further inform the process \parencite{Williamson2025}. Better representation of instream biogeochemical processes is proposed as a route to enhance the reductions generated by best management practices \parencite{Schilling2012b}, and the interaction between hillslopes and riparian areas needs stronger representation to predict low-flow events \parencite{Basu2010}.

Sediment dynamics require analogous refinement. Estimating gully and river-bed erosion will demand different approaches than upland modeling, and the contribution of gully areas to the overall sediment budget deserves closer scrutiny, while the use of WEPP for upland sediment flux needs verification against well-recorded observations \parencite{Maalim2013}. A more robust treatment of sediment from non-field sources should explicitly represent the mechanisms and physical processes behind streambank failure and downstream mobilization \parencite{Dalzell2018}. At the catchment scale, sediment budgets could underpin the design of control and management strategies for lowland agricultural catchments with extensive underdraining, and their precision should be carried forward explicitly into future budget refinement \parencite{Walling2002}.

\subsection{Uncertainty, model evaluation, and structural choices}

Beyond adding processes, the findings press for rigorous quantification of uncertainty and disciplined model evaluation. Assessments of model uncertainty should be built into P-transport modeling \parencite{Wang2020b}, and structural uncertainty deserves explicit treatment---for example by including water-quality models beyond SWAT when projecting hydrologic and nutrient change \parencite{Kujawa2020}. Standardized evaluation metrics recur as a methodological aspiration, with the coefficient of efficiency of Nash and Sutcliffe proposed for future watershed-model evaluations \parencite{STONE1995,SAVABI1995}. For the specific problem of drainage water management effectiveness, \textcite{Shedekar2021} argue that a probabilistic approach accounting for stochastic variation in effectiveness across time and scales may be more appropriate than a single static value or range, and that large datasets combining short- and long-term monitoring and modeling at regional scales may soon be needed to support large-scale assessments. Model transferability is itself a research question: whether a representative 10--20~km\textsuperscript{2} scale identified in one watershed applies to others in the region needs to be examined, and the validity of a given modeling approach must be established by comparison against hydrograph data from additional watersheds \parencite{Basu2010}. Newly proposed physically based tools warrant similar scrutiny---for instance, applying the Advanced Terrestrial Simulator (ATS) to larger watersheds with different soils and topography to probe scaling and physiographic controls, and extending it to explore land-use change, surface-drainage modification, future climate, and drainage intensification \parencite{Rathore2025}. Where field data are unavailable, calibrated and validated physically based models such as HYDRUS-2D are recommended for reliable flux estimation, provided calibration and validation datasets are made more comprehensive \parencite{Naderi2025}. Individualized, station-by-station analysis is also advised for regional trend work, since a single approach may not yield accurate results across heterogeneous stations \parencite{Kumar2009}.

\subsection{The field-to-watershed scaling problem}

A defining open problem is the disconnect between field-scale process knowledge and watershed-scale response. \textcite{Williams2015} identify quantifying the relationships between field-scale processes and watershed response as a critical need for developing water-quality strategies, and cast the field--watershed linkage as pivotal for tile-drained regions of the Midwest and beyond. Integrating field and watershed models for BMP calibration---and scaling field results up to larger areas---is an explicit modeling priority \parencite{Wang2020b}. Small, intensively monitored catchments are proposed as the empirical bridge: they can reveal system functioning, serve as a basis for upscaling to agricultural land where information is less detailed, and provide the data for calibrating and validating modeled outputs \parencite{Kyllmar2023}. Scaling questions also arise for individual practices. Effectiveness estimates for drainage water management should be scaled up from field to watershed \parencite{Wang2021}, and phosphorus mitigation likewise needs scale-up from field to watershed in future work \parencite{Wang2021}. Selenium-removal and other treatment findings are called to be extended to scaled-up operational flow-through systems \parencite{Gao2003}, and complex questions remain about the effectiveness of widespread practice implementation at watershed scales, compounded by lag times in water-quality response and low adoption rates in key watershed locations \parencite{Roley2016}. Continued large-scale watershed modeling is repeatedly recommended to prioritize the areas that will benefit most from drainage water management \parencite{Ross2016}.

\subsection{Data, monitoring, and long-term records to constrain models}

Nearly every modeling ambition is bottlenecked by data. \textcite{Christianson2015} call for more intensive year-round monitoring in some locations, improved monitoring of newly drained areas, ditch-drained areas, and surface intakes, more long-term studies with coordinated controls across multiple sites and years, and continued construction of region-specific drainage water-quality data for underrepresented regions---adding that understanding drainage-design impacts grows more important as aging infrastructure is replaced and upgraded. Long-term records are singled out as necessary to disentangle the possibly counteracting effects of land management and climate change on biogeochemical and hydrological processes, with the corollary that evaluating policy measures will demand patience \parencite{Bauwe2020}. Long-term, catchment-scale study is deemed essential to understand management and climate-change impacts \parencite{Kelley2017}, and the establishment and use of long-term studies tracking the effects of management changes on runoff phosphorus losses is a standing recommendation \parencite{Gillingham2000}. Follow-up investigations of implemented strategies are described as critical for adaptive management \parencite{Schilling2012b}, and long-term watershed data are advocated as the foundation for models that target high-pollution-potential areas and project watershed responses to BMP implementation \parencite{Lemke2011}.

The tracer and water-age literature adds specific data demands for constraining transport models. Further quantification of water ages in drained agricultural landscapes with high-frequency (daily to sub-daily) tracer measurements is needed, along with additional water and tracer datasets across fields with differing management, soils, and climates, and more complex models capable of representing both celerities and velocities \parencite{Williams2021}. Event sampling is flagged as an important next step to constrain the younger water fraction, and investigating the isotope dynamics of ``bulk'' soil water would more completely characterize water partitioning \parencite{DimitrovaPetrova2020}. Further quantification of water ages across drained landscapes would clarify the trade-offs between maintaining drains for food production and the exacerbation of flooding and contaminant transport \parencite{DimitrovaPetrova2020}. High-resolution specific-conductance data are expected to enable more end-member-mixing hydrograph separations as monitoring networks grow, with the caveat that method choice should reflect the flow and specific-conductance characteristics of the stream \parencite{Kronholm2015}. Improved national drainage-extent datasets open new analyses of drainage impacts and nutrient-control strategies, though further study of how farmers report ambiguous cases such as nonpatterned drainage would aid interpretation \parencite{Jame2022}. Coordinated, multidisciplinary, and international collaboration---including consistent reporting of basic performance parameters such as influent/effluent concentrations, hydraulic residence and loading rates, and watershed-to-treatment ratios---is urged to build the shared evidence base for constructed-wetland and treatment performance \parencite{Messer2021,Sims1998}.

\subsection{Extending to underrepresented and less intensely drained landscapes}

A clear frontier is geographic and physiographic breadth. Applications developed in intensely drained watersheds should be extended to watersheds that are \emph{not} intensely drained \parencite{Kujawa2020}. Regional trend and discharge analyses call for larger basin areas, longer study periods, and replication across multiple watersheds while controlling for weather variability, including the influence of small sub-field wetlands \parencite{Yang2017}. Drainage-flow analyses should be extended to larger drainage networks---for example the many US Midwest networks exceeding 29~ha---and linked to measured nitrogen loading across drainage scales and across additional states \parencite{Christianson2025}. Broader applicability is sought by explicitly addressing regional variability, external factors, and climate change \parencite{Naderi2025}, and additional studies across a broad range of climates and topographic features are recommended to strengthen emerging hydrologic paradigms for headwater streams \parencite{King2009}. The importance of physical-hydrologic dynamics as drivers of nutrient loss in dryland regions worldwide remains to be assessed \parencite{Kelley2017}. Regional hydro-climatic feedbacks---including the effects of biofuel expansion and of wetland drainage on regional hydrology and climate---are proposed as further avenues \parencite{Kumar2009}. Effects of drainage-related structures on wetland hydrology across a range of site and meteorological conditions require dedicated field data \parencite{Tangen2018}, and inland-basin remediation treatments are recommended for replication across affected areas \parencite{Kumar2006}. Theoretical drainage results are also candidates for generalization---for example to the case of deep impervious barriers and to water tables drained by channels or rivers \parencite{Zimmer2023}.

\subsection{Modeling to guide practice placement, stacking, and decision support}

Increasingly, watershed modeling is asked to inform \emph{where} and \emph{how} to deploy practices. New conservation-planning tools are needed to determine the best placement of practices in the landscape and to evaluate alternative conservation scenarios, ideally via a hydro-biogeochemically based tool that compares practice effectiveness by landscape position and identifies downstream sediment-deposition zones \parencite{Kröger2013}. Coupling practice implementation with site- and watershed-scale monitoring and modeling is presented as a way to advance water-quality improvement and practice-effectiveness assessment simultaneously, within an adaptive-management framework that maintains effectiveness over time \parencite{Kröger2013}. Simulation models are proposed to test and refine watershed scenarios, alongside continued development of placement criteria for new and emerging practices and better understanding of controls on nutrient-removal efficiency \parencite{Tomer2015}. Identifying and targeting the critical areas within watersheds that generate the largest share of nutrient and sediment export is a shared priority \parencite{Lemke2011}, echoed by calls to identify fields, farms, or watersheds exporting environmentally significant phosphorus via subsurface runoff and to improve soil-testing and characterization methods for prioritization \parencite{Sims1998}. Coarse-scale loss estimates are valued for flagging where deeper modeling and mitigation of nitrogen, phosphorus, and sediment losses should be pursued at catchment and regional scales \parencite{McDowell2021}. Decision-support systems are expected to advance through GIS-based non-point-source models that better capture spatial and temporal variability, incorporation of remote sensing, and software interfaces linking their components \parencite{Djodjic2002}. Combining ("stacking") drainage water management with other in-field and edge-of-field practices to meet phosphorus-reduction goals is an explicit design frontier \parencite{King2022}, and the nitrogen-removal capacity of stacked conservation practices is highlighted as a hypothesis to test \parencite{Roley2016}. Conservation planning in tile-drained watersheds is expected to require combining surface-water BMPs with practices that intercept and retain subsurface runoff, supported by watershed plans and by policies and incentive programs that specifically target tile-drainage nutrient sources \parencite{Lemke2011}. Wider involvement of land managers in regulatory assessments---supported by online teaching tools and analytical services---is likewise encouraged \parencite{Stutter2018}, and cost-share programs promoting drainage water management for watershed nitrogen and phosphorus targets are advocated \parencite{Sunohara2015}.

\subsection{Coupling nutrient, carbon, and greenhouse-gas processes}

The modeling frontier extends to biogeochemical coupling that current watershed tools rarely close. Reactive and sediment-transport models coupled to physically based hydrology are proposed to reveal drivers of adverse watershed impacts and to generate new insight into watershed-scale nutrient transport \parencite{Rathore2025}. For phosphorus specifically, investigating the multitude of P species and flow pathways for all relevant practices, and continuing to study surface-runoff dynamics---particularly under controlled drainage---are identified priorities \parencite{Mitchell2023}. Future work on the interaction between legacy soil phosphorus and hydrology in drained landscapes and closed depressions is needed to quantify P-loss risk \parencite{Mumbi2024}, and phosphorus-management strategies should jointly incorporate precipitation-pattern dynamics and soil-nutrient management, prioritizing application rate, timing, and placement plus legacy-P mitigation, especially where extreme weather is intensifying \parencite{Mumbi2025b}. Mechanistic controls on elevated, solubility-driven phosphorus loads to Lake Erie and other downstream waters require further study of spatial and temporal interactions between site characteristics and P solubility \parencite{Hanrahan2020}. Greenhouse-gas coupling is a notable gap: quantifying fluxes---including carbon sequestration, pre-implementation emissions, and dissolved nitrous oxide in tile drainage and conservation-practice outflows---is called for \parencite{Mitchell2023}. Coordinated, large-scale studies are needed to constrain the climate-mitigation potential of conservation drainage practices, few of which have simultaneously measured N\textsubscript{2}O, CH\textsubscript{4}, and change in soil organic carbon at the same sites, or included dissolved-gas measurements \parencite{Hall2025}. Practice-specific unknowns include the fate of water held back by control structures, interactions among bioreactor design, catchment hydrology, and organic media, controls on soil-organic-carbon accrual and N\textsubscript{2}O yield in saturated buffers, and N\textsubscript{2}O implications of drainage water recycling \parencite{Hall2025}. In the Midwest, longer-term dynamics of water-quality-wetland carbon storage, nitrous oxide, and methane over a wetland's expected lifetime---and the effects of upgrading drainage infrastructure and drainage coefficients on greenhouse gases and downstream hydrology and sediment---remain to be resolved \parencite{Mitchell2022}. Carbon budgets should also be integrated into wetland design models when very high nitrate loadings require carbon supplementation, within a more balanced and integrated approach to whole-system wetland evaluation over time \parencite{Kadlec2012}. Dissolved organic matter dynamics likewise demand attention to the range of relevant scales, changes in DOM quality and microbial availability across watershed scales, paired-watershed designs, and sampling that accounts for the episodic influence of subsurface drainage and extreme precipitation \parencite{Dalzell2011,Bellmore2015}.

\subsection{Practice-scale modeling frontiers: wetlands, controlled drainage, and drainage water recycling}

Detailed process modeling of individual conservation practices is a recurring need. Dynamic modeling at time-steps on the order of hours is required to approximate nitrate-N removal in response to the complex flow patterns of tile-drain-fed wetlands, alongside measures to reduce or supplement phosphorus removal and means to buffer and more gradually release tile-drain flows \parencite{Tanner2011}. Constructed-wetland siting and sizing depend on the wetland-to-catchment area ratio and seasonal discharge regime, and long-term studies over a broad range of hydraulic and nutrient loads are needed to establish sustainable nitrogen-retention efficiency \parencite{Steidl2019}. Wetland size ratios need refinement for each local context, together with deeper study of N\textsubscript{2}O emission processes, the N\textsubscript{2}/N\textsubscript{2}O ratio, and pesticide-metabolite accumulation \parencite{Tournebize2017}. Before large created-wetland projects proceed, critical questions of location, design, and function must be explored, supported by adaptive-management research and studies of optimal wetland siting for nitrogen removal across the Mississippi River Basin \parencite{Kovacic2006}. Passing tile-drained water through wetlands is proposed as a leading strategy for lowering nitrogen loads to Midwest rivers and ultimately the Gulf of Mexico, motivating analyses that identify nitrogen-management and drainage-remediation strategies and even reconsideration of river management \parencite{Baeumler2020}. Upstream improvement of water quality before infiltration to aquifers---potentially requiring substantial increases in forested area to lower recharge nitrate---is advanced as a watershed-scale design principle \parencite{Billy2013}. Constructed-wetland studies for irrigation reuse of treated drainage water are ongoing \parencite{Madramootoo2007}, and whole-wetland behavior must be assembled and interpreted over periods long enough to identify temporal trends \parencite{Kadlec2012}.

For controlled and managed drainage, future research should track complete water and nutrient budgets within and across field boundaries \parencite{Shedekar2021} and investigate effects on water and nutrients lost by other pathways---surface runoff, preferential flow, groundwater recharge, and biological uptake \parencite{Ross2016}. Additional research on drainage water management should span a range of soil textures, cropping systems, and climates and evaluate effects on surface and subsurface phosphorus dynamics \parencite{King2022}, with greater attention to phosphorus given the paucity of research and to combinations with practices such as sub-irrigation \parencite{Ross2016}. Whether drainage water management within channelized streams can restore more natural hydrology---and how such hydrologic change affects headwater biogeochemistry---requires comprehensive, integrated hydrology--water-quality--ecology assessment \parencite{King2009}. The potential of controlled tile drainage to reduce downstream flood risk by significantly reducing watershed-scale stream discharge is highlighted for further development \parencite{Sunohara2015}, and control structures on subsurface tile drainage, together with wetland restoration, are noted as promising means of storing more water on the landscape in spring \parencite{Dalzell2018}. Long-term monitoring with moisture sensors and automated systems is sought to improve drainage water recycling efficiency in tile-drained landscapes \parencite{Kaur2021}, and models such as DRAINMOD-DWR are needed to aid the design and management of recycling systems, supported by longer-term performance monitoring across weather, soil, and design conditions and by economic optimization of implementation and management costs \parencite{Moursi2024}. Full-scale field studies of drainage water recycling and saturated buffers under varied conditions, and study of emerging water-quality-wetland types such as reconstructed oxbows, round out the practice-scale agenda \parencite{Mitchell2023}. Restoration of multipurpose oxbows should continue with careful monitoring to determine whether observed relationships with tiles are representative, including further study of temperature differences among oxbow types \parencite{Leberg2024}, and installed weirs and additional floodplain work are being used to resolve surface- and groundwater-flow directions at restored oxbow sites \parencite{Schilling2017}. Edge-of-field filters and phosphorus-sorbing materials warrant testing at higher runoff loadings and in both surface and subsurface pathways \parencite{Plach2023}, filter and grass strips need evaluation of dormant-season loading and vegetation harvest \parencite{Holly2016,Vallée2015}, and the long-term effectiveness and wider deployment of small constructed wetlands and grass strips deserve continued evaluation \parencite{Vallée2015}.

\subsection{Integrated, adaptive, and stakeholder-connected modeling frameworks}

A final cross-cutting frontier is methodological integration. \textcite{Dollinger2015} articulate perhaps the most explicit long-range vision: developing numerically explicit modeling approaches at the landscape scale that integrate and couple the major processes governing the provision of landscape services by ditches, optimizing the spatial arrangement of management practices according to catchment heterogeneity and ditch connectivity, and defining efficient multi-service maximization strategies informed by an a priori, stakeholder-valued ranking of services---while acquiring new knowledge on feedbacks between processes that have most often been studied in isolation. Better mapping of key ditch characteristics (hydraulic, vegetation, and sorption properties) and study of poorly understood processes such as pollutant sorption and degradation are prerequisites \parencite{Dollinger2015,Herzon2008}. Landscape-scale, integrated approaches tailored to local conditions---combining on-farm changes with off-site wetland and riparian restoration and attentive site- and landscape-level design and monitoring---are broadly endorsed \parencite{Blann2009}. Managing catchment nitrogen export is argued to require acknowledging groundwater as well as farm-scale losses and intercepting edge-of-field pathways such as tile and open drains, using stream-rehabilitation and drainage water management tools especially during wet periods \parencite{Goeller2019}. Truly integrated watershed management is said to require better information on the controlling processes---such as spatial groundwater-head distribution---that link topography--geology interactions to the drain fraction \parencite{Mahmood2023}. Coupled field--watershed models enable scenario analyses of water-table management not possible when component models are used separately, and, once tested, can evaluate environmentally sound management in cold regions \parencite{Dayyani2010}. Modeling frameworks should remain extensible---for example, modifying loading-estimation approaches to incorporate additional processes and pathways and the distribution of planting dates across a watershed \parencite{Basu2010}, and refining and dynamically estimating proposed indices through field calibration \parencite{Tao2025}. Detailed, site-specific nutrient-management planning is recommended to handle the interactions between site hydrology and management practices \parencite{Kurz2005}. More catchment-scale research on water quality, wetland carbon sequestration, habitat quality, and cultural and educational services---and on the effects of upgrading existing drainage infrastructure rather than only new installations---is needed to fully populate these integrated frameworks \parencite{Mitchell2022}. Ultimately, tighter interaction among stakeholders, experimentalists, and modelers is identified as essential to make watershed modeling a credible basis for prioritizing drainage water management \parencite{Wang2020b,Kröger2013}.

8.body:
\subsection{Overview}

The literature on agricultural subsurface (tile) drainage repeatedly converges on the same meta-conclusion: many of the most policy-relevant questions about nutrient loss, water balance, and soil evolution in drained landscapes---and especially in drained organic and problem soils---cannot be resolved with the short, single-site, single-nutrient datasets that dominate the field. Across a wide geographic span, authors call for (i) \emph{longer observation periods} that span climatic variability and slow soil evolution, (ii) \emph{better metrics}, particularly phosphorus (P) saturation and sorption indices that outperform standard agronomic soil tests, (iii) \emph{isotope- and tracer-based approaches} to resolve loss pathways and source attribution, (iv) \emph{multi-scale and multi-species designs} that partition water and nutrient loads among competing pathways, and (v) \emph{mechanistic and modelling advances} that couple hydrology, biogeochemistry, and human-induced soil change. \textcite{Gramlich2018} frame the headline gap directly: longer-term studies---especially those exceeding three to four years---on drained organic soils are needed with respect to water balance and nitrogen (N) losses, along with case studies that compare drained and undrained conditions directly for N, P, and plant-protection products. This section organises the field's open questions and stated priorities around these lines of evidence, emphasising what remains uncertain, which hypotheses need testing, and which methods and data are still missing.

\subsection{The recurring call for long-term, multi-climate, multi-site observation}

The single most common recommendation is simply to keep observing for longer and across more conditions. \textcite{Kokulan2019} argue that additional longer-term field studies are needed to capture climatic variability and that continued observation is required to evaluate whether and how field hydrology and soil structure evolve into the future. Similar multi-year, multi-climate appeals recur for N-fertiliser effects on yield \parencite{Kaur2024}, for high-carbon soil mineralization and management \parencite{Waring2022}, and for developing management strategies that balance economic production with environmental protection across soil types and climatic zones \parencite{Kladivko2004}. \textcite{RANDALL1995} stress that long-term studies conducted over a range of climatic conditions are essential to derive appropriate guidelines, and that detailed plant, soil, and water measurements must be gathered together if interactions among crop production, climate, and environmental impact are to be uncovered.

Long-term designs are also invoked to detect slow or subtle change. \textcite{Kumar2014} note that because tillage and drainage differences in their system were small, long-term monitoring of crop yield is important, and that a switch from continuous corn to a corn--soybean rotation may help regain soil organic carbon over long durations. \textcite{Manninen2023} recommend keeping long-term field studies running to obtain more accurate estimates of discharge-induced organic-matter export from various soil types and cultivation practices, and to clarify the processes behind soil organic carbon and N losses. \textcite{Frison2009} extend the horizon further, asking whether observed drainage-induced soil evolution will continue or stabilise over decades and what the consequences will be for agricultural and environmental soil function; they also call for measurements along drains at different slope positions and on drainage networks of different ages. Long-term operation is itself framed as a management imperative: \textcite{Singh2007} argue that fields with subsurface drainage must continue to operate the system and keep reclaimed land under cultivation to maintain soil health on a long-term basis.

\subsubsection{Sampling standardisation and consistency}

Several authors emphasise that longer records are only useful if sampling is standardised. \textcite{Sherbine2023} recommend that soil biological indicators be evaluated over longer periods and that sampling be done at consistent times each year under similar soil moisture and temperature conditions. \textcite{Li2023b} likewise argue that the timing of sampling for soil health index (SHI) determination should be standardised to enable comparable results, practically aligning with post-harvest sampling for standard soil nutrient testing. For meta-analytic comparability, \textcite{Ghirardini2019} call for a complete hydrologic description of each plot, standardised sampling and analytical methods, monitoring periods longer than 40 days (especially for dewatered sludge), and reporting of concentrations with descriptive statistics (minimum, maximum, standard deviation).

\subsection{Phosphorus: better metrics, pathways, and loss prediction}

Phosphorus dynamics attract the densest set of open questions, spanning the adequacy of soil tests, the mechanisms of desorption and transport, and the tools needed for prediction.

\subsubsection{Beyond agronomic soil-test P: saturation and sorption metrics}

A consistent theme is that agronomic soil-test P (STP) is insufficient for environmental risk. \textcite{Sharpley1995} argue that agronomic P tests may not reliably assess eutrophication potential and that environmental tests estimating bioavailable P and P sorption capacity should be used on soils flagged as ``high risk,'' while comprehensive P-management plans address soils already containing P in excess of crop requirements. \textcite{Hooda1999} similarly propose that a measure of the degree of soil P saturation (DPS) may better estimate loss potential than extractable P such as Olsen-P, and that a full accounting of all P output forms is needed for mitigation strategies. \textcite{Nguyen2002} call for establishing critical DPS values---including for New Zealand sediments---calibrated across a range of sediment P tests and equilibrium P concentrations at net-zero sorption--desorption. \textcite{MILLER1979} adds a design-stage caution: P-adsorption capacity should be considered before organic soil areas are developed for crop production. \textcite{Osterholz2020} recommend assessing additional fields in the 100--400~mg~kg\textsuperscript{-1} STP range to clarify STP--P relationships and examining more detailed soil properties, including P sorption saturation, macropore formation and stability, and mineralogy.

\subsubsection{Mechanisms of desorption, colloidal and particulate transport}

Multiple authors identify the sorption/desorption interface and particulate pathways as poorly resolved. \textcite{Mumbi2025} call for quantifying when and how varying water sources and fluxes interact with soil to desorb and adsorb P---a need considered critical to advancing prediction of subsurface dissolved reactive P (DRP) transport---supported by data across soil types and management (e.g., long-term till vs.\ no-till). \textcite{Flores2021} note the need to understand the mechanisms controlling particulate settling and P desorption. \textcite{Nguyen2002} call for investigating P release--retention characteristics of drainage sediments under prolonged flooding and alternating waterlogged--drying conditions, and the interaction of sediment P with overlying drainage water across flooding, drying, wetting, and P-loading regimes. \textcite{Jiang2010} recommend assessing P fractions after leaching to understand transformations under polyacrylamide (PAM), including its effect on different purple-soil layers and interactions among broadcasting rate, mixing procedure, and leaching losses. \textcite{Wilson2019} highlight the need to quantify the contribution of colloidal P to DRP measurements and to determine whether erosion-mitigating BMPs or nutrient management better control it, alongside clarifying P stratification, preferential flow through tile drainage, and the effectiveness and time-to-effect of subsurface P application in stratified fields. \textcite{Norgaard2013} recommend including both dissolved and colloid-associated leaching parameters in field-scale risk assessment, testing a modified Dexter concept using clay and silt as a linking indicator, and considering water-dispersible colloids and tillage events as indicators of particle release.

\subsubsection{Multi-species reporting, temporal effects, and databases}

Fragmented reporting hampers synthesis. \textcite{Christianson2016} call for increasing the number of field-scale studies documenting drainage P losses and the effects of cropping, nutrient application, soil properties, and drainage design; including multiple P species (DRP, particulate P, total P); further developing the Drain Load database to capture peak flowrate and a drainage-concentration table; and using individual-study source data to evaluate temporal effects on drainage P transport. \textcite{Macrae2019} call for examining the hydroclimatic drivers of event responses and their impact on differing P concentrations in tile drainage, determining how tillage or P placement affects such relationships, and improving understanding of small-scale hydrologic and biogeochemical processes under various flow conditions and soil types. \textcite{Tanga2025} note that controlled-drainage effects on soluble P remain variable in the literature and recommend monitoring both particulate and dissolved forms while selecting site-specific stoplog depths based on field slope, drain slope, soil type, and climate. \textcite{Grant1996} call for further study of soil properties and P transport and for determining the applicability of in-situ optical turbidimeters to estimate particulate-matter content in drainage water. \textcite{Daigh2015b} note that subsurface-drainage total reactive P concentrations and yields in bioenergy systems need further evaluation in areas prone to higher P losses.

\subsubsection{Organic (Histosol/peat) soil phosphorus}

Phosphorus in cultivated organic soils is singled out as poorly understood. \textcite{Grenon2022} call for mechanistic studies to elucidate the pathways, hot spots, and hot moments of P transfer from agricultural organic soils to adjacent water bodies, including the dynamics of residual P fractions, soil--water P dynamics through long-term water-quality study, and P-sorption dynamics to compute more accurate P balances. \textcite{Grenon2021} propose a concrete methodological agenda: assessing P geochemistry in agricultural Histosols using isotope tracing (\textsuperscript{32}P/\textsuperscript{33}P and oxygen isotope ratios in phosphates), XANES, and \textsuperscript{31}P~NMR alongside sequential fractionation; assessing microbial turnover times; conducting long-term studies of drainage water management to reduce total P loads; developing a new mathematical model accounting for geo-mechanical instability and variability in organic-soil properties; and creating P-transport indices to predict loss. \textcite{Höyhtyä2025} note that nutrient leaching from abandoned peat fields is poorly studied, calling for research into the effects of ditch blockage, energy-willow production, or forestation, and for exploring the true nature of H\textsubscript{2}SO\textsubscript{4}-P via P K-edge XANES spectroscopy.

\subsubsection{Isotope-exchange and kinetic prediction of P loss}

\textcite{McDowell2001} propose that P isotope-exchange kinetics (IEK) has the potential to explain and eventually predict P loss to overland flow and subsurface drainage across a wide range of STP concentrations, and that with further work the IEK approach may predict P loss more accurately.

\subsection{Nitrogen: mineralization, testing, and management options}

Nitrogen questions cluster around residual and mineralizable N, testing methods, and the search for management levers beyond fertiliser rate. \textcite{Waring2022} call for measuring mineralization rates from high-carbon soils and for N-management options beyond fertilisation practices to reach water-quality reduction goals. \textcite{Bosompemaa2021} recommend further quantifying the mass of N in soil during both growing and non-growing seasons. \textcite{RANDALL1995} suggest a soil N test in the spring following dry years to measure residual nitrate and credit it against fertiliser applications, while \textcite{Conrad2009} recommend measuring mineral N to evaluate leaching potential before a new crop is sown and modifying crop rotation to prevent long periods without soil cover or N uptake. \textcite{Gentry2014} argue that more accurate quantification of fertiliser-N rates and timing would sharpen estimates of fertiliser-management influence on nitrate transport, and that the appropriateness of fall N application on tile-drained land in east-central Illinois may need revisiting in light of warmer winters. \textcite{ElGhannam2023} recommend that future studies measure not only nitrate outflow but also soil nitrate and plant N status. \textcite{Saiki2020} call for assessing the effect of water flow on soil N accumulation and discharge by evaluating N balance in paddy fields. On tooling, \textcite{Masri2024} suggest resin-membrane strip technology as a future tool to characterise soil N cycling and release under variable conditions, and \textcite{Kaur2024b} point to technology that improves the efficiency of enhanced fertiliser management in tile-drained fields. \textcite{Aide2016} frame an integrated agenda: developing effective crop-nutrient-management systems to improve uptake efficiency and reduce nitrate leaching, and refining denitrification-bioreactor design (reactor size, bed packing materials, equilibrium thresholds, elimination of preferential flow paths, and pH maintenance).

\subsubsection{Predicting long-term nitrate concentrations}

\textcite{Skaggs2012} identify a distinct methodological gap in estimating long-term flow-weighted average N concentrations for drainage waters: methods are needed for benchmark soils under typical rotations; the approximate method requires improvement for years when concentrations deviate strongly from the long-term average; and additional testing across soils, crops, and climates is needed, ideally with simple methods relating concentrations to prior yields, fertilisation, and weather patterns.

\subsection{Isotopes, tracers, and pathway partitioning}

A cross-cutting methodological priority is the use of isotopic and tracer techniques to resolve otherwise ambiguous pathways and sources. \textcite{Grenon2022} argue that more frequent post-fertilisation sampling and isotope tracers would help elucidate P dynamics in cultivated organic soils, and that applying isotopes within soil--water dynamic studies could shed new light on loss pathways. \textcite{Islam2022} propose measuring the stable isotope composition of water ($\delta$D and $\delta^{18}$O) to identify plant water sources late in the season, and \textcite{YliHalla2022} recommend verifying preliminary groundwater flow-path estimates with stable water isotopes. \textcite{Komor1997} show a source-attribution application, calling for additional $\delta^{11}$B analyses from closely spaced wells around feedlots and cultivated fields to define spatial variation in manure-leachate proportions, and for further investigation of the isotopic compositions of B sources to strengthen $\delta^{11}$B as a contaminant tracer. At the pathway-partitioning level, \textcite{Flores2021} urge future drainage studies to comment explicitly on surface-inlet presence and to partition both water and nutrient loads by pathway using before-after control-impact designs, hydrograph separation, or tracers, and to report both N and P loads for each site-year (including additional N forms such as NH\textsubscript{4} and organic N). \textcite{Kokulan2019} similarly recommend evaluating the chemistry and relative contributions of overland flow (OF) versus tile flow (TF) to edge-of-field nutrient losses. \textcite{Laubel1999} advocate combining studies at different scales---intact soil columns, experimental plots, and catchments---to examine subsurface loss of particulate matter and P in relation to macroporosity, soil P content, and cultivation practice.

\subsection{Soil biology, health indicators, and human-induced soil evolution}

\subsubsection{Biological and soil-health indicators}

The soil-biology response to changing drainage hydrology is repeatedly flagged as understudied. \textcite{Sherbine2023} call for longer-term evaluation of soil biological indicators to understand how soil biology responds to gradually changing hydrology. \textcite{Yang2025} identify the interaction mechanisms between subsurface drainage (SSD) and soil physicochemical properties, and the response of soil microbial activity, as priorities. \textcite{Kaur2024b} and \textcite{Kumar2014} call for continued research on specific soil microbial properties and microbial community structure and function to explain tillage and drainage impacts. \textcite{Li2023b} identify a scalability gap for nematode measurements, recommending DNA-sequencing methods and statistical approaches that quantitatively integrate nematodes with the SHI, and provide a basis for expanding to multiyear assessments of SHI linkages with nutrient losses and crop productivity. \textcite{Nakajima2015} argue that additional long-term studies are needed on the effects of no-till and subsurface drainage on the soil quality index (SQI), that valid, reliable, sensitive, repeatable, and accessible indicators must be identified, and that a framework for overall soil-quality evaluation and a systems approach are needed. \textcite{Singh2007} suggest that soil-enzyme activities could help identify management practices to maintain soil health. \textcite{Masri2024} argue that further research is needed to prove that no-till improves soil-health functions enough to sustainably reduce nitrate leaching, and that an extended research period is needed to relate nitrate load, water-quality parameters, and soil-health functions. \textcite{Caron2025} recommend adding dynamic to static indicators to diagnose compaction (extending to deeper layers and more corn-producing areas) and investigating whether long-term forage rotation or agroforestry can restore soil physical health, including comparative long-term economic viability.

\subsubsection{Drainage-induced soil evolution over decadal scales}

A distinctive line of inquiry treats subsurface drainage as a driver of long-term, potentially chaotic soil evolution. \textcite{Montagne2009} argue that quantifying human-induced soil evolution as a function of time should be a research priority, that further research is needed to evaluate drainage's impact on clay-mineral dissolution, and that more quantitative data as a function of soil properties are needed to model impacts on soil functions. \textcite{Montagne2010} argue that soil evolution in response to climate change must be explicitly studied and included in global climate-change models. \textcite{Montagne2013} suggest that significant material losses over a few decades make human-induced perturbation a powerful tool for studying the time-scales of such chaotic soil evolution. \textcite{HOOGHOUDT1952} note the continuation of a long experiment to determine whether soil structure will eventually be affected. \textcite{Ouellet2008} recommend using variables expressing tillage practice, surface-residue coverage, years in no-till, and soil particle size for empirical prediction (considering variable interactions), suggest soil organic matter as a crude surrogate for residue cover in regional modelling, and note that predictions of earthworm activity would likely be stronger for no-till than conventional-tillage systems.

\subsection{Drainage water management, BMPs, and policy tools}

Several authors target the effectiveness and siting of best management practices (BMPs) and drainage water management (DWM). \textcite{Wilson2019} present an extensive agenda: determining the impact of DWM on surface and tile flow of water and P; mapping the spatial distribution and range of STP values (especially elevated fields); improving understanding of P cycling at in-field, edge-of-field, and in-stream scales; developing innovative policy solutions (manure cooperatives, transport incentives, market-based strategies) and assessing farmer willingness and site-specific cost-benefit relationships; creating standardised methods to track adoption; and developing easy-to-use decision-support tools and field-scale P-loss projections to guide BMP placement. \textcite{Singh2020c} likewise call for extensive long-term, field- and watershed-scale work: measuring BMP effectiveness in site-specific areas; long-term field-scale trials of BMPs serving as P sinks; studies of adsorption/desorption with P-binding amendments; investigation of the time and pathways over which flushes of available P are lost or fixed; simulation of measured P loss from bioreactors and saturated buffers supplemented with P-adsorption materials; field evaluation of the P-index and critical-source-area concepts; long-term studies on P stratification linked to water-quality impairment; field-scale evaluation of lime, gypsum, and slow-release P fertilisers; further work on P source, rate, placement, and timing; year-round natural-rainfall runoff studies; multi-year cover-crop and tillage studies; long-term monitoring of new controlled-drainage, bioreactor, and saturated-buffer designs; better-funded watershed-scale P-flux monitoring; and criteria to maintain and remove P from BMPs before retention capacity is exceeded. \textcite{Zhang2017} add that impacts of modified no-till on DRP losses need investigation and that new soil-P testing and optimised P application timing, rates, and methods are needed to reduce DRP losses under no-till. \textcite{Nash2015c} call for determining whether an observed P-loss response between managed and free drainage was due to soil type, pH, anaerobic conditions, shallow tile placement, plant uptake, and/or rainfall. \textcite{Gottschall2012} argue that field-scale studies of environmental concentrations and loading across different soil-management systems are needed to validate predicted concentrations, elucidate dominant exposure pathways, and characterise dissipation kinetics.

\subsection{Modelling, reactive transport, and specialised soils}

\subsubsection{Model development and reactive transport}

Modelling gaps span parameterisation, coupling, and validation. \textcite{Conrad2009} recommend a range of parameter values rather than single estimates, improved understanding of organic--inorganic transformations in soil and plant (including plant growth), and statistically based automatic calibration. \textcite{Kokulan2019} note that runoff models for tiled landscapes with cracking clay soils can be improved by including temporal variability in the activation of hydrological pathways and hydroclimatic drivers of activation patterns. \textcite{Dadfar2010} plan to incorporate clay-mineralogy data into a CF algorithm as it becomes available, to better define the clay-content threshold, and to complete field validation against water-quality data from several Canadian watersheds. \textcite{KARAJEH1994c} propose extending a reactive solute-transport formulation to account for lime (CaCO\textsubscript{3}) precipitation/dissolution as a salt sink/source and for the reactivity and mobility of toxic trace elements (selenium, boron, molybdenum, arsenic) to serve irrigation, drainage, and shallow-groundwater management where salinity and trace elements matter. \textcite{Singh2020c} also call for simulating and validating measured P loss from amendment-supplemented bioreactors and saturated buffers.

\subsubsection{Acid sulfate and boreal soils}

Drained acid sulfate (AS) soils raise specialised questions. \textcite{Salo2023} recommend that future model applications consider short-term hydro-meteorological effects on sulfide oxidation under different drainage setups, and that soil macroporosity, the effect of ripening on hydraulic properties, formation of secondary minerals and weathering, and nitrate and gaseous emissions be incorporated, with the effects of moisture, temperature, and soil structure on sulfide oxidation examined. \textcite{Virtanen2017} note that the role of the N cycle in sulfide oxidation is unknown in boreal cultivated AS soils and that sulfide oxidation coupled with nitrate reduction, together with alternative neutralising agents, warrants study. \textcite{YliHalla2022} call for detailed study of the peat-cover thickness required to hinder sulfidic-material oxidation. \textcite{Åström2005} call for more detailed maps of actual and potential AS soils, more knowledge of the relative contribution of anthropogenic versus organically derived acidity in forested catchments, new environmentally friendly ways of draining actual AS soils, and cessation of drainage of potential AS soils.

\subsubsection{Saline, waterlogged, and reclaimed soils}

For reclaimed saline and waterlogged Vertisols, \textcite{Mukhopadhyay2023} and \textcite{Mukhopadhyay2023b} call for studies of how SSD integrated with crop management (inorganic fertilisers, crop residues, compost, green manure) affects soil-quality improvement and carbon-sequestration potential, of N/P/K losses and field-scale budgeting across more sites and larger Vertisol areas, of efficient recycling of drained water, and of greenhouse-gas fluxes before and after SSD installation to assess climate-mitigation potential. \textcite{ElGhannam2023} call for long-term study of controlled drainage's effect on soil salinity and for site-specific salinity-management practices to avoid salinization under long-term shallow groundwater. \textcite{Colombani2016} call for investigation of salt-crust formation and the subsequent transport of dissolved species, detailed soil-physical analysis of long-term tillage, and more sustainable practices (accurate fertiliser scheduling, frequent small irrigations after fertilisation, and buffer zones to abate excess N). \textcite{SoltaniGerdefaramarzi2020} suggest investigating leaching effects on other elements such as plant-available potassium, analysing at least three soil samples at longitudinal distances from the drain and at different depths by combined sampling, and testing organic modifiers such as animal manures to improve leaching efficiency. \textcite{Qian2021} recommend monitoring soil properties such as elemental toxicities in each year of multi-year waterlogging experiments, extending research to different crops and regions, and scheduling field drainage to account for different waterlogging forms, especially during budding, flowering, and boll-forming stages. \textcite{Bahçeci2018} recommend further field research to verify long-term resistance to root growth (especially for perennial crops) and performance under other soil, hydrological, and agricultural conditions, while \textcite{Bahceci2008} note that drainable-porosity ($\mu$) values can be determined in the laboratory, in situ, or by correlation, with more reliable values obtained when soil texture is considered. \textcite{Sadhukhan2019} call for further tests at additional locations under different fertilisation (including manure), soil, climate, and crop conditions over longer periods with more observational data. \textcite{Isa2023} call for studies of additive effectiveness (soil strength, rate, cost, impact on rice, physicochemical properties, and soil interaction), land-replacement characterisation and economic evaluation, and integration of rehabilitation methods to maximise soil strength and paddy yield.

\subsection{Other emerging topics and cross-cutting data needs}

Several findings point to under-studied constituents and processes. \textcite{Sethna2022} call for analysis of particulate silicon (amorphous and biogenic) to quantify total-Si transport and cycling, study of how warming and changing precipitation affect phytolith mineralization and dissolved-Si inputs to freshwaters, and explicit studies of nutrient limitation and uptake to quantify the role of diatoms and ambient nutrient stoichiometry. \textcite{Manninen2023} recommend that future discharge monitoring focus on variations in nutrient stoichiometry to assess the fate of organic matter in aquatic ecosystems. \textcite{Ghirardini2019} call for addressing the release of accumulated compounds during intense rain and drought/wet cycles, microbial indicators in surface runoff and tile drainage across rainfall events and soil types, and the occurrence of antibiotic-resistant genes and bacteria in sludge-amended soils, alongside comparative studies of sludge types, application rates and methods, and irrigation frequencies on different soils. \textcite{Gramlich2018} additionally call for clarifying the relevance of preferential flow in loamy and sandy soils and for defining categories of relevant compounds and soil--site characteristic combinations that create high cumulative environmental risk under intensive agriculture. \textcite{DambenieceMigliniece2018} recommend detailed fertiliser-use records to confirm crop-area/nutrient-load correlations and analysis of seasonal changes in nutrient concentrations and loads. \textcite{Norberg2025} note that long-term maintenance of soil fertility will probably require additional P and K fertilisation to preserve availability. \textcite{Kladivko2004} argue that detailed analysis of individual storm hydrographs is needed to test event-scale hypotheses and that comparing low-organic-matter soils with the high-organic-matter soils of much of the U.S.\ Midwest is essential for understanding the system and designing region-appropriate management. Collectively, these priorities define a research frontier in which longer records, richer metrics, isotopic tracing, and multi-scale, multi-species monitoring are prerequisites for the mechanistic understanding and predictive models the field still lacks.

7.body:
\subsection{Overview}

A recurring theme across the subsurface (tile) drainage literature is that the current evidence base remains provisional: many headline results are drawn from short records, single sites, or specific agro-ecological conditions, and their authors explicitly caution against premature generalisation. The dominant frontier message is therefore not a settled conclusion but a research agenda---one that calls for extended monitoring, broader spatial and geographic replication, harmonised and standardised methods, field-scale validation of models and emerging technologies, and stronger links between environmental, agronomic, economic, and social evidence. Several authors frame these needs at the level of the whole discipline: \textcite{Rogger2017} argue for adopting complex-systems thinking to link processes across time scales, conducting long-term experiments on physical--chemical--biological process interactions, focusing on connectivity and patterns across spatial scales, developing typologies and meta-analyses to generalise findings, strengthening multidisciplinary research consortia with shared data and terminology, and using long-term observations under controlled or known boundary conditions wherever possible. This section synthesises the specific monitoring and research needs identified across the reviewed studies, organised by the temporal, spatial, methodological, integrative, and programmatic dimensions along which the field is asked to advance.

The scale of the outstanding uncertainty is captured by the frequency of direct appeals for more work. Studies conclude, variously, that ``more research is needed'' and that ``more verification would be beneficial'' \parencite{SHALIT1995}, that ``the complexity of this subject requires more research'' \parencite{Malone2014}, that ``further research in this area is needed'' \parencite{Woo2023}, and that ``further evaluations are warranted before any concrete conclusions could be drawn'' \parencite{GUPTA1993}. These are not rhetorical closings but signposts to genuine gaps in evidence, method, and synthesis that the subsections below make explicit.

\subsection{The Case for Long-Term, Multi-Year Monitoring}

The single most widely voiced need is for records that are long enough to separate genuine treatment effects from the natural variability of weather, hydrology, and crop growth. \textcite{Clifford2023} emphasise the need for truly long-term experiments and monitoring to accurately assess management impacts, and \textcite{BUHLER1993} caution that a lag time of several years may be necessary before the impact of altered practices can be objectively evaluated. \textcite{Ali2013} judge that longer monitoring periods---at least five years---and continued data collection are desirable, and warn that long-term consequences may need continued observation if drainage systems are expanded in length or depth over time. In Egypt, \textcite{Ali2001} report that monitoring work will continue, that within a few years more and longer time series will become available for the whole country, and that a more detailed analysis is expected to become possible as those records lengthen.

At the site scale, numerous studies attach their conclusions to explicit requirements for additional years of observation. \textcite{Jaynes2008} state that continued monitoring of the denitrification-wall treatment for additional years is necessary to clearly quantify its effectiveness given natural variability in weather and crop growth, that extended monitoring is required to quantify effectiveness over time, and that further work is needed to determine the long-term cost per unit of nitrate removed and the system's potential as a management tool. \textcite{Dalhem2019} note that continued sampling and measurements will reveal how long the positive effects of the treatments persist. \textcite{Osterholm2005} judge it worthwhile to monitor a test site for several more years to better assess the functionality of a combination of two techniques, while \textcite{ROLLIN1987} plan to gather additional observations covering a longer service-life period. Similar calls come from \textcite{Wesström2015}, who require verification over a longer monitoring period, \textcite{Reinhart2020}, who call for more widespread and longer-duration evaluations to better understand a practice's potential impact, and \textcite{Rijal2012}, who identify a need to assess the replicability of results in time. \textcite{GiménezValero2025} similarly conclude that additional multi-year measurements will be necessary to confirm whether observed differences persist under comparable environmental and management conditions. Underlying all of these is the disciplinary-scale argument of \textcite{SPALING1995} for long-term monitoring of environmental change at various scales.

\subsection{Temporal Resolution and Seasonal Coverage}

Beyond record length, several authors highlight gaps in the temporal \emph{resolution} and seasonal coverage of monitoring. \textcite{Klaiber2020} make this explicit, calling for additional years of monitoring, improved monitoring during the non-growing season, and year-round monitoring to better understand seasonal phosphorus exports and develop more robust conclusions. The importance of avoiding gaps in high-frequency records is stressed by \textcite{Hodaj2017}, who advise that future studies check instruments as frequently as possible to limit gaps in the data collected. \textcite{Wesström2015} add a timing consideration specific to infrastructure interventions, noting that it is more appropriate to monitor leaching of nutrients after drain repair.

\subsection{Spatial Replication and Geographic Diversity}

A second major frontier is the need to test whether site-specific findings hold across other locations, soils, climates, and management regimes. \textcite{Menegaz2025} state that additional studies are needed before broad adoption of their results can be encouraged beyond the local conditions of their study, and \textcite{Zimmerman2017b} call for similar data to be reported for other sites and years. \textcite{Ali2001} plan to collect more data from other sample areas in Egypt reflecting the different agro-ecological conditions prevalent in the country. \textcite{Hodaj2017} argue that further exploration of the two-stage ditch in other settings would benefit research on the practice, and \textcite{Bigelow2001} recommend that further research address effects where putting greens are severely compacted, possess low percolation rates, or are maintained in unfavourable environments such as shade or excessive wetness, while suggesting the technology may have merit under other situations. \textcite{Terrado2015} likewise conclude that additional monitoring and research supporting their ideas and findings is warranted. At the level of evidence synthesis, \textcite{Wu2025} argue that future syntheses will require more robust studies that are geographically diverse and methodologically harmonized, and that with more extensive experimental evidence future meta-analyses will be better positioned to disentangle management effects.

\subsection{Field-Scale Validation of Models and Emerging Technologies}

Much of the reviewed work rests on models, pilot studies, or laboratory-scale results whose authors explicitly defer confident application until field-scale validation is available. \textcite{Mitchell2025} state plainly that ``field-scale research is needed to validate these results'' and that ``further research to validate our models is needed before they are widely applied.'' \textcite{Kalcic2018} similarly conclude that continued efforts to validate practice effectiveness at the field and reach scales will be needed to verify if models are correct, and that these areas are ripe for future modeling development and validation. \textcite{Garmdareh2018} recommend more rigorous testing of a model before its extensive use, and \textcite{Yu2016} call for validating their conclusions with more experiments. \textcite{SHALIT1995} identify a specific validation target, arguing that additional investigations should determine how well the leaching-fraction model applies to predicting concentrations from preferential-flow situations. For emerging technologies, \textcite{DarziNaftchali2018b} stress that before new technologies can be implemented on a large scale, their environmental effects need to be quantified at the level of pilot areas, and \textcite{GUPTA1993} require further measurements and evaluations before concrete conclusions can be drawn.

\subsection{Monitoring Infrastructure, Instrumentation, and Data Tools}

A distinct set of needs concerns the practical apparatus of monitoring---the samplers, loggers, diagnostic methods, and software that make long-term data collection feasible and reliable. \textcite{Kanwar1999} describe how automatic samplers can be connected to a monitoring system to collect discrete water samples alongside continuous samples, how additional equipment can be connected to data loggers to measure soil temperature or soil moisture, and how relays or pressure switches could replace electronic transmitters to detect sump-pump operation; they further advise minimising the distance between monitoring equipment and data loggers, downloading data automatically at set intervals to minimise data loss, and performing annual maintenance including cleaning check valves and calibrating flowmeters. Diagnostic methods themselves are a research target: \textcite{Petersen2012} propose reducing pressure variations by doubling the equipment and injecting smoke from both ends of a drain section, potentially enabling work with longer drain lines, while noting that further studies are needed to discover the details of the pathways and to disclose reasons for observed clustering. \textcite{MartínezBeltrán2007} recommend observing selected pipes to determine the state of the envelope and perforations, observing the surrounding soil because of its influence on radial flow in the drain trench, and permanently monitoring the sampling process to verify that favourable results are maintained over time.

Software and data infrastructure form an increasingly prominent part of this frontier. \textcite{Shedekar2018} advise readers to explore data products and tools further, to visit the National Elevation Dataset and USGS websites for the latest data availability and updates, and to track ongoing developments of these tools, while also arguing that improved drainage-management practices are needed to increase adaptation to a changing climate. A series of tool-development efforts describe themselves as explicitly ongoing and open to community input: \textcite{Falasy2025} note that development of their tools is continuing---spanning creation of topologically sound, one-way-flow tile networks and application tools for configuring the burying and sizing of drainage networks---and welcome suggestions and testing with datasets beyond those provided; \textcite{Falasy2025b} report an ongoing development process with numerous revisions based on test-user feedback and encourage application to data beyond the samples provided; and \textcite{Falasy2024} state that development of their tools will be continual and encourage testing with other data.

\subsection{Standardisation, Harmonisation, and Comparability of Data}

For the accumulating body of monitoring data to support robust synthesis, several authors identify a need for standardised and harmonised methods. \textcite{Wu2025} tie the value of future meta-analyses to methodologically harmonized studies and comprehensive, long-term monitoring of the full suite of environmental impacts. \textcite{Zhang2018} set out a detailed programme for environmental indicators: improving the quality and comparability of existing indicators over time through harmonized methods for data collection, monitoring, interpretation, and reporting; developing new indicators that address emerging environmental and policy concerns while ensuring scientific credibility, sensitivity to change, and ease of interpretation; developing standardized procedures or guidelines for evaluation, including standards, thresholds, reference points, and complementary information for validation; recognising data gaps and improving methodologies for data upscaling, uncertainty assessment, and analysis of contradictory indicators; and improving or adapting environmental models for indicator modeling while incorporating earth-observation data and reducing barriers to remote-sensing data access. These priorities complement the call by \textcite{Rogger2017} for typologies, meta-analyses, and shared data and terminology across research consortia.

\subsection{Understanding Complex Processes, Pathways, and Interactions}

A further frontier concerns mechanistic understanding: identifying the pathways, relationships, and process interactions that current data only partially resolve. \textcite{Malone2014} conclude simply that the complexity of the subject requires more research, and \textcite{Cui2016} state that more research is needed to define the relationships they observed. \textcite{Petersen2012} identify unresolved transport pathways and clustering patterns requiring further investigation. \textcite{Tomer2008} intend to pursue their central idea as an avenue of research, to undertake bacterial source tracking in future work within their watershed, and note that a specific transport pathway remains a subject for future research. \textcite{Ali2013} call for a separate geochemical modelling study to validate whether geochemical reactions bind free heavy metals with the solid matrix. Process-level questions extend to plant response: \textcite{GiménezValero2025} note that an observation consistent with the role of phosphorus in flowering merits further study under controlled conditions, and \textcite{Tamura2021} report that further experiments are required to clarify a specific point in more detail. \textcite{LAL1993} indicate that results of experiments on plant-nutrient relations will be presented in a companion report and that a long-term experiment is being analysed to test their hypotheses. These needs echo the overarching argument of \textcite{Rogger2017} for long-term experiments on physical--chemical--biological process interactions and for a focus on connectivity and patterns across spatial scales.

\subsection{Integrating Environmental, Agronomic, Economic, and Social Evidence}

Several authors argue that monitoring must move beyond environmental endpoints alone to encompass agronomic, economic, and social dimensions, and to connect with decision-making. \textcite{Zhang2018} call for improved linkages of environmental indicators with indicators tracking economic and social change, in terms of both causes and effects. \textcite{Zhu2025} require further assessment to evaluate actual growth in conjunction with economic, social, and environmental factors, and \textcite{Brikmans2025} argue that future investigations should elucidate strategies for prolonging physical benefits while critically assessing the agronomic and economic feasibility of such approaches. \textcite{Monaghan2016} identify a need for further research on the practical implications of management changes---in their case, having cows off-pasture for extended periods. At the science--policy interface, \textcite{Letey1999} argue that plans should be updated as monitoring, additional studies, and local actions reveal new facts, and that the scientific community needs to evaluate its role in policymaking and focus research on questions of greatest value to decision makers as well as to scientific peers. \textcite{Clifford2023} call for scientific assessment and prioritisation of which approaches have the largest effects under what circumstances, alongside truly long-term experiments and monitoring. \textcite{Kalcic2018} likewise argue that further work is needed to assess current knowledge and prioritise knowledge gaps, that clear messaging through education and extension will be imperative to achieve implementation at the scales needed to treat water-quality needs in the Mississippi River Basin and Great Lakes regions, and that these areas are ripe for future modeling development and validation.

\subsection{Ongoing and Planned Monitoring Programmes}

Finally, the literature documents a number of monitoring programmes that are explicitly ongoing or planned, underscoring that many conclusions are interim by design. \textcite{Tournebize2012} report that in 2011 a performance-assessment programme was established to determine whether co-constructed wetlands comply with new regulations, that intensive hydrological monitoring will continue to assess the implemented system's performance, and that resizing will be considered if needed in accordance with current regulations. \textcite{Kjaersgaard2016} note that monitoring was to begin in spring 2016, and \textcite{Ali2001} confirm that monitoring work will continue. \textcite{Ohlendorf2002} describe how data from a monitoring programme were used to identify needs for contingency plans, to recommend further research and monitoring to improve site management, and how further studies were subsequently conducted to resolve unknowns identified by a risk assessment. Other authors flag forthcoming results: \textcite{Drury2014} indicate that data from remaining treatments will be presented in a subsequent paper. Additional calls for continued or expanded empirical effort come from \textcite{Rekha2011}, who state that more treatment studies are required, and from \textcite{Ogino2007}, who express hope that research and development in the field will be actively promoted. Collectively, these programmes and appeals reinforce the central frontier message: robust, generalisable conclusions about subsurface drainage await the longer, broader, better-harmonised, and more integrative evidence base that the field is actively working to build.

10.body:
\subsection{Overview}

Despite decades of field and modelling work, the literature synthesised here converges on a shared diagnosis: our capacity to predict how subsurface (tile) drainage alters water, sediment, and nutrient export remains limited by sparse field verification, incomplete knowledge of where and how drains are installed, and process representations that are too coarse for the scales at which decisions are made. The recurring research frontier therefore spans four interlocking gaps. First, watershed-scale hydrologic effects of tiling must be assessed with both models and field observations, across events of all sizes and multiple landscapes, rather than inferred from single sites \parencite{Schilling2008,Sloan2017,Sloan2016}. Second, the spatial location, extent, and specifications of drainage networks are largely unknown, motivating calls for mapping, databases, and validated prediction of tile lines \parencite{Naz2009,Zhou2025,Valayamkunnath2020}. Third, existing agricultural models (APEX, DRAINMOD, SWAT, MIKE~SHE, and others) require improved tile, water-table, macropore, and phosphorus routines, together with careful treatment of parameter uncertainty when drainage specifications are unknown at calibration \parencite{Williams2016,Bauwe2016,Guo2018b,Nelson2018}. Fourth, key transport processes---preferential and macropore flow, phosphorus source partitioning, sediment storage and exhaustion, and cold-season dynamics---remain underquantified and must be constrained with longer-term, higher-resolution monitoring \parencite{King2015,Grangeon2021,Trentman2020}. The subsections below organise the identified gaps, hypotheses, and methodological needs around these themes.

\subsection{Watershed-Scale Hydrology and Cross-Scale Integration}

A dominant line of argument is that the hydrologic effects of tile drainage remain inadequately characterised at the watershed scale and must be pursued through combined modelling and field verification. \textcite{Schilling2008} argue that more in-depth research is needed to assess tile drainage effects on watershed hydrology using both models and field data, with specific attention to streamflow recessions and how those recessions differ seasonally between tiled and non-tiled watersheds. Extending this, \textcite{Schilling2012} call for work that quantifies the specific hydrologic effects beneath patterned tiling systems at the watershed scale. \textcite{Sloan2017} frame the outstanding questions in terms of scope, recommending that the influence of tile drains be explored for events of all sizes and at all scales, alongside more comprehensive analyses across different landscapes.

Integrating field-scale responses into basin behaviour is itself an open problem. \textcite{Sloan2016} conclude that hydrologic models must include routing between spatially variable depressional stores, and that a detailed modelling study is needed to understand flow accumulation and attenuation when responses from multiple tiled fields are integrated across a river network and their combined role in large-scale watershed flooding. Related scaling questions concern how the collective drainage network shapes flood behaviour: \textcite{Fonley2021} recommend a comprehensive study of the statistical improvement in flood prediction offered by their model, further study of how the flow duration curve evolves as the tile population evolves, and note that such work could ultimately inform recommendations for where artificial drainage is applied on the landscape. At the regional scale, \textcite{BolandBrien2014} identify a need to decouple the effects of row-crop cover from artificial drainage and to separate the contributions of surface versus subsurface drainage in altering hydrologic response; they hypothesise that bypass-flow networks contribute to hydrologic homogenisation and argue that simple models for tiled landscapes should be developed, in part because long-term data are sparse.

Several studies emphasise interactions between drains and the receiving channel network as a frontier. \textcite{Grangeon2021} suggest future investigation of the interactions between rivers/ditches and tile-drain flows, while \textcite{Clement2017} call for research on the fate and transport of tile-drain effluent after it reaches the surface drainage system. \textcite{Zhou2025} argue for field-scale monitoring of the direct impact of tile flow on downstream water discharge, stream power, and channel geomorphology across multiple locations within watersheds to provide higher-resolution ground-truth measurements. Efforts to extend field-scale drainage models to catchment and watershed scales are ongoing, as with the application of MIKE~SHE to Midwestern tile-drained lands \parencite{Zhou2013}.

\subsection{Locating, Mapping, and Cataloguing Drainage Networks}

A cross-cutting obstacle is that the physical drainage network is often unknown, which propagates uncertainty into every downstream model. At the field scale, \textcite{Amado2018} note that having actual tile locations, and measured tile-flow discharge at the site, would improve model accuracy and calibration, and that additional field data and delineation of the subsurface drainage network are needed to refine the model (alongside a grid-sensitivity study). Predictive mapping of tile lines from imagery is promising but immature: \textcite{Naz2009} call for further validation of predicted tile maps against observed locations from GPS survey, and for methods that minimise the misidentification of image noise as tile lines and that connect broken segments erroneously split during detection. \textcite{Schilling2019} likewise identify improving the ability to locate field tile systems in agricultural watersheds as an ongoing research focus.

At larger scales, \textcite{Valayamkunnath2020} anticipate updating their tile-drainage map repository as new data become available. \textcite{Zhou2025} set out the most explicit data agenda: future studies should report information on subsurface tiles beyond areal extent---particularly tile diameter, depth, and spacing, as well as the mains that collect water from pattern tiles---and a tile-drainage database should be constructed through joint efforts of government agencies, researchers, private landowners, and drainage contractors, with pipe networks mapped using remote sensing and ground-based surveys where possible.

\subsection{Model Structure, Routines, and Parameterisation}

\subsubsection{Improved and New Tile, Water-Table, and Crop Routines}

Many authors call for better process routines within existing agricultural models. \textcite{Williams2016} argue that incorporating identified flow pathways and processes into models such as APEX, DRAINMOD, and SWAT is critical for improving estimates of phosphorus transport in tile-drained landscapes. Within SWAT and its variants, \textcite{Schilling2019} recommend testing new tile-drainage algorithms that give a more physically based representation of tile function and can directly account for pattern-tile configurations; \textcite{Guo2018b} see an opportunity to improve representation of tile systems---especially individual tiles---and functionality at watershed scales, including improving water-table-depth calculation during long dry periods and testing improved routines and curve-number methods on additional individual tiles and watersheds. \textcite{Moriasi2012} call for follow-up studies comparing dynamic and constant surface-depression storage ($S_d$) approaches for tile-flow and streamflow simulation, and for additional testing of the Modified SWAT's tile-flow and water-quality simulation against measured tile flow and tile-flow water-quality data. In APEX, \textcite{Nelson2018} note that improvements in tile-drainage, water-table, and crop-growth routines may be needed, and that new routines for water-table fluctuation and subsurface tile drainage were in development but not yet implemented. For rangeland modelling, \textcite{Chen2016} conclude that a tile-drainage module is needed in the GPFARM-Range model to predict drainage from subsurface-drained pastureland on shorter time scales and in a timely manner. \textcite{Boles2015} report that their new routine's parameter values provide a basis for fine-tuning tile parameterisation, suggest that modellers vary LATKSATF on a hydrologic-response-unit rather than watershed basis, and identify additional studies to build understanding of the routine's effects.

\subsubsection{Parameter Uncertainty When Drainage Specifications Are Unknown}

A distinct methodological caution concerns calibration under unknown drainage specifications. \textcite{Bauwe2016} stress that much care should be taken when calibrating the depth-to-impervious-layer parameter (DEP\_IMP) because it is critical in dividing subsurface flow into tile flow and groundwater flow, that the choice of infiltration model matters when flow components are relevant, and that this is especially important when drainage specifications are unknown and tile-drain parameters are used for calibration. Consistent with this, \textcite{Guo2018b} propose separating DEP\_IMP into two parameters---depth to impervious layer and a soil-permeability factor---to improve representation of the tile system.

\subsubsection{Process Formulations and Higher-Dimensional Models}

Some gaps concern the underlying physics of flow and solute movement. \textcite{Qi2018} suggest that the method of simulating subsurface water flow in the soil matrix should be revisited or replaced by a soil-matrix-potential-based Richards equation, and that the algorithm for particulate-phosphorus loss to tile drainage should be revisited. \textcite{Mohanty1998} argue that tile flow and conservative solute transport would perhaps be better interpreted and modelled with a three-dimensional model considering regional water and chemical management, and that more detailed consideration of regional hydrology and adjacent-bench irrigation/fertiliser scheduling is needed to improve prediction. \textcite{DeSchepper2015} report developing a larger-scale model to simulate seasonal variation in tile-drainage outflow with heterogeneities of clayey tills represented in greater detail, and argue that more realistic simulated water-flow patterns in heterogeneous drained fields are a cornerstone of future nutrient-transport modelling; they anticipate future work focusing on nutrient-transport modelling. \textcite{Garrison1999} propose that integrating spatial images of surface wetness (before emergence) and within-season vegetative index, coupled with final yield, will refine tile-model parameters, and that their tile-water and nitrogen-flow methods may found improved spatial representation of soil-water and nitrogen dynamics.

\subsubsection{Field Verification of Model Predictions}

Calls for direct measurements to validate models recur throughout. \textcite{Muma2017} recommend further validating the CATHY model by installing an automatic tile-drain system for flow measurement, using field measurements of evapotranspiration to validate simulated actual evapotranspiration, and installing a piezometer at the centre of the tile-drain plot to evaluate model performance against water-table elevation. \textcite{Nazari2021} suggest their approach can help inform numerical-model evaluations and assess environmental gradients across sites, and that accounting for redox and other conditions affecting sorption--desorption dynamics between events could reduce uncertainty and improve model fit (Nash--Sutcliffe efficiency).

\subsection{Preferential and Macropore Flow Processes}

Preferential and macropore flow are repeatedly named as critical, underquantified controls. \textcite{Boles2015} identify macropore-related processes as a critical area of future research. \textcite{Williams2016} call for investigation of macropore-flow contributions to tile drains during different periods of the year to clarify their role in annual water budgets, and for work on the interaction between macropores and the surrounding matrix. \textcite{King2015} frame the central questions as quantifying the development, extent, and behaviour of preferential-flow paths under different soils, management practices, and antecedent conditions, and determining what fraction of phosphorus is delivered to tile drains via preferential flow versus matrix flow. \textcite{Nazari2021} recommend that their methodology be evaluated at sites where matrix--macropore exchange forms a larger share of the tile water budget, and that it be considered in water-quality research on practices---such as drainage water management---that directly affect water-table dynamics. Methodologically, \textcite{Nielsen2015} note their staining technique should be most useful in studies of preferential flow and transport in drained soils (for example, leaching of strongly sorbing contaminants), and that longer infiltration times could increase the number of stained pathways reaching the tile lines. \textcite{Ruark2009} argue that further explanation of preferential-flow interpretation requires detailed analyses of hydrograph and chemograph behaviour, and \textcite{BACKLUND1995} call for research on contamination transported by old and new tile systems through preferential-flow paths and on means of modelling it.

\subsection{Phosphorus and Nutrient Transport}

Improving the representation and understanding of nutrient transport---especially phosphorus---is a persistent priority. \textcite{King2015} call for developing and enhancing phosphorus routines in deterministic models to better represent subsurface drainage and P transport, and for measuring phosphorus losses in surface runoff and subsurface drainage simultaneously to determine partitioning between flow paths and avoid unintended consequences. \textcite{Williams2016} stress the importance of differentiating sources of tile-drained phosphorus (e.g., soil versus fertiliser) to understand how P is transported through macropore paths. \textcite{Qi2018} encourage revisiting the particulate-P loss algorithm and suggest measuring the size distribution of soil particles that act as phosphorus carriers, comparing them with soil-matrix pore sizes to enhance understanding of particulate-P transport. \textcite{Geohring2001} note that additional research would help determine which processes most significantly reduce total-phosphorus loadings to tile drains. \textcite{Zimmer2016} recommend more intensive monitoring of discharge and P forms from tile-drained areas, and considering retention of phosphorus at tile-drain outlets to prevent excessive input to water bodies.

Broader nutrient questions extend to nitrogen and receiving-water impacts. \textcite{Clement2017} call for research on P retention and uptake lengths in reaches with and without best management practices, on sampling tile drains from different areas of the watershed, on the potential effects of nitrogen given its prevalence in inorganic and organic fertilisers, and on the role of internal P loading from lake sediments in driving algal blooms. \textcite{King2018} argue that additional work is needed to understand phosphorus losses in subsurface tile-drained landscapes and to mitigate harmful and nuisance algal blooms in receiving waters, examining both individual and stacked nutrient-management practices and the processes controlling P transport across fields with varying characteristics. \textcite{Ruark2009} note that both the nature and drivers of dissolved-organic-carbon dynamics in relation to effluent applications require further investigation, and that the ecological implications of DOC from tile drainage remain largely unknown.

\subsection{Sediment Dynamics and Storage}

Sediment transfer through drains is highlighted as an emerging area requiring longer records and targeted tracers. \textcite{Grangeon2021} recommend monitoring tile-drained catchments over longer periods to gain insight into sediment storage and exhaustion, further quantifying the factors controlling sediment storage from the cultivated topsoil to the drains during ephemeral flow, using short-lived fallout radionuclides or alternative low-cost measurements to quantify sediment storage and transfer, and further quantifying the impacts of these transfers on sediment and contaminant exports from drained catchments. \textcite{Gaillot2023b} argue that the influence of cropping practices on soil-surface characteristics and tile-drain connectivity---and on the temporal dynamics of suspended sediment---should be explored further; they recommend combining high-temporal-resolution acquisition of hydrosedimentary fluxes with the dynamic evolution of soil structure at the surface, in the topsoil, and in the subsoil, and suggest that a suspended-sediment tracer allowing event-scale discrimination could clarify sediment dynamics. At the reach scale, \textcite{Ahiablame2010} call for investigations accounting for manure application, soil types, and nutrient loadings from individual tile outlets to examine the impact of tile inputs on sediments across different reach scales and ditch conditions (vegetated, non-vegetated, dredged, and non-dredged).

\subsection{Management Practices, Trade-offs, and Mitigation}

A further frontier concerns rigorous, holistic evaluation of management and treatment practices. \textcite{King2015} call for holistic evaluations of management and treatment practices---including cumulative effects and trade-offs between surface runoff and subsurface drainage---and for exploring practices that target phosphorus in drainage water, such as tillage directly over tile lines and withholding phosphorus application over tile lines. \textcite{King2016} recommend investigating cover crops, drainage water management, and 4R nutrient management as strategies to reduce nitrogen and phosphorus transport, particularly practices targeting the non-growing season to decrease annual loads. \textcite{Williams2016} call for examining different tillage operations (moldboard plow, chisel plow) and application methods (banding or injection of fertiliser). \textcite{Kokulan2021} recommend assessing tile drainage's role in hydrochemical export together with practices such as fertilisation, tillage, and variable tile configuration to develop a complete regional picture. \textcite{Qi2018} suggest testing further off-season management and modelling practices under manure-phosphorus application and different soil and climate conditions. In the context of agricultural-waste land application, \textcite{BACKLUND1995} call for research on nutrient leaching, interception, denitrification, and fecal-coliform transport; on the interaction between cedar wood chips and iron-ocher buildup (including chemical substitutes or filter-fabric applications); on reuse of drainage water and its impact on crop production and downstream water quality; and, where contamination exceeds acceptable levels, on alternatives such as tile-outflow pumpback systems, reuse, or other outlet options. \textcite{Fortin2002} note that evaluating the effects of different soil treatments on chemical movement to tile drains under natural rainfall probably requires long-term experiments to capture climatic variability.

\subsection{Seasonality, Cold Climates, and Long-Term Monitoring}

Several gaps concern temporal coverage. \textcite{King2015} identify the need to expand research on hydrology and phosphorus transport in cold climates, especially the winter and snowmelt periods that are underrepresented in the literature. \textcite{Trentman2020} recommend prioritising seasonal sampling across a range of antecedent-precipitation conditions in light of a changing climate, note that the effect of snow on tile drainage and nutrient export is a topic of interest, and observe that soluble-reactive-phosphorus reduction estimates could be better constrained with continuous (e.g., daily) tile-drain measurements. \textcite{Millar2025} call for multiyear studies of tile-drain nutrient loads and for coverage of important periods outside the growing season, including winter, postharvest, and early spring. Longer and more intensive observation is also needed to diagnose drain performance itself: \textcite{Rimidis2003} argue that a more elaborate measuring set-up and long-term observations are required to ascertain the cause of drain malfunctioning, recommending additional piezometers close to the drain to measure approach-flow head loss and to distinguish head due to entrance resistance from backpressure within the drain.

\subsection{Data, Tracers, and Methodological Needs}

Finally, the reviewed studies point to methodological and data infrastructure requirements that cut across the themes above. Beyond drainage-network mapping and databases \parencite{Zhou2025,Valayamkunnath2020,Naz2009}, authors call for tracer- and chemistry-based approaches to partition flow components: \textcite{Frederiksen2023} suggest process-based modelling or water-chemistry studies using water isotopes or conservative tracers to estimate fast-flowing components in quick flow and the tile-flow component in baseflow, and recommend adding predictor variables such as landscape characteristics to better explain spatial variation in tile-flow generation and improve modelling accuracy and precision. \textcite{Schilling2019} propose adding less-sensitive parameters such as specific conductance to end-member mixing analysis to better account for seasonal deployment and operational variations. Collectively, these needs---higher-resolution and longer-term monitoring, event-scale tracers, three-dimensional and physically based process models, and open shared datasets on drainage-network specifications---define a research agenda in which improved field verification and improved model structure advance together rather than separately \parencite{Schilling2008,Bauwe2016,Zhou2025}.

